/* Generated by spec/build_intel.py from data/intel/intel2026C.json - edit the JSON, not this file. */
window.INTEL = {"navLabel": "2026C Predicted Exam", "pageTitle": "2026 Moed C Predicted Exam — Practice", "overviewTitle": "Intel briefing — where his questions come from, how the July prediction did, and what to expect on 16 October", "sections": [{"tier": 1, "open": true, "title": "Predicted 2026 Moed C — full mock exam", "blurb": "The single most likely problem for each slot, in the 2026 format (4 T/F × 7 = 28, then four written problems). Take it as a timed 3-hour paper with your two A4 sheets, then mark it honestly. No single problem here is above about 6% likely, so do the alternates too.", "items": [{"id": "c26-mock-p1", "sid": "2026C MOCK · P1", "difficulty": "medium", "src": "T/F block, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>28</mn></mrow></math> pts. (a) ellipsoid on the primal-dual polyhedron, the flipped twin of 2026B-1a (LP deck slide 80); (b) Schwartz-Zippel with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> (chain 2023B-c, 2024C-b, 2025B-a, 2026A-b); (c) zero traces imply <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> (chain 2024B-d, 2026A-c, 2026B-c; Horn-Johnson 2.4.P10); (d) spectral gap implies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> diameter (recitation 4; re-skin of 2023B-1d).", "stem": "<p><b>Problem 1</b> (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>28</mn></mrow></math> points)</p><p>For each of the following statements, write whether they are true or false, and briefly explain your answer.</p>", "parts": [{"part": "a", "difficulty": "easy", "summary": "If the ellipsoid method is run on the combined primal-dual polyhedron P' and its center lies in P', the primal part of the center is optimal (True).", "q": "<p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> be the linear program: minimize <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> subject to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> be its dual: maximize <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> subject to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>. Assume that both <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are feasible. Suppose we run the ellipsoid algorithm, as in class, on the polyhedron</p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02223;</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><p>If the center <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> of the current ellipsoid lies in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math>, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> is an optimal solution of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>", "basis": "<p><b>Block as a whole.</b> The 4 T/F items worth 7 points each (28 in total) were the format of both 2026A and 2026B, and the format stays fixed within a year: probability about 0.85. Expected exact or near hits in this block: about 0.18. Expected sub-topic hits: about 0.9.</p><ul><li><b>Same slide, other half.</b> 2026B-1a ('if the center of the current ellipsoid is a feasible solution of the LP, then it is necessarily an optimal solution', False) was built from the LP deck's ellipsoid slides. Slide 80 of the same deck, 'Reducing Feasibility to Optimization', gives the true version: 'Step 3: Find optimum by running the ellipsoid algorithm on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02223;</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>. By strong duality, if (P) and (D) are feasible, there exist feasible solutions whose objective are equal. This step will return them.' Slide 85 adds 'At step <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>: if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></math>, return <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></math>.' Recitations 7-9 say 'We use the Strong Duality theorem to reduce from optimal solution to feasibility.'</li><li><b>In a Moed C, slot (a) takes the topic of that year's Moed B.</b> 2024C-1a (the number of vertices) took the topic of 2024B-1b (the vertex test). 2025C-1a (roots of unity) took the topic of 2025B-1a. Each later sitting reused at least one T/F sub-topic from the sitting before it (6 of 6). Slot (a) held an LP item in 7 of 10 exams, including both 2026 papers.</li><li><b>Answers flip along a chain.</b> Schwartz-Zippel went F, T, F, F and isolation went T, T, F. Volk comes back to a slide claim and reverses the answer. This is the True counterpart of 2026B-1a (which was False by design): 'feasible implies optimal' does hold for a center in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math>, but not for a center in the LP's own feasible region. The mined digest gives this exact twin its top ranking (L4, F5).</li></ul><p><b>Probability.</b> Ellipsoid sub-topic in the 2026C T/F block: about 0.22. This item or a near copy: about 0.06.</p>", "sol": "<p><b>True.</b> If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math>, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> is a feasible solution of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Also <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is a feasible solution of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. By weak duality, every feasible solution <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> satisfies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. Together with the constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math>,</p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>for&#x000A0;every&#x000A0;feasible&#x000A0;solution&#x000A0;</mtext><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mtext>&#x000A0;of&#x000A0;</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><p>so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> is optimal for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. In fact <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is optimal for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> as well. This is Step 3 of the reduction from optimization to feasibility that we saw in class. When both programs are feasible, strong duality gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo></mrow></math>, so the ellipsoid algorithm on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math> returns an optimal pair (as on slide 80; strictly speaking, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math> has no interior, since it lies in the hyperplane <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, so one writes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02223;</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and runs the algorithm on the slightly relaxed system <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></msup><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow></mrow></math> of Problem Set 4, Question 1. The statement is about a center that lies in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math>, so this does not change the answer).</p><p><em>Compare 2026B-1a (False):</em> there the algorithm runs on the feasible region of the LP alone, and a feasible center need not be optimal. For example, for max <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> subject to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the starting center <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> is feasible but not optimal. The extra constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is what makes the difference.</p>", "srcline": "<p>Volk, LP lecture deck, slide 80 'Reducing Feasibility to Optimization', Step 3 (LP lecture deck), and slide 85 'Ellipsoid Method Overview', 'At step <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>: if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></math>... return <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></math>'. Also the 'Ellipsoid Method' slide of recitations 7-9. This is 2026B-1a with the answer flipped.</p>"}, {"part": "b", "difficulty": "easy", "summary": "A nonzero degree-d polynomial need not have a non-root in S^n when S has exactly d elements (False).", "q": "<p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> be a non-zero polynomial over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0211A;</mi></mrow></math> of degree <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mi>&#x0211A;</mi></mrow></math> be a set of size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>. Then there exist <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> such that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p>", "basis": "<ul><li><b>The longest T/F chain in the bank.</b> Schwartz-Zippel was a T/F item in 4 of 10 exams: 2023B-1c (F: a non-zero polynomial in 5 variables that vanishes on all integers), 2024C-1b (T: a non-root in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math>), 2025B-1a (F: roots of unity of order <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>) and 2026A-1b (F: an arbitrary set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><msup><mi>&#x0211A;</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math> of size 100). Each one is a one-line twist on the slide bound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>: 2023B-1c applies it over the integers, 2024C-1b and 2025B-1a apply it to a specific set larger than the degree, and 2026A-1b drops the product-set hypothesis. 2024C-1b is the key sentence of the official 2023B-1c solution, restated as an item.</li><li><b>Moed C re-skins old bank items in slots (b) and (c).</b> 2024C-b and 2024C-c re-skinned 2023B-c and 2023A-c. 2025C-b and 2025C-c re-skinned 2023A-d and 2023A-b. Changing the constant from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> is his 'swap a constant' trap (2023A-c, 2023B-d, 2024C-b, 2025B-a). The T/F research ranks 'Schwartz-Zippel with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>' as the top reused twist.</li><li><b>Textbook pair.</b> Jukna, Extremal Combinatorics, Exercise 16.4 is the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> statement ('If S a subset of F that has d+1 elements, then any nonzero polynomial of local degree d has a nonzero point in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math>'). Exercise 16.3 asks to show that the bound is tight, with a product of linear factors as the hint.</li><li><b>Why the False goes here.</b> 11 of the 40 past items were False, and 3 of those sat in slot (b). The randomized unit has 5 False out of 15. Items (a), (c) and (d) are all True, so (b) carries the False.</li></ul><p><b>Probability.</b> Schwartz-Zippel appears in the block: about 0.18. This item: about 0.04. The answer key 3T/1F with the False in (b): about 0.18.</p>", "sol": "<p><b>False.</b> Write <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and take</p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x0220F;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><p>This is a non-zero polynomial of degree exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>. For every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> we have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> for some <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>, so the factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> vanishes and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. The Schwartz-Zippel lemma only gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, which says nothing. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> the bound is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and the statement becomes true, as in 2024C-1b.</p>", "srcline": "<p>Volk, Randomized lecture deck, slide 38 'Schwartz Zippel Lemma' (Randomized lecture deck). A re-skin of 2024C-1b (the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> version, True; 2024C official solution P1(b)), which is itself the key step of the official 2023B-1c solution. Textbook pair: Jukna, Extremal Combinatorics, 2nd ed., Exercises 16.3-16.4, p.235.</p>"}, {"part": "c", "difficulty": "medium", "summary": "If the traces of A, A^2, ..., A^n are all zero, then A^n = 0, by Newton's identities and Cayley-Hamilton (True).", "q": "<p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>&#x0211D;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p>", "basis": "<ul><li><b>The only T/F sub-topic in both 2026 papers.</b> 2026A-1c (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><msubsup><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> by repeated squaring, True) and 2026B-1c (equal <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> implies the same eigenvalues, True) both come from the Algebraic deck slides 'Eigenvalues of Powers', 'Power sum polynomials' and 'Newton's identities' (slides 58-60). The chain continues 2024B-1d. Algebraic T/F items were True in 8 of 9 cases.</li><li><b>The next sentence of the same textbook problem.</b> Horn and Johnson, Matrix Analysis, 2nd ed., Problem 2.4.P10: 'Show that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> have the same characteristic polynomials, and hence the same eigenvalues, if and only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></math> for all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. Deduce that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> is nilpotent if and only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>.' 2026B-1c is the first sentence, and this item is the second. Prasolov, Problems and Theorems in Linear Algebra, Theorem 24.2.1, states the same fact.</li><li><b>An unused lecture claim closes the argument.</b> Cayley-Hamilton (slides 68-69, used there for matrix inversion) has never been examined. Here it gives the conclusion in one line.</li><li><b>Competing outcome.</b> A topic that was a T/F item in both A and B became a free-response problem in C twice: lattices in 2024 and spectral graphs in 2025. In 2026, traces and Newton's identities are that topic, so they may move to a free-response problem instead of this block.</li></ul><p><b>Probability.</b> Trace/Newton sub-topic in the block: about 0.30, the highest of the four. This item: about 0.05.</p>", "sol": "<p><b>True.</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x02102;</mi></mrow></math> be the eigenvalues of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>, with algebraic multiplicities. As we've seen in class, the eigenvalues of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></math> are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msubsup><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow></math>. So the power sums are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. By Newton's identities,</p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><p>so by induction on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x022EF;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Dividing by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> is allowed over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0211D;</mi></mrow></math>. Hence the characteristic polynomial of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> is</p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x0220F;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>k</mi></msup><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><p>and by the Cayley-Hamilton theorem <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. A shorter route: as we have seen in class, by Newton's identities, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> determine the characteristic polynomial of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> (Algebraic deck slides 59-63; this is also the argument of the official 2026B-1c solution). The zero matrix has the same traces of powers, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> has the same characteristic polynomial as the zero matrix, namely <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math>, and Cayley-Hamilton gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p><em>Sister trap:</em> the conclusion '<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>' would be false. The matrix <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x00028;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, but <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p>", "srcline": "<p>Volk, Algebraic lecture deck, slides 58-60 ('Eigenvalues of Powers', 'Power sum polynomials', 'Newton's identities') and slide 68 (Cayley-Hamilton, 'Matrix Inversion'), which follow Kozen Lecture 31 (Algebraic lecture deck). Horn and Johnson, Matrix Analysis, 2nd ed. (2013), Problem 2.4.P10, second sentence, p.126. Prasolov, Theorem 24.2.1. The sibling of 2026B-1c.</p>"}, {"part": "d", "difficulty": "medium", "summary": "If the lazy walk matrix of a regular graph has second eigenvalue at most 0.9, the graph has logarithmic diameter (True).", "q": "<p>There exists a constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> (that doesn't depend on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>) such that the following holds. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> be an undirected connected <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>-regular graph on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> vertices, let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> be its normalized adjacency matrix, and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>. If the second largest eigenvalue of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.9</mn></mrow></math>, then the diameter of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>.</p>", "basis": "<ul><li><b>A rested topic.</b> Random walks and spectral facts were examined in 2023B-1d, 2025A-1d and 2025B-1d (all in slot (d), all True) and in 2025C-P3 (lazy walk). Neither 2026 paper had one. Every Moed C so far added 1-2 class topics that its year's A and B skipped. Slot (d) has been True in 9 of 10 exams.</li><li><b>The 2026 recitation 4 works through exactly this claim.</b> Slides 13-16: '2. Claim: If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow></math> then the diameter of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>', using the lazy walk <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>, with the two-cliques counterexample to the converse. The recitation's first claim (the multiplicity of eigenvalue 1 equals the number of components) is 2025A-1d. The second claim has never been an exam item. The 2026 recitations rework past exam items (2023B-1c, 2023B-1d, 2024B-P4 and others), so their other claims are likely exam material too.</li><li><b>A re-skin of 2023B-1d.</b> That item said: if all eigenvalues except the largest are at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> in absolute value, a walk from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math> reaches <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> within <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> steps with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> (True). Its official solution is one line ('using the same analysis we saw in class'). Here the constant is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.9</mn></mrow></math> and the conclusion is about the diameter. The sister trap, 'diameter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> implies a constant spectral gap', is False by the recitation's two-cliques example.</li></ul><p><b>Probability.</b> A spectral or random-walk item in the block: about 0.20. This item: about 0.03.</p>", "sol": "<p><b>True.</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo>&#x022EF;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> be the eigenvalues of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow></math>. Fix vertices <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> be the vector that is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> in coordinate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> elsewhere. As we've seen in class,</p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x02016;</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x02016;</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msubsup><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><p>Take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x02308;</mi><mn>10</mn><mi>ln</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x02309;</mi></mrow></math> (for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>). Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mn>0.9</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>0.105</mn></mrow></math>, we get <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mn>0.9</mn><mrow><mn>10</mn><mi>ln</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>10</mn><mi>ln</mi><mn>0.9</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1.05</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>. So every coordinate satisfies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, which means <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for every vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>. In words, a lazy random walk of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> steps from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math> reaches <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> with positive probability. Removing the steps where the walk stays in place leaves a walk from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> of length at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>&#x02308;</mi><mn>10</mn><mi>ln</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x02309;</mi></mrow></math> for all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1.45</mn><mi>ln</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>,</p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>&#x02308;</mi><mn>10</mn><mi>ln</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x02309;</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>ln</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>12</mn><mi>ln</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>12</mn><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><p>so the statement holds with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></math> (whether <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>log</mi></mrow></math> is to base <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>e</mi></mrow></math>).</p><p><em>Note:</em> the converse is false (recitation 4). Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> be the second largest eigenvalue of the normalized adjacency matrix <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>; the second largest eigenvalue of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> is then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math>. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>. Take two cliques of size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, delete an edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> inside the first and an edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> inside the second, and add the edges <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>. The result is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>-regular with diameter 3. The vector <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> that is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> on the first clique and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> on the second is orthogonal to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow></mrow></math>, so writing it in the orthonormal eigenbasis of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow></mrow></math> (as in class) gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mi>x</mi><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>. A direct count gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mi>x</mi><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Hence the second largest eigenvalue of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> is at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow></math> once <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>, while the diameter is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>.</p>", "srcline": "<p>Recitation 4 (2026), slides 13-16, 'Claim: If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow></math> then the diameter of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>' (recitation 4). The bound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow><msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msubsup><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msubsup></mrow></math> is on Randomized deck slides 73-78 ('Calculating the error', 'Bounding the error'). A re-skin of 2023B-1d.</p>"}]}, {"id": "c26-mock-p2", "sid": "2026C MOCK · P2", "difficulty": "easy", "src": "Algebraic algorithms / fast matrix multiplication, 16 pts; continuation of 2026B-P2 (common neighbours via AA^T); ancestors Zwick TAU 2016 PS1 Ex 1.3(b) and Zwick matrix-graph slides Exercise 1(b)", "parts": [{"part": null, "difficulty": "easy", "summary": "Count the 4-cycles of a bipartite graph in O(n^omega) time by computing AA^T and summing (B_uv choose 2) over unordered pairs of left vertices.", "q": "<p><b>Problem 2 (16 points)</b></p>\n<p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> be a bipartite graph such that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. A <em>4-cycle</em> in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> is a set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> of four distinct vertices with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></math> such that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are all edges of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>Find an algorithm that runs in time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and, given <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math>, computes the number of 4-cycles in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math>.</p>", "basis": "<p><b>Lineage.</b> 2026B-P2 (18 pts) asked for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> for every pair <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> in time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Its official solution is one line: compute <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math> (2026B official solution). This problem keeps the same preamble, \"Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> be a bipartite graph such that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>\", and adds one counting step. 2025C-P2 continued 2025B-P2 in the same way, with the same set-cover preamble and dual rounding added.</p>\n<p><b>Pattern.</b> In Volk's exams (2023A-2026B), written matrix-multiplication problems have appeared only in Moed B/C: 2023B-P5 (transitive closure), 2024B-P4 (diameter), 2024C-P2 (Boolean product) and 2026B-P2 (common neighbours). His Moed A papers used the topic only as T/F items (2023A-1d, 2025A-1c, 2026A-1c). The rule does not hold before him: the pre-Volk 2018A Moed A paper asked for Strassen's recurrence in P1(a) (catalog 2018A-p1-a). In 2024, the Moed C algebraic problem (2024C-P2) was a sibling of the Moed B one (2024B-P4) from the same Zwick slides, but it used a different trick. In both Moed C papers so far, about two thirds of the points re-skinned earlier templates (2024C: 64 pts; 2025C: 68 pts).</p>\n<p><b>Source adjacency.</b> The Zwick TAU pool produced 2023B-P5, 2024B-P4 and 2024C-P2. It also contains the unused exercise \"Give an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>-time algorithm for finding a simple 4-cycle\" (Advanced Algorithms 2016, Problem Set 1, Ex 1.3(b)). This is the same as the slide \"Exercise 1... b) a simple quadrangle\". The pool also has the slide \"A graph G contains a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></math> iff <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> ?\". Volk's own homework counts triangles as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math> (Spring-2024 Problem Set 2, Problem 4: 2025 homework 2 solution, catalog id hw2025s2-p4; the folder is named 2025). Its solution first notes that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> counts the paths of length 2 from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>, but it never computes common-neighbour counts for each pair. 2026B-P2 is plausibly a bipartite re-skin of the Algebraic deck's 'Adjacency Matrix' <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> slide and this homework (the research findings: PLAUSIBLE, unverified). The research findings also lists 2024B-P4 as a candidate parent, and the source-pool analysis credits the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> step to the Zwick Matrix-Graph slides. Whatever its parent, counting 4-cycles from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> is the next step.</p>\n<p><b>Against.</b> The mined-source digest scores this candidate L3/F5 (the mined-exercise list), but it calls a close variant \"possible but unlikely right after B\". The reason is that 2026B-P2 already used the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math> idea, and in 2024 the Moed C sibling switched tricks. For this reason the solution also covers the sibling problems (squaring, Seidel diameter, witnesses) and the trace/characteristic-polynomial promotion variant.</p>\n<p><b>Probability.</b> This problem or a near variant (detecting or counting 4-cycles or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, per-vertex counts, general graphs via <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>): <b>0.05</b>. Any matrix-multiplication graph-counting problem at P2: 0.25. Any algebraic or PIT problem at P2: 0.62. PIT at P2 has never happened (about 0.10). FFT at P2 is about 0.13, spread over several instances. Even at 0.05, this is still the single most likely P2 instance.</p>", "sol": "<p><b>Algorithm.</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> be the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> biadjacency matrix of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math>. Its rows are indexed by the vertices of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> and its columns by the vertices of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> otherwise. Compute <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math> using fast matrix multiplication and output\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nwhere the sum runs over all unordered pairs of distinct vertices <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> (fix any order on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>).</p>\n<p><b>Step 1: the entries of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>.</b> For distinct <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>, let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></math> be the set of common neighbours of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>. Then\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nsince <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow></math> is a common neighbour of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> otherwise. (This is the identity from Moed B, Problem 2. It takes one line, so write it out.)</p>\n<p><b>Step 2: every 4-cycle is counted exactly once.</b> <em>Claim:</em> the number of 4-cycles in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> equals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p><em>Proof.</em> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi></mrow></math> be the set of pairs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> such that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> has two elements and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x02286;</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> has two elements. For a fixed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> there are exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> ways to choose <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. We show that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x021A6;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x02229;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x02229;</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is a bijection from the set of 4-cycles onto <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>It maps into <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi></mrow></math> and is one-to-one.</em> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> be a 4-cycle. By definition <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x02229;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x02229;</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, each with exactly two elements. Each of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> is adjacent to both <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x02286;</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x02229;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x02229;</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, different 4-cycles have different images.</li>\n<li><em>It is onto.</em> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></math>. The four vertices are distinct because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x02229;</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo></mrow></math>. The edges <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are all in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow></math> because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is a 4-cycle, and its image is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Hence the number of 4-cycles is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>. By Step 1, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>, so the algorithm outputs exactly the number of 4-cycles. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x025FB;</mi></mrow></math></p>\n<p>Bipartiteness is what makes each 4-cycle count once. The cycle alternates between the sides, so its two vertices in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> are opposite each other. A 4-cycle therefore has exactly one opposite pair inside <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>, and we only sum over pairs in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>. Watch for two mistakes. Summing over ordered pairs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> counts every 4-cycle twice. Including the diagonal adds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>deg</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and those terms are not 4-cycles.</p>\n<p><b>Running time.</b> Building <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math> takes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> time. Computing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math> is one product of two <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> matrices. As we have seen in class, this takes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> time (for example <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>7</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with Strassen's algorithm). There are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> pairs, and each term <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> takes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> arithmetic operations on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>-bit integers: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and the answer is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></math>. The total is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p><b>Sanity check.</b> For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> for all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>, so the output is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. This is correct, since any two vertices of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> together with any two vertices of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow></math> form a 4-cycle. For a single 4-cycle plus isolated vertices, exactly one pair has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> and the output is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p><b>Where the points are</b> (estimated weighting; no official rubric exists for this mock): the product <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math> with its entry identity; the no-double-counting argument (unordered pairs, diagonal excluded, \"choose 2\" rather than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></math>); and the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> running-time analysis.</p>\n<p><b>Variants with the same idea (practise these).</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Detect a 4-cycle and output one.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> has a 4-cycle if and only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> for some <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>. Scan the off-diagonal entries of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. For such a pair, scan rows <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and take the first two <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The total is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>4-cycles through a given vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>.</b> The vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> must be one of the two <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>-vertices, so the count is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Only row <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> is needed. It equals the vector-matrix product of row <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> time is enough. For a vertex of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow></math>, use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> instead. Summing the per-vertex counts over all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> gives twice the total.</li>\n<li><b>A general undirected graph</b> with adjacency matrix <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> edges and degrees <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math>:\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x00023;</mi><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nThe factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> is needed because a 4-cycle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> has two opposite pairs, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, and is counted once for each. For the trace form, sort the closed walks of length 4 into three kinds. A walk around a 4-cycle gives 8 walks per cycle (4 starting points times 2 directions). A walk <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></math> walks from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>. A walk <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>&#x0007E;</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> walks from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>. Summing over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>8</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00023;</mi><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>v</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. Both formulas were checked by brute force on random graphs.</li>\n<li><b>Triangles.</b> The number of triangles is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math> (Spring-2024 homework, Problem Set 2, Problem 4; catalog id hw2025s2-p4), and the number of triangles through <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Sibling problems from the same pool.</b> In 2024 the Moed C problem used a different trick from the same deck as Moed B, so practise at least one of these.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Squaring is as hard as multiplying</b> (Zwick PS1 Ex 1.1; DPV Ex 2.27(c)). Suppose <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> matrices can be squared in time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. For any <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></math>,\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>&#x00028;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mi>X</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>Y</mi></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x00028;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mi>X</mi><mi>Y</mi></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mi>Y</mi><mi>X</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nso <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mi>Y</mi></mrow></math> is the top-left block of one <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> square. This takes time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The other direction is trivial, since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>. DPV reaches the same result through <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>B</mi><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>, in time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Diameter at most 2 (Seidel's graph).</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> otherwise. The diameter of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> is at most 2 if and only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>. Checking this takes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> time. As seen in class, the graph <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math> whose adjacency matrix is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> satisfies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x02308;</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x02309;</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Witnesses for the Boolean product, unique case</b> (Zwick slides, 'Exercise n+1 (a)'). Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msubsup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mi>k</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, and this equals the witness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> whenever position <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> has exactly one witness. That is one product, in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> time.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Promotion variant (about 0.03).</b> Traces and the characteristic polynomial were T/F items in both 2026A and 2026B. <em>Statement:</em> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> be a simple undirected graph with adjacency matrix <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> edges and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> triangles, and write <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo>&#x022EF;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Prove that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p><em>Solution.</em> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> be the eigenvalues of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>, and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup></mrow></math>. As we have seen in class, the eigenvalues of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></math> are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Therefore <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> (there are no self-loops), <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>v</mi></msub><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>6</mn><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>. The coefficient of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>k</mi></msup><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. By Newton's identities (from class):\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>6</mn><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nSo <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, which gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>. This was checked numerically on random graphs. A related variant: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> is nilpotent if and only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> (by Cayley-Hamilton, since the characteristic polynomial is then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math>), and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math> can be computed by repeated squaring in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msup><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> time.</p>", "srcline": "<p>A predicted continuation of Volk's 2026 Moed B, Problem 2 (same preamble; the official solution computes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>). It is not a verbatim textbook exercise. <em>Ancestors</em> (finding, not counting, in directed graphs): Zwick, TAU Advanced Algorithms 2016, Problem Set 1, Exercise 1.3(b), \"Give an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>-time algorithm for finding a simple 4-cycle\". Zwick, 'Matrix and Graph Algorithms' slides, \"Exercise 1: Give <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> algorithms for finding, in a directed graph, a) a triangle b) a simple quadrangle\", and \"A graph G contains a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></math> iff <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> ?\". Volk, Spring-2024 Problem Set 2, Problem 4 (triangles <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>; 2025 homework 2 solution, catalog id hw2025s2-p4). <em>Siblings:</em> DPV Exercise 2.27(c) (squaring vs. multiplication); Volk's Algebraic deck, 'Adjacency Matrix' slides (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>, Seidel's graph <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math>).</p>"}]}, {"id": "c26-mock-p3", "sid": "2026C MOCK · P3", "difficulty": "easy", "src": "Randomized construction (Chernoff + union bound), 16 pts; Volk's 2026B-P3 with 2 colors changed to 3, the step that turned 2024B-P6 into 2025C-P5; ancestors 2023B-P6 and Mitzenmacher-Upfal 4.4", "stem": "<p>Exam rules as in 2026A/B: you may use any claim that was presented in class or proved in the homework. Unless stated otherwise, prove the correctness of your claims and analyze the running times of your algorithms.</p>", "parts": [{"part": null, "difficulty": "easy", "summary": "Prove that for large enough n, a graph on n vertices whose minimum degree is at least log squared n has a 3-coloring in which every vertex has at least 0.3 of its neighbors in each color class.", "q": "<p><b>Problem 3 (16 points).</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> be a graph with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> vertices in which the degree of every vertex is at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. Prove that for large enough <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, there's a coloring of the vertices in three colors, red, green and blue, such that every vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></math> has at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.3</mn><mi>deg</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> neighbors in each of the three color classes.</p><p><em>Moed C wording to expect (the form of 2024B-P6 and 2025C-P5):</em> Prove that for large enough <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, there is a randomized algorithm that, given <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math>, runs in time polynomial in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> and with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.9</mn></mrow></math> outputs a coloring with this property.</p>", "basis": "<p><b>Why this problem.</b> It is 2026B-P3 with two colors changed to three. That is the step Volk used to turn 2024B-P6 into 2025C-P5. 2025C is the paper after 2025B, whose own Chernoff problem was an estimator.</p><ul><li><b>Both Moed C papers re-skinned a Moed B Chernoff construction.</b> 2024C-P3 (a vector at Hamming distance at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> from up to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2024</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math> given vectors) applied the 2024B-P6 template to a new object in the same year. 2025C-P5 (the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>-tertiary matrix) is the previous year's 2024B-P6 with 2 symbols changed to 3: entries <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> became <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> became <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>. The two official solutions share whole sentences: <em>Set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> large enough such that for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, the above is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, and now do a union bound for all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></em>. In that step the tolerance did not change: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">|</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">|</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>100</mn></mrow></mfrac><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> became &quot;the number of entries of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></math> equal to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> is within <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.01</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>&quot; (2024B paper, 2025C paper). The literal analogue for 2026B-P3, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.4</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, keeps <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> and gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>7</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>30</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02248;</mo><mn>0.233</mn></mrow></math>, which a paper would state as &quot;at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.2</mn><mi>deg</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>&quot; (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>15</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, same proof). The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.3</mn></mrow></math> used here is one natural round threshold, not the literal analogue: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.3</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>30</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> changes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math> from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>30</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> and the allowed deviation from 20% to 10% of the mean. Only the constant in the exponent changes, so both versions are drilled below.</li><li><b>The slot.</b> In both 2026 papers, P3 was a single-part, 16-point randomized construction: 2026A-P3 (shattering, union bound) and 2026B-P3 (the 2-coloring, Chernoff plus a union bound). P3 was worth 16 points in 7 of Volk's 10 exams (2023A, 2023B, 2025A, 2025B, 2025C, 2026A, 2026B), including all five from 2025 and 2026; in 2024A/B/C it was worth 14, 12 and 18.</li><li><b>The topic.</b> A Chernoff or union-bound written problem appeared in 9 of the 10 exams from 2023A to 2026B. The only exception is 2023A, and both Moed C papers had one. Constructions outnumber estimators 7 to 2. The constructions are 2023B-P6, 2024B-P6, 2024C-P3, 2025A-P4, 2025C-P5, 2026A-P3 and 2026B-P3. The estimators are 2024A-P2 (the fraction of satisfying assignments of a CNF formula) and 2025B-P5.</li><li><b>Taught material.</b> The 2026 recitation 3 works the 2024B-P6 exam question as a slide titled &quot;Exam Question (2024B)&quot;. 2026A-P1(d) is the same template as a True/False item; 2023B-P6 (18 points, Small Discrepancy: a coloring with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi></mrow><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>C</mi><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>) is the same template as a written problem. The Moed C analysis and the lecture-flow analysis both predict a re-skin of 2026A-P3 or 2026B-P3.</li><li><b>Against.</b> 2026A/B reused no older written problem near-verbatim, so this exact skin is one of several. Other skins: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> colors, a two-sided band, a set family, a re-skin of the 2026A-P3 shattering problem, or the Jukna tournament theorem. The coverage analysis prefers a sampling estimator (last used in 2025B-P5) as the variation that avoids repeating 2026; that is the alternate alt-p3-a.</li></ul><p><b>Calibrated probabilities.</b> This exact re-skin (3 or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> colors, any constant thresholds): <b>0.06</b>. A Chernoff or union-bound construction as P3: <b>0.48</b>. A Chernoff or union-bound written problem anywhere in 2026C: <b>0.85</b>.</p>", "sol": "<p><b>The random coloring.</b> Color every vertex independently and uniformly at random with one of the colors <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> (red, green, blue), each with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>. We show that for large enough <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.9</mn></mrow></math> every vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> has at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.3</mn><mi>deg</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> neighbors of each color. In particular, such a coloring exists.</p><p><b>One vertex, one color.</b> Fix <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></math> and a color <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>. For every neighbor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>, let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> be an indicator random variable for the event that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is colored <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>, and let<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math></div>be the number of neighbors of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> colored <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> has no self-loops, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> is not one of its own neighbors, and the color of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> plays no role. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, linearity of expectation gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>.</p><p>Different vertices are colored independently. So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is a sum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>deg</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> independent indicator random variables, each equal to 1 with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>. As we have seen in class (the Chernoff bound), such a sum <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math> satisfies, for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>,<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>p</mi><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>We apply it with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>30</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>. Note that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>30</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>9</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>30</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.3</mn></mrow></math>. Hence<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0.3</mn><mi>deg</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>30</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>deg</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>900</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>deg</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>450</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>ln</mi><mi>n</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>450</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>The last step uses <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msup><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>ln</mi><mi>n</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>, which holds when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>log</mi></mrow></math> is to base 2 or base <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>e</mi></mrow></math>. Any other fixed base only changes the constant 450.</p><p><b>Union bound.</b> There are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> pairs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The events for different pairs are not independent, because neighborhoods overlap. The union bound does not need independence:<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mo>&#x02203;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02203;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>:</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0.3</mn><mi>deg</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>ln</mi><mi>n</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>450</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>6</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>ln</mi><mi>n</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>450</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>The exponent tends to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo>&#x0221E;</mo></mrow></math>, so for large enough <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> this is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.1</mn></mrow></math>. Concretely, if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>455</mn></mrow></math> then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>ln</mi><mi>n</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>450</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>455</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>450</mn></mrow></mfrac><mi>ln</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>ln</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></math>, so the bound is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>6</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p>Therefore, with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.9</mn></mrow></math>, the random coloring gives every vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.3</mn><mi>deg</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> neighbors of each of the three colors. This probability is positive, so such a coloring exists.</p><p><b>The algorithmic (Moed C) wording.</b> The algorithm colors the vertices at random as above and outputs the coloring. It runs in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> time and, by the analysis above, is correct with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.9</mn></mrow></math>. A coloring can also be checked in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> time by counting, for every vertex, its neighbors of each color. So, for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> beyond the threshold of the proof (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>455</mn></mrow></math>), one can re-sample until the check passes. Each round succeeds with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.9</mn></mrow></math>, so the expected number of rounds is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>0.9</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. For small <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> a valid coloring may not exist at all (see the next paragraph), and then this loop never stops.</p><p><b>Where marks are lost.</b></p><ul><li><b>&quot;For large enough <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>&quot; is needed.</b> Without it the claim is false. In the complete graph <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mn>20</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> every degree is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>19</mn><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>20</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02248;</mo><mn>18.68</mn></mrow></math>, but every vertex needs at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x02308;</mi><mn>0.3</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mn>19</mn><mi>&#x02309;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math> neighbors of each color. So every color class has at least 6 vertices, and then a vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> in a class needs 6 more vertices of its own color besides itself, so every class has at least 7 vertices, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>21</mn><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow></math>. The complete graphs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>22</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>23</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>25</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>26</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>28</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>29</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>32</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>35</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>38</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> fail in the same way. Keep the hypothesis in your answer, and say where the proof uses it (the last step of the union bound).</li><li><b>Why <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>.</b> With a constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math>, one pair <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> fails with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>deg</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, and this has to beat the factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> of the union bound. When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msup><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, it is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. If only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>ln</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> is given, the bound is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>450</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>6</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>450</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math> is useless. You would need <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>ln</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>450</mn></mrow></math>; for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>900</mn></mrow></math> the union bound gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>6</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>6</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>.</li><li><b>The constant-free form.</b> The official 2026B solution writes &quot;at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>deg</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><msup><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math> for some constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>&quot;. The 2024B-P6 and 2025C-P5 solutions write <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msup></mrow></math> in the same way. This is fine if you say which Chernoff bound you use and what <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math> is. Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>deg</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>450</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>deg</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>450</mn><mi>ln</mi><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math>.</li><li><b>Use the class form of Chernoff.</b> The 2026 lecture deck and recitation 3 present only the additive form <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>p</mi><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>; neither they nor the 2026 homework state a multiplicative bound. The lower-tail bound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math> (Mitzenmacher-Upfal Theorem 4.5) would give <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>deg</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>600</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math> here (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.9</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.3</mn></mrow></math>), but it is not a claim presented in class or proved in the homework, so under the exam rules you would have to prove it. The class form above is the safe choice.</li><li><b>Two-sided for free.</b> The same event <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>30</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> also gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>11</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>30</mn></mrow></mfrac><mi>deg</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0.37</mn><mi>deg</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. So the band variant, &quot;between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.3</mn><mi>deg</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.37</mn><mi>deg</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> neighbors of each color&quot;, has the same proof.</li></ul><p><b>Same template, other skins (worth drilling).</b></p><ul><li><b>Three colors with the 2026B tolerance,</b> at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.2</mn><mi>deg</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> neighbors of each color (the literal analogue of the 2024B-P6 to 2025C-P5 step keeps <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, giving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>7</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>30</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02248;</mo><mn>0.233</mn></mrow></math>, rounded down to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.2</mn></mrow></math>). Take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>15</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>. One pair fails with probability at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>15</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>deg</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>8</mn><mi>deg</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>225</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>8</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>ln</mi><mi>n</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>225</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, and the union bound over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> pairs gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>6</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>8</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>ln</mi><mi>n</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>225</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, which is below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.1</mn></mrow></math> once <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow></math>.</li><li><b><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> colors,</b> at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> neighbors of each, when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>C</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>ln</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. Take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>. One pair fails with probability at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>deg</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. The union bound over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> pairs gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>.</li><li><b>Set family</b> (a variant inspired by 2023B-P6 and 2026A-P1(d), not a past exam problem as stated). Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msup><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. Show there is a red/blue coloring in which every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> is between 40% and 60% red. Take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>. One set fails with probability at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>ln</mi><mi>n</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, and the union bound over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></mrow></math> sets tends to 0.</li><li><b>Far vector</b> (2024C-P3). A uniform <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math> satisfies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>18</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math> for each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>). The union bound over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2024</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math> vectors gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2024</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>18</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li><li><b>Balanced bipartite graph and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>-tertiary matrix</b> (2024B-P6, 2025C-P5). Apply Chernoff to one pair <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. Take a union bound over the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math> pairs, and then over all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>.</li><li><b>Tournaments</b> (Jukna, <em>Extremal Combinatorics</em>, Thm 3.1, Erd&#337;s). If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, there is a tournament on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> players in which, for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> players, some other player beats all of them. For a set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> players, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. Then take a union bound over the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sets. This is the theorem right before Thm 3.2, the source of 2026A-P3; about 0.03.</li></ul>", "srcline": "<p>A re-skin of Volk's own problem. <b>Parent:</b> Advanced Algorithms 2026 Moed B, Problem 3 (16 pts, Aug 17, 2026; 2026B official solution). Two colors became three and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.4</mn></mrow></math> became <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.3</mn></mrow></math> (one natural threshold; the tolerance-preserving analogue is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.2</mn></mrow></math>). <b>Same step:</b> 2024B-P6 (2024B paper) became 2025C-P5 (2025C paper). <b>Ancestors:</b> 2023B-P6 (Small Discrepancy); Mitzenmacher-Upfal &#167;4.4 &quot;Application: Set Balancing&quot;, Theorem 4.11, p. 71; 2026A-P1(d). <b>Chernoff form used:</b> the Randomized lecture deck (Randomized lecture deck, slide &quot;Chernoff Bound&quot;, formula on) and the recitation 3 worked exam question &quot;Exam Question (2024B)&quot; (recitation 3; the Chernoff form it uses is on).</p>"}]}, {"id": "c26-mock-p4", "sid": "2026C MOCK · P4", "difficulty": "medium", "src": "LP duality, 20 pts (6+6+8); LP deck 'Min-cut Max-flow' slides + DPV Exercise 7.25(c)-(e); same-year sibling of 2026A-P4 (DPV 7.28)", "stem": "<p><b>Problem 4 (6+6+8=20 points)</b></p>\n<p>Recall the max-flow LP from class. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> be a directed graph with a source <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math> and a sink <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></math>, such that no edge enters <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math> and no edge leaves <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>. Every edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math> has a capacity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. The maximum flow problem is described by the following linear program:</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>maximize</mtext></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\" /><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>subject&#x000A0;to</mtext></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>for&#x000A0;every&#x000A0;</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\" /><mtd columnalign=\"left\" /><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>for&#x000A0;every&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi><mi>&#x029F5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></math></div>\n<p>An <em><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math>-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> cut</em> is a set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>. Its capacity is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, the total capacity of the edges leaving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>.</p>", "parts": [{"part": "a", "difficulty": "medium", "summary": "Derive the dual of the max-flow LP through standard form and bring it to the min-cut LP with x_s = 1, x_t = 0 and y_uv >= x_u - x_v.", "q": "<p><b>[6 points]</b> Compute the dual linear program of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and show that it can be written as</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>minimize</mtext></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\" /><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>subject&#x000A0;to</mtext></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>for&#x000A0;every&#x000A0;</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\" /><mtd columnalign=\"left\" /><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\" /><mtd columnalign=\"left\" /><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>for&#x000A0;every&#x000A0;</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></math></div>\n<p>where there is a variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> for every edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math> and a variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> for every vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></math>, and the variables <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> are unrestricted in sign.</p>", "basis": "<p><b>Probability.</b> This problem or a near variant (a 2-3 part max-flow/min-cut dual problem): about 0.04. The family 'derive a dual and interpret it' in the P4 slot: 0.15 (0.30 anywhere on the exam). An LP-duality part anywhere on the exam: 0.60.</p>\n<p><b>For.</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is a sibling of 2026A-P4 (min-cost unit flow, dual = shortest path, also 6+6+8). It comes from the same slides: LP deck 'Another example: Max flow' (txt) and 'Min-cut Max-flow'. It also comes from the same exercise block of Dasgupta-Papadimitriou-Vazirani. Exercise 7.25, 'The dual of maximum flow', is three exercises before 7.28, the plausible source of 2026A-P4. Moed C re-skins problems from the same year: 2024C-P2 (Boolean matrix product) followed 2024B-P4 (diameter via matrix powers).</li>\n<li>The deck prints (D) without a derivation ('After taking the dual and cleaning it up a little bit, we get'). Every Moed C so far has a problem that fills in or re-runs a lecture proof: 2024C-P5 (LLL bound), 2024C-P6 (Farkas case of strong duality), 2025C-P3 (lazy random walk; its official solution works out the new eigenvalue bounds and then says that the rest of the proof is identical to the proof from class).</li>\n<li>2026B had only 7 LP points (T/F 1a). After the LP-light 2024B (12 points), 2024C gave LP 36 points. The lecture-flow analysis ranks this card 4.5/5.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Against.</b> the coverage analysis and the mined digest rate it lower because it repeats 2026A's flow LP in the same year. So the exact instance stays at 0.04, only marginally above the Farkas twin (0.035).</p>\n<p><b>This part.</b> The task is 2026A-P4(b), 'Compute the dual LP to (P)', also worth 6 points there, and it is DPV 7.25(c). Here the target (D) is printed, because it is on the slide, so the part is graded on the derivation.</p>", "sol": "<p>As we have seen in class, the dual of the standard-form LP <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> subject to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> subject to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>. We first bring <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to this form:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Maximizing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the same as minimizing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>For every edge add a slack variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and replace <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Write the conservation constraint of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> as (flow in) minus (flow out) equals 0.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>We get the standard-form LP</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>minimize</mtext></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\" /><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>subject&#x000A0;to</mtext></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>for&#x000A0;every&#x000A0;</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\" /><mtd columnalign=\"left\" /><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>for&#x000A0;every&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>t</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\" /><mtd columnalign=\"left\" /><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></math></div>\n<p>Its dual has a variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> for the capacity row of every edge and a variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> for the conservation row of every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>. They are unrestricted because they come from equality rows. The objective is to maximize <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, since the conservation rows have right-hand side 0. There is one dual constraint per column of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The column of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> has a single 1, in the capacity row of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and cost 0. It gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>The column of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> has a 1 in the capacity row of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> in the conservation row of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> (if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>) and a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> in the conservation row of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> (if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></math>). Its cost is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></math> and 0 otherwise. No edge enters <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math> and no edge leaves <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> always hold. This is where the assumption is used, and it leaves exactly four cases:</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>t</mi><mi>:</mi></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mi>t</mi><mi>:</mi></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>t</mi><mi>:</mi></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mi>t</mi><mi>:</mi></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></math></div>\n<p>Now substitute <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Negating a free variable leaves it free. The constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> becomes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. The objective becomes maximizing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, that is, minimizing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. After multiplying by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the four cases become</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>Finally, add the two variables <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></math> with the constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></math>, so all four cases read <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math>. This is exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>About the values: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and the dual of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> has optimum <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The two minus signs cancel. So the duality theorems from class, applied to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and its dual, say for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> that the value of every feasible solution of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is at most the value of every feasible solution of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and that the two optima are equal when both LPs are feasible.</p>\n<p>(A shortcut, with the same result: by the general rules, a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo></mrow></math> constraint of a maximization LP gets a nonnegative dual variable, an equality gets a free one, and a nonnegative primal variable gives a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo></mrow></math> constraint. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> on the capacity constraints and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> on the conservation constraints written as in minus out, this gives the four constraints above directly.)</p>\n<p><em>Grading notes.</em> The deck already prints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, so the points are for the derivation. Typical errors:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The wrong sign on the free variables. Taking <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. This is the same LP after renaming <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, but the renaming has to be written out.</li>\n<li>Mishandling the edges at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>. The objective coefficient of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> becomes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. There are no conservation rows for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>; the constants <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></math> stand in for them.</li>\n<li>Putting a sign constraint on the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math>. They come from equality constraints and are free.</li>\n</ul>", "srcline": "LP deck (LP lecture deck), slides 'Another example: recall the max-flow LP' and 'Min-cut Max-flow', which print (D) 'after taking the dual and cleaning it up a little bit'. Dasgupta, Papadimitriou, Vazirani, <em>Algorithms</em>, Exercise 7.25(c), p.244: 'Write down the dual of this general flow LP, using a variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math> for each edge and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></math> for each vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>'. Task wording as in 2026A-P4(b)."}, {"part": "b", "difficulty": "easy", "summary": "Turn every s-t cut into a feasible dual solution of the same value and conclude by weak duality that every flow is at most every cut.", "q": "<p><b>[6 points]</b> Show that for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math>-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> cut <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> there is a feasible solution of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> whose value is exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Conclude that the value of every flow is at most the capacity of every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math>-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> cut.</p>", "basis": "<p>This is DPV 7.25(e) almost word for word: 'Show that any s-t cut in the network can be translated into a dual feasible solution whose cost is exactly the capacity of that cut.' It is also the lecture's own interpretation of (D): 'Suppose we have a solution where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>... Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>:</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>... The objective is to minimize the sum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>'s crossing the cut'. The 'conclude' clause is weak duality. Volk asked students to prove weak duality in 2023B-P2 and to prove it for SDPs in 2026B-P5(c). If this problem appears, this part is near certain (DPV has it as a separate item), and it is the easy 6 points of the problem.</p>", "sol": "<p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> be an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math>-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> cut. Set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>. For every edge set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> (the edge leaves <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>), and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> otherwise.</p>\n<p><b>Feasibility.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> we check the cases:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Value.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p><b>Conclusion.</b> By (a), <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the dual of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. By weak duality, as we have seen in class, the value of every feasible solution of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, that is, of every flow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math>, is at most the value of every feasible solution of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Applied to the solution above, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">v</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> for every flow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> and every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math>-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> cut <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>Weak duality for this pair can also be checked directly. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> be a flow and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> feasible for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. We use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, the fact that no edge enters <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math>, and conservation at every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">v</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>The first inequality uses <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The second uses <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p><em>Grading notes.</em> All three sign cases must be checked, including the edges that enter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> is only feasible because the constraint is an inequality. For 'conclude', citing weak duality is enough.</p>", "srcline": "DPV, <em>Algorithms</em>, Exercise 7.25(e), p.244. LP deck slide 'Min-cut Max-flow', the cut reading of 0/1 dual solutions. DPV §7.4 'Duality', p.222 (dpv-chap7.txt): 'it is possible to assign interpretations to the dual variables that show the dual to be none other than the minimum-cut problem (Exercise 7.25)'."}, {"part": "c", "difficulty": "hard", "summary": "Prove the class claim that the dual has an optimal 0/1 solution by rounding any dual solution with a random threshold r, conclude that the dual optimum is the minimum cut, and get max-flow min-cut from strong duality.", "q": "<p><b>[8 points]</b> Prove the following claim, which was stated in class without proof: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> has an optimal solution in which <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></math>. Conclude that the optimal value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> equals the minimum capacity of an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math>-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> cut in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math>, and that the maximum value of a flow equals the minimum capacity of an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math>-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> cut. You may not use the max-flow min-cut theorem in this part.</p>\n<p><em>Hint:</em> given a feasible solution <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, choose <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow></math> uniformly at random in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and consider <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi><mi>:</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>", "basis": "<p>This is the step the lecture skips: 'One can also prove that the optimum of the dual is such that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>... This proves (using LP duality) the min cut max flow theorem' (LP deck). The deck repeats the claim on the matching-relaxation slide: 'A similar thing happened earlier when we considered the dual of the max flow LP, which was the min cut LP'. Filling in a skipped lecture step is the Moed C re-prove habit (2024C-P5, 2024C-P6, 2025C-P3), and the wording follows 2024C-P6, 'Prove the following case of the strong duality theorem'. The threshold rounding <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>:</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow></math> uniform is the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow></math>-trick of hw2026 sheet 3 Q3 (densest subgraph: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>v</mi><mi>:</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow></math> uniform in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>). So the tool counts as proved in the homework. The exam rules allow any claim presented in class, so a statement that only asked for 'optimum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> = minimum cut' would let a student cite the slide's integrality claim and finish in three lines. Asking to prove that claim rules this out. The clause forbidding the max-flow min-cut theorem closes the other shortcut: with (b) and strong duality, the theorem gives the claim at once. The hint keeps this a fair 8 points. If Volk wants a lighter (c), expect DPV 7.25(d) (every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math>-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> path has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>-length at least 1), the same telescoping sum as 2026A-P4(c).</p>\n<p><b>LP-slot variants to practise.</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The Farkas twin, hw2026 sheet 3 Q5(b): if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is infeasible and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is feasible, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is unbounded. Its twin, 2024C-P6, was taken from the homework verbatim. About 0.035. The official solution is at 2026 homework 3 solution.</li>\n<li>The dual of the vertex-cover LP is fractional matching, and weak duality gives that every matching is at most every vertex cover. This is 2023A-P4 in reverse.</li>\n<li>Pre-Volk 2015B-P7: the independent-set LP has value at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> (all halves), a clique shows the gap, and the dual ILP is minimum edge cover. Volk has reused pre-Volk items 3 times (2014A to 2023A, 2014B to 2024C, 2021B to 2023A), once in a Moed C.</li>\n<li>The densest-subgraph LP (hw2026 sheet 3 Q3).</li>\n</ul>", "sol": "<p><b>Step 1: a 0/1 solution of value equal to the minimum cut.</b> There is at least one <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math>-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> cut (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>) and there are finitely many. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup></mrow></math> be a cut of minimum capacity. By (b), the solution <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02209;</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> exactly on the edges leaving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup></mrow></math>, is feasible for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and has value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. All its <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup></mrow></math> are in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p><b>Step 2: every feasible solution of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> has value at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> be feasible for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Choose <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow></math> uniformly at random in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi><mi>:</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math> is always an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math>-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> cut: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x02209;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>For an edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math>, let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> be an indicator random variable for the event that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> leaves <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>, that is, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02209;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. This happens exactly when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></math>, that is, when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow></math> lies in the interval <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>.\n<ul>\n<li>If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math>, the interval is empty and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Otherwise, since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow></math> is uniform in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> is the length of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02229;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>. This is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> need not lie in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, but intersecting with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> only shortens the interval.</li>\n</ul>\nIn both cases <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, linearity of expectation and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> give</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>A random variable is at most its expectation with positive probability. So there is an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p><b>Proof of the claim.</b> By Step 2, every feasible solution of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> has value at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. By Step 1, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is feasible and has value exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an optimal solution of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p><b>The optimum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</b> The same two steps give <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>min</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, the minimum capacity of an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math>-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> cut. (Starting instead from any optimal solution with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>: for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>:</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> every edge leaving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and (b) gives the other inequality.)</p>\n<p><b>Max-flow min-cut.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is feasible (take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>), and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is feasible by (b). By strong duality, as we have seen in class, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. So the maximum value of a flow equals the minimum capacity of an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math>-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> cut.</p>\n<p><em>Remark (no randomness needed).</em> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math> changes only when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow></math> passes one of the values <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></math>. For every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></msub></mrow></math> where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>. This minimum exists and lies in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></math>. So trying the at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> values <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> finds a cut of capacity at most the value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Applied to an optimal solution of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, this computes a minimum cut in polynomial time.</p>\n<p><em>Grading notes.</em> Common gaps:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>not checking that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math> contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math> and not <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>;</li>\n<li>writing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> without handling <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> or values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> outside <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>;</li>\n<li>proving only one of the two inequalities;</li>\n<li>citing the claim itself (it is what the part asks to prove), or the max-flow min-cut theorem: with (b) and strong duality, the theorem gives the claim at once, so it earns nothing here.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A route through total unimodularity is also acceptable, but only if it proves that the constraint matrix of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, brought to the form of the class claim, is TUM, and that an optimal vertex exists. It still needs a rounding step, since TUM gives integral <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, not <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>: for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>:</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, integrality gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> on every edge leaving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>, so the cut solution of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> from (b) has value at most the optimum and is itself optimal.</p>\n<p><b>Lighter variant (DPV 7.25(d)).</b> 'Show that every feasible solution of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> satisfies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for every directed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math>-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> path <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow></math>.' Solution: for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>a telescoping sum, exactly as in 2026A-P4(c).</p>", "srcline": "LP deck slide 'Min-cut Max-flow': 'One can also prove that the optimum of the dual is such that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>... This proves (using LP duality) the min cut max flow theorem. Rule of life: if you have a linear program, try to take its dual and interpret it!' Threshold rounding as in hw2026 sheet 3 Q3(d) (2026 homework 3 solution: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> defined in Q3(c), <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>v</mi></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> in Q3(d)). Lighter variant: DPV Exercise 7.25(d), p.244 (dpv-chap7.txt)."}]}, {"id": "c26-mock-p5", "sid": "2026C MOCK · P5", "difficulty": "medium", "src": "Approximation deck / SDP, 20 pts (6+6+8): continues 2026B-P5 (same (P), (D), weak duality); lifted from Feige, Weizmann Approximation Algorithms 2018/19 (https://www.wisdom.weizmann.ac.il/~feige/Apx2018/), Handout 6 homework item 2 (triangle SDP = 9/4), the item right after the W&amp;S Ex 6.1 item; W&amp;S 6.1 is the plausible source of 2026B-P5 (graded PLAUSIBLE/NEAR, not confirmed)", "stem": "<p><b>Problem 5 (6+6+8 = 20 points)</b></p>\n<p>Let (P) be the following SDP problem:</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>minimize</mtext></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\" /><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>subject&#x000A0;to</mtext></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>&#x02203;</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>&#x0211D;</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mtext>&#x000A0;such&#x000A0;that</mtext></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\" /><mtd columnalign=\"left\" /><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>&#x027E9;</mi><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>for&#x000A0;every&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\" /><mtd columnalign=\"left\" /><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>for&#x000A0;every&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></math></div>\n<p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>&#x0211D;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math> be the matrix whose <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>-th entry is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, and suppose <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> is symmetric. For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> variables <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> denote a diagonal matrix whose <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>-th entry is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The dual problem (D) is the following problem:</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>maximize</mtext></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\" /><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>subject&#x000A0;to</mtext></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;is&#x000A0;PSD</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></math></div>\n<p>You may use without proof the <b>weak duality theorem</b>: if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> is a feasible solution for (P) and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is a feasible solution for (D), then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Denote by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> the optimal (minimum) value of (P).</p>\n<p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> be an undirected graph with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>. Recall the SDP relaxation of Max Cut that we saw in class:</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>max</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>&#x027E9;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>&#x0211D;</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;for&#x000A0;every&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></mrow></math></div>\n<p>(every edge appears once in the sum), and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> denote the maximum number of edges crossing a cut <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mi>&#x029F5;</mi><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math>.</p>", "parts": [{"part": "a", "difficulty": "easy", "summary": "Show that the Max-Cut SDP value equals |E|/2 minus the optimum of the 2026B primal (P) with C = A/4, and that it is at least the max cut.", "q": "<p>(6 points) Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> be the adjacency matrix of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math>, and consider (P) with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>, that is, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> otherwise. Prove that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>", "basis": "<p><b>Why this problem.</b> Moed C continues same-year problems: 2025C-P2 continued 2025B-P2 with the set-cover preamble word for word (the B part did primal threshold rounding and the C part did dual rounding, W&amp;S section 1.4). 2024C and 2025C each re-used 36-38 points of same-year templates. This problem copies the (P) and (D) preamble of 2026B-P5 word for word and turns 2026B-P5(c), the weak duality theorem that students had to prove, into a given fact. So a student who solved 2026B-P5 already has every tool. Part (a) is the glue any such continuation needs. It identifies 2026B's abstract (P) with the Max-Cut SDP of the Approximation deck (slides 'Max Cut SDP' and 'Bounding the ratio'). That identification is exactly what W&amp;S Exercise 6.1 takes for granted. W&amp;S 6.1 is the plausible source of 2026B-P5 (graded PLAUSIBLE/NEAR in the findings, not confirmed): 2026B-P5 proves weak duality for a general symmetric <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> through the vectors <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while W&amp;S 6.1 is the Max-Cut special case.</p>\n<p><b>Slot.</b> P5 was a 6+6+8 = 20 problem from the Approximation deck in both 2026A (Max DiCut) and 2026B (SDP duality).</p>\n<p><b>Estimated probability</b> (this item's own rough estimates; no research report gives these numbers). This continuation, whether the triangle or the general bound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with the same (P) and (D): about 0.045. Any SDP or Goemans-Williamson problem anywhere on the paper: about 0.22. Some Approximation-deck problem at P5: about 0.55. For comparison, the Moed C analysis rates, as families anywhere on the paper, a multi-part LP/SDP duality problem at about 80% and an LP-based approximation problem at about 65%.</p>", "sol": "<p>Fix unit vectors <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>&#x0211D;</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math> and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>&#x027E9;</mi></mrow></math>. The only nonzero entries of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math> (there are no self-loops, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>). Every edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> therefore appears twice in the objective of (P), once as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and once as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Hence</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>&#x027E9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>&#x027E9;</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>The Max-Cut SDP objective of the same vectors is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>&#x027E9;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>&#x027E9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>Both problems range over exactly the same feasible solutions (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> unit vectors in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>&#x0211D;</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math>), and on every feasible solution the Max-Cut objective equals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> minus the objective of (P). So a solution maximizes the first objective if and only if it minimizes the second, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. (Both optima are attained, because the set of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>-tuples of unit vectors is compact and both objectives are continuous.)</p>\n<p>For the second claim, let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mi>&#x029F5;</mi><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> be any cut and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></math>. These are unit vectors, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>&#x027E9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> are on the same side of the cut and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> otherwise. So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>&#x027E9;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> if the edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> crosses the cut and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> otherwise, and the SDP objective of this solution is exactly the number of edges crossing the cut. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is a maximum over all feasible solutions, it is at least the size of every cut, and in particular <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. (This is the fact <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math> used in class: the SDP is a relaxation of the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x000B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> integer program.)</p>", "srcline": "<p>Preamble: Volk, 2026 Moed B, Problem 5. The definitions of (P), <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and (D) are copied word for word and in the same order (2026B official solution). The weak duality theorem is the statement of 2026B-P5(c), given here without proof, whereas 2026B asked students to prove it. The rest of the stem (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, the Max-Cut SDP, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>) is new. SDP objective: Approximation deck, 'Max Cut SDP' slides (Approximation lecture deck). The identity is the (P)-form of Williamson &amp; Shmoys, Exercise 6.1 (ws-book.txt; https://www.designofapproxalgs.com/book.pdf). Substituting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> turns W&amp;S's dual (minimize <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02211;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02211;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> subject to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>&#x02AB0;</mi><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>) into <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>.</p>"}, {"part": "b", "difficulty": "easy", "summary": "Give three unit vectors at 120 degrees showing that the Max-Cut SDP value of the triangle is at least 9/4.", "q": "<p>(6 points) Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> be the triangle, that is, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>. Give unit vectors <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>&#x0211D;</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></math> which show that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>9</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>.</p>", "basis": "<p><b>Source adjacency.</b> In Feige's Weizmann course <em>Approximation Algorithms</em> (2018/19), Handout 6 homework item 1 is '(Problem 6.1 in [WS11])... the dual of the max-cut SDP... W + diag(<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B3;</mi></mrow></math>) is positive semidefinite'. That is the plausible source of 2026B-P5 (graded PLAUSIBLE/NEAR, not confirmed: 2026B-P5 is the general symmetric-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> version of this Max-Cut dual). The very next item is 'For the unweighted triangle graph (three vertices, three edges), the max-cut value is 2. Show that the maximum value of the SDP relaxation is exactly 9/4.' Feige's Handout 5 homework Q3 ('Based on questions 5.3 and 5.6 in [WS11]') has exactly 2026A-P5's (a) random 1/4, (b) the IP, (c) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> layout (the confirmed source of 2026A-P5 is W&amp;S 5.3 + 5.6 itself; Feige's handout is a sibling copy of it). So both 2026 P5 problems likely sit in this one pool.</p>\n<p><b>Method track record.</b> Mining the unused neighbours of verified sources is the only method that has predicted exact problems: Erickson FFT Exercise 2(c) and W&amp;S 5.3 + 5.6 made up 36 points of 2026A. This part is the primal (lower-bound) half of that neighbour.</p>\n<p><b>Fallback.</b> Even if the problem is not asked, the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>120</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02218;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math> computation is a natural 7-point T/F: 'For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> the Max-Cut SDP optimum equals the max cut' is False (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>9</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>). It is in the mined T/F pool.</p>", "sol": "<p>Take three unit vectors in a plane, at angles of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>120</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02218;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math> from each other:</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>They are unit vectors: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Their inner products are</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>&#x027E9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mi>&#x027E9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mi>&#x027E9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>So each of the three edges contributes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> to the objective, and the objective value of this feasible solution is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>9</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the maximum over all feasible solutions, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>9</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>(In the language of part (a): <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>&#x027E9;</mi></mrow></math> is feasible for (P) with value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>.)</p>", "srcline": "<p>Feige, <em>Approximation Algorithms</em>, Weizmann Institute 2018/19 (course page https://www.wisdom.weizmann.ac.il/~feige/Apx2018/), Handout 6 (30 Jan 2019), homework item 2: 'For the unweighted triangle graph (three vertices, three edges), the max-cut value is 2. Show that the maximum value of the SDP relaxation is exactly 9/4. (Use the primal SDP to give a lower bound and the dual SDP to give an upper bound.)' (online the file is presumably https://www.wisdom.weizmann.ac.il/~feige/Apx2018/handout6.pdf, following the pattern of the verified handout5.pdf, but that URL was not opened online in this session). Item 1 of the same handout is W&amp;S Exercise 6.1, the plausible source of 2026B-P5 (graded PLAUSIBLE/NEAR).</p>"}, {"part": "c", "difficulty": "medium", "summary": "Find a feasible dual solution and use SDP weak duality to show that the triangle's SDP value is at most 9/4, hence exactly 9/4, while its max cut is 2.", "q": "<p>(8 points) Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> as in part (a). Give a feasible solution <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> of (D), and use the weak duality theorem to prove that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>9</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>. Conclude that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>9</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, whereas <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</p>", "basis": "<p><b>Evidence.</b> Feige's item states the method outright: '(Use the primal SDP to give a lower bound and the dual SDP to give an upper bound.)'. The upper-bound half is the natural 8-point part. You exhibit a dual certificate, check that it is PSD, and apply the weak duality that students proved in 2026B-P5(c). Volk lifts textbook items nearly verbatim and keeps their parameters. That is why the instance here is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> and the number is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>9</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>. If he generalizes instead, expect the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></math> bound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. For the 5-cycle this bound gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>5</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x02248;</mo><mn>4.52</mn></mrow></math>, which equals its SDP value. All numbers here were checked numerically.</p>\n<p><b>Against.</b> This would be a second SDP problem in the same year, and SDP duality appears on no slide; it is known only from 2026B-P5 itself. The LP-rounding family is more likely at P5 (about 0.28 by this item's own estimate; the Moed C analysis rates LP-based approximation at about 65% as a family anywhere on the paper). So this is one of the most likely SDP instances, not a likely event: about 0.045, again our own rough estimate. No evidence ranks it against single LP-rounding instances, and one of those (a continuation of 2026B-P4 prize-collecting vertex cover, or set cover by dual or randomized rounding) may well be more likely.</p>", "sol": "<p>The adjacency matrix of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>J</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>J</mi></mrow></math> is the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> all-ones matrix, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>J</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mrow></math>. Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mi>I</mi></mrow></math> and</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>J</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mi>J</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x00028;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>This matrix is PSD. For every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>&#x0211D;</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>which is the first definition of PSD from class (equivalently, its eigenvalues are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, all non-negative). So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is a feasible solution of (D), with value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>By the weak duality theorem, every feasible solution <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> of (P) satisfies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>. By part (a), with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>9</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>Together with part (b), <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>9</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>. Also <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is an optimal solution of (D) and here the two optima coincide.</p>\n<p><b>Max cut.</b> The cut <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> cuts the two edges <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. No cut cuts all three edges: every cut has two of the three vertices on the same side (pigeonhole), and the edge between them is not cut. Hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>9</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The relaxation is not tight, and on this instance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>8</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>9</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02248;</mo><mn>0.889</mn></mrow></math>. This is consistent with the class guarantee <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0.878</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, since that requires <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0.878</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p><b>How to find <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> (not required).</b> Try <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math>. The matrix <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>t</mi><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>J</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>t</mi><mi>I</mi></mrow></math> has eigenvalue <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> on the all-ones vector and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> (twice) on the vectors whose coordinates sum to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. So it is PSD if and only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, and the best choice is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>. The same idea works for every graph: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> is feasible, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></math> has eigenvalues <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. This gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, that is, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and the bound is again <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>9</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>. In the notation of W&amp;S Exercise 6.1 (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>), the certificate is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>J</mi><mi>&#x02AB0;</mi><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>9</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>.</p>", "srcline": "<p>Feige, <em>Approximation Algorithms</em> (Weizmann 2018/19, course page https://www.wisdom.weizmann.ac.il/~feige/Apx2018/), Handout 6, homework item 2, upper-bound half: 'Use... the dual SDP to give an upper bound'. The dual is Volk's (D) from 2026B-P5, with the weak duality of 2026B-P5(c) (2026B official solution); equivalently the W&amp;S Exercise 6.1 dual (ws-book.txt).</p>"}, {"part": "alt", "difficulty": "hard", "summary": "Variant B: write MAX 2SAT as a plus-minus-one quadratic program, relax it to an SDP, and prove that random-hyperplane rounding is a 0.878-approximation.", "q": "<p><b>Variant B: an alternative continuation (6+6+8 = 20 points; it would replace the problem above).</b> In the MAX 2SAT problem the input is a CNF formula <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C6;</mi></mrow></math> over the variables <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> in which every clause has at most two literals. The goal is to find an assignment that maximizes the number of satisfied clauses.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) (6 points) As in the case of the maximum cut problem, show that MAX 2SAT can be expressed as an integer quadratic program in which the only constraints are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and the objective function is quadratic in the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>. (Hint: introduce a variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> which indicates whether the value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> is TRUE.)</li>\n<li>(b) (6 points) Relax the program from (a) to an SDP, and describe how to round a solution of the SDP using a random hyperplane.</li>\n<li>(c) (8 points) Prove that the resulting algorithm is a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.878</mn></mrow></math>-approximation algorithm for MAX 2SAT, in expectation.</li>\n</ul>", "basis": "<p>This is the other neighbour in the same pool. Feige's Handout 6 item 3 is '(Based on Problem 6.2 in [WS11])': max restricted cut, and then MAX 2SAT with ratio <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mi>W</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. W&amp;S Exercise 6.2(a)(b) asks for the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x000B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> quadratic program with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and a.878-approximation. The Goemans-Williamson rounding analysis is on the slides but has never been examined, and it would follow naturally from 2026A-P5 (rounding) and 2026B-P5 (SDP). hw2026 s4-p4 (Max-2-LIN) already trained the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#x003B8;</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mi>&#x003B8;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> trick. A lighter version would simply ask to re-prove the class claim <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;is&#x000A0;cut</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#x003B8;</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>. Estimated probability (this item's own rough estimate): about 0.035.</p>", "sol": "<p><b>(a)</b> Use variables <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> be TRUE if and only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>. For a literal <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x02113;</mi></mrow></math> on the variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>, let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>&#x02113;</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>&#x02113;</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x000AC;</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>, and write <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>&#x02113;</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>&#x02113;</mi></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x02113;</mi></mrow></math> is true if and only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>&#x02113;</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, so</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>&#x02113;</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></div>\n<p>equals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x02113;</mi></mrow></math> is true and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> otherwise. A clause <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>&#x02228;</mo><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math> is false if and only if both literals are false, so, using <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>&#x02228;</mo><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>The program maximizes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mtext>unit&#x000A0;clauses&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>&#x02113;</mi></mrow></msub><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mtext>clauses&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>&#x02228;</mo><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>&#x02228;</mo><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> subject to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. Every term is a constant plus multiples of products <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></math> (note <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>), so the objective is quadratic. For every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, the objective is exactly the number of clauses satisfied by the assignment '<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> is TRUE if and only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>'. Every assignment arises this way (take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>). So the optimum of the program is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> As in class, replace every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> by a unit vector <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>&#x0211D;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math> and every product <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></math> by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mi>&#x027E9;</mi></mrow></math>. For a literal <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x02113;</mi></mrow></math> on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> write <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>&#x02113;</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>&#x02113;</mi></msub><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The SDP is</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mtext>maximize</mtext><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mtext>unit&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>&#x02113;</mi></mrow></msub><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>&#x02113;</mi></msub><mi>&#x027E9;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>&#x02228;</mo><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mi>&#x027E9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mi>&#x027E9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mi>&#x027E9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>subject&#x000A0;to&#x000A0;</mtext><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>It is linear in the entries <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mi>&#x027E9;</mi></mrow></math> of a PSD matrix with ones on the diagonal, so it is an SDP and can be solved (to any desired accuracy) in polynomial time with the ellipsoid method, as in class. It is a relaxation: setting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> for an optimal <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x000B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> solution gives the same value, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>Rounding: pick a random hyperplane through the origin, with normal vector <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow></math>, as in the Goemans-Williamson algorithm. Set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> to TRUE if and only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> fall on the same side of the hyperplane, that is, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>&#x027E9;</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>&#x027E9;</mi></mrow></math> have the same sign.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi></mrow><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>&#x027E9;</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>&#x02113;</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>&#x02113;</mi></msub><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>. With probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> no inner product <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>&#x027E9;</mi></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>&#x02113;</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi></mrow><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>&#x02113;</mi></msub><mi>&#x027E9;</mi></mrow></math>. The rounded assignment is exactly the one that (a) associates with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0005E;</mo></mover></mrow></math>, so the number of satisfied clauses equals the objective of (a) at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0005E;</mo></mover></mrow></math>. We bound each term separately. By the claim from class, two unit vectors at angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B8;</mi></mrow></math> are separated by the random hyperplane with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#x003B8;</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#x003B8;</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mi>&#x003B8;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B8;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>min</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>&#x003B8;</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></msub><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#x003B8;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mi>&#x003B8;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02248;</mo><mn>0.878</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cut-type terms. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B8;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02220;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mtext>&#x000A0;are&#x000A0;separated</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#x003B8;</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mi>&#x027E9;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Agreement terms. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B8;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02220;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mtext>&#x000A0;are&#x000A0;not&#x000A0;separated</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#x003B8;</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>&#x003B8;</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B8;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, the angle between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#x003B8;</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>&#x003B8;</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><msup><mi>&#x003B8;</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mi>&#x003B8;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mi>&#x027E9;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>. This is the same substitution as in the Max-2-LIN homework.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Every clause value is a combination of such terms with positive coefficients: a unit clause is one agreement term, and a 2-clause is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> times each of two agreement terms and one cut-type term. By linearity of expectation, the expected contribution of every clause is at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B1;</mi></mrow></math> times its contribution to the SDP. Summing over the clauses,</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02248;</mo><mn>0.878</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>", "srcline": "<p>Williamson &amp; Shmoys, Exercise 6.2(a)(b) (ws-book.txt; https://www.designofapproxalgs.com/book.pdf), wording kept: 'express the MAX 2SAT problem as a \"integer quadratic program\" in which the only constraints are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>... (Hint: it may help to introduce a variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> which indicates whether the value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> is \"TRUE\")'. It is the same exercise as Feige Handout 6 item 3 (course page https://www.wisdom.weizmann.ac.il/~feige/Apx2018/). The analysis follows the official solution of hw2026 s4-p4 (2026 homework 4 solution).</p>"}]}]}, {"tier": 1, "open": false, "title": "Slot alternates — the next two most likely problems for P2–P5", "blurb": "Chosen so that, together with the mock, they cover the families that carry most of the probability: a Schwartz–Zippel algorithm, an FFT counting problem, a sampling estimator, a spectral lecture-theorem re-run, write-an-ILP, the prize-collecting VC continuation, LP rounding, and LLL.", "items": [{"id": "c26-alt-p2-a", "sid": "ALT · P2-a", "difficulty": "medium", "src": "PIT / Schwartz-Zippel, 18 pts, single part (P4 form: 6+12): re-skin of Volk 2023A-P5 (matrix completion) onto bipartite matching; the hint is Edmonds' theorem, M&amp;R Problem 7.7, which extends the 'Symbolic Biadjacency Matrix' slide", "parts": [{"part": null, "difficulty": "medium", "summary": "Compute the maximum matching size of a bipartite graph by substituting random values into the symbolic biadjacency matrix and taking the rank. The key lemma, Edmonds' theorem, says the symbolic rank equals the maximum matching size; the error is one-sided and at most 1/20.", "q": "<p><b>Problem (18 points)</b></p>\n<p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> be a bipartite graph such that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. A <em>matching</em> in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> is a set of vertex-disjoint edges. Denote by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> the size of a maximum matching in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>Recall the <em>symbolic biadjacency matrix</em> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> that we have seen in class: the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> matrix <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math> whose rows are indexed by the vertices of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> and whose columns are indexed by the vertices of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow></math>, defined by</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>if&#x000A0;</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div>\n<p>where the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> are distinct variables.</p>\n<p>Give an efficient randomized algorithm that, given <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math>, computes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The algorithm should be based on the matrix <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math> (there are also deterministic algorithms for this problem, for example via max-flow, but they are not what is asked here). Prove its correctness, and analyze both the running time and the error probability.</p>\n<p>The error probability should be less than 0.1.</p>\n<p>Hint: first prove that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Here the rank of a matrix is the largest <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow></math> such that the matrix has an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></math> submatrix with a non-zero determinant, and for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math>, non-zero means non-zero as a polynomial in the variables <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>.</p>", "basis": "<p><b>Why this family.</b> A Schwartz-Zippel/PIT randomized algorithm was a written problem in 2023A-P5 (18 pts, matrix completion), 2024A-P6 (16, an invertible matrix in a span), 2025A-P5 (16, determinantal equivalence) and 2025C-P4 (14, branching programs). In 2026 it appeared only as the 7-point T/F 2026A-1b, and 2026B had no PIT item at all. By points, PIT is the third-heaviest topic in Volk's exams: 90 pts in 8 of 10 sittings, after Chernoff constructions (109) and ILP formulation (94) (the coverage analysis). Each Moed C so far turned a class topic that its year's A and B had only as T/F (not as a written problem) into a written problem. Lattices were T/F in 2024A-1c and 2024B-1c and became the written 2024C-P5 (LLL). Random walks were T/F in 2025A-1d and 2025B-1d and became the written 2025C-P3 (lazy walk) (the Moed C analysis). Of the topics that 2026 tested only as T/F (PIT, isolation, trace/Newton, lattices, ellipsoid, BFS), PIT has by far the most points in earlier exams (90, against 34 for isolation). It is also Volk's research area (algebraic complexity; PhD with Shpilka).</p>\n<p><b>Why this item.</b> The core is on the course slides. The Randomized deck's \"Symbolic Biadjacency Matrix\" slide proves \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> (as a polynomial) iff <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> has a perfect matching... we get a monomial that can't be cancelled out by any other monomial\". The hint extends that claim from perfect to maximum matchings, which is Motwani-Raghavan Problem 7.7 (due to J. Edmonds) in the course textbook. In 2026 Volk lifted textbook exercises nearly verbatim (W&amp;S Exercises 5.3 and 5.6 became 2026A-P5, confirmed; W&amp;S Exercise 6.1 is the likely source of 2026B-P5). He has already put the matching determinant into a Moed C: 2024C-1d asks whether the parity of the number of perfect matchings is efficiently decidable (via <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo></mrow></math> mod 2). Both research mines rate the item high: L4/F5 in the deck mine and L3/F5 in the M&amp;R mine. No exam or homework has used it.</p>\n<p><b>Template.</b> The algorithm re-skins Volk's own 2023A-P5 (Matrix Completion, 18 points, single part: \"compute what is the maximal rank of a matrix that can be obtained from A by replacing the stars\", with the hint \"think about the relation between determinants and rank\") onto a graph. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math> is a completion instance with an independent star on every edge and zeros elsewhere, so the problem says that its maximal completion rank is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The official 2023A solution is exactly Step 3 here. It takes a non-singular <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> submatrix, notes that its determinant is a non-zero polynomial, applies Schwartz-Zippel to that one minor, and observes that the rank can never exceed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>. The sentence \"The error probability should be less than 0.1.\" is copied from 2025A-P5 and 2025C-P4. Every Volk PIT solution substitutes values from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> is the degree bound: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>10</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> in 2023A and 2024A and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>100</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> in 2025A (degree at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>), and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>100</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> in 2025C (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> matrices, degree at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>). Moed C re-skins earlier templates for about 2/3 of its points (2024C, 2025C), so a re-skin of a template skipped in the same year fits.</p>\n<p><b>Slot and format.</b> Written in the P2 form: one part, 18 points, the weight of 2023A-P5 and 2026B-P2. In both 2026 papers P2 was a single-part algebraic algorithm (16 in 2026A, 18 in 2026B), and the format scorecard gives \"P2 and P3 single-part\" 0.75. If the problem comes at P4 instead, expect the 2026B-P4 split 6+12 = 18: (a) prove <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> (M&amp;R 7.7, Steps 1-2 of the solution); (b) using (a), the algorithm (Step 3). P4 in 2026 was 6+6+8 = 20 or 6+12 = 18.</p>\n<p><b>Counter-evidence.</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The research analyses disagree on the family's odds. The format scorecard gives 0.25, noting that his PIT exam problems moved into hw2026; the Moed C report gives 0.55.</li>\n<li>The T/F-to-written promotion pattern (n=2: lattices 2024, spectral 2025) needs a topic in the T/F of both A and B. PIT was a T/F only in 2026A, so the pattern supports it weakly at best.</li>\n<li>No Moed C problem has been traced to a fresh textbook exercise (the Moed C analysis and the research findings). Both C papers took textbook content from a section or a theorem (W&amp;S §1.4 in 2025C-P2, Galbraith Thm 17.2.12 in 2024C-P5), never from an exercise. M&amp;R 7.7 is an unused exercise, so this item needs the 2026 change (2026A and 2026B each used a fresh exercise) to carry over to C. The Moed C report puts at least one new textbook exercise in 2026C at about 0.50. The algorithm itself re-skins his own 2023A-P5, which is how both C papers were built, so this weighs on the hint more than on the problem.</li>\n<li>The July mock exam's P4 was essentially this algorithm, as a decision version: \"decides whether H contains a matching of size at least k, with error probability at most 1/10\". It gave <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo></mrow></math> max-matching size as a hint, wrongly described as \"seen in class / homework\", and never asked for its proof. That prediction appeared in neither 2026A nor 2026B.</li>\n<li>Homework is neutral: hw2026 s1 drilled PIT, but it also drilled topics that did not come back.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Probability.</b> PIT/Schwartz-Zippel as a written problem anywhere in 2026C: about 0.33 (P4 0.13, P2 0.10, P5 0.06, P3 0.04). Given a PIT problem, P4 is slightly more likely than P2, because the last one, 2025C-P4, sat at P4. This file uses the P2 form because it fills the ALT·P2 slot. This specific problem (Edmonds rank plus random substitution, in either form): about 0.035.</p>\n<p><b>Same-template variants to rehearse</b> (symbolic matrix, then a determinant or product, then random substitution, error below 0.1):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Similarity of matrices</b> (M&amp;R Problem 7.5, due to J. Naor; the other textbook lift for this slot). <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> are similar iff <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>B</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow></math> for some invertible <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math>. Solve this homogeneous linear system in the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> entries of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math> to get a basis <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></math> of its solutions. Then test whether their span contains an invertible matrix: pick <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>20</mn><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and check whether <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. The error is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>20</mn><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow></math>. This is the 2024A-P6 = hw2026 s1-p4 claim, which may be cited. Trap: equal traces or equal spectra do not imply similarity. Example: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x00028;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the zero matrix.</li>\n<li><b><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>-red perfect matching</b> (recitation 4 exercise; also Problem 3 of Problem Set 2 in the 2025-solutions homework folder, and Zwick's Adv-Alg 2016 PS1 Ex 1.6). Multiply the variable of every red edge by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, so that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></mrow></math>. Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math> collects the perfect matchings with exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow></math> red edges, and nothing cancels, as in Step 1. Substitute random values for the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>'s, evaluate at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and interpolate. For exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> red edges, test <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></math>. For at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>, test <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>: different <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math> share no monomial, so the sum is non-zero iff some <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> is. Each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math> has degree <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>20</mn><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> gives error at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Freivalds</b> (Algebraic deck \"Verifying Matrix Multiplication\"; never examined). To check <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, compare <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow></math> for a random <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>100</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math>. A non-zero row <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></math> gives a non-zero linear polynomial <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>r</mi><mi>&#x027E9;</mi></mrow></math>, which vanishes with probability at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow></math>. The slide version uses <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math>, with error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Common basis (hw2026 s1-p5, via Cauchy-Binet), equality of formulas, and the 2025C-P4 branching programs.</li>\n</ul>", "sol": "<p><b>Notation.</b> For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>, let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> be the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> submatrix of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math> with rows <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>I</mi></mrow></math> and columns <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>J</mi></mrow></math>. Its determinant is a polynomial in the variables <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mo>&#x0220F;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></math></div>\n<p>where the sum is over all bijections <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p><b>Step 1 (key claim).</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> as a polynomial if and only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> has a matching of size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> whose left endpoints are exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>I</mi></mrow></math> and whose right endpoints are exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>J</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>Proof. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> is exactly the symbolic biadjacency matrix of the induced bipartite graph <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, whose sides are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>I</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>J</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. So this is the claim we have seen in class, \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> (as a polynomial) iff <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> has a perfect matching\", applied to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>. For completeness, here is the argument. The term of a bijection <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C3;</mi></mrow></math> is non-zero iff <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></math>. That happens iff <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>&#x003C3;</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>:</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is a set of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> edges of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math>. These edges are vertex-disjoint because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C3;</mi></mrow></math> is a bijection, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>&#x003C3;</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a matching of size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> that covers exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></math>. In that case the term equals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x000B1;</mi><msub><mo>&#x0220F;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. This monomial determines <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C3;</mi></mrow></math>, since the variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> appears in it iff <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>. Therefore different bijections give different monomials, each with coefficient <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x000B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, and no monomial can be cancelled by another. So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> is a sum of distinct monomials, one for each perfect matching of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>. It is non-zero iff there is at least one such matching. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x025FB;</mi></mrow></math></p>\n<p><b>Step 2: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></b> (the hint).</p>\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>: let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> be a maximum matching and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></math> be the sets of left and right endpoints of the edges of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. By Step 1, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> contains the monomial <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x0220F;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> with coefficient <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x000B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and is therefore non-zero. So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math> has a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> submatrix with non-zero determinant, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>: let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. There are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. By Step 1, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> has a matching of size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>Together, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and both sides are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p><b>Step 3: the algorithm.</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>20</mn><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>. For every edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math>, pick <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> uniformly at random, independently of all other edges. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> be the numeric matrix obtained from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math> by substituting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. That is, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> otherwise. Compute <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> by Gaussian elimination over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0211A;</mi></mrow></math> and output it.</p>\n<p><b>Running time.</b> Building <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> takes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> time, and Gaussian elimination uses <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> arithmetic operations. The entries of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> are integers in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>20</mn><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>. With fraction-free elimination, every intermediate number is, up to sign, a minor of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>. Such a minor has absolute value at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x00021;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>20</mn><mi>n</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math>, so it has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> bits, and the algorithm runs in polynomial time.</p>\n<p><b>Substitution commutes with determinants.</b> For all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></math>, let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>. Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, because the determinant is a polynomial in the matrix entries. Substituting and then taking the determinant gives the same value as taking the determinant and then substituting.</p>\n<p><b>The output is never larger than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. By Step 2, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>, so every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> minor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math> is the zero polynomial. Hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for every choice of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B1;</mi></mrow></math>. So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> has no non-zero <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> minor, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> always. The error is one-sided: the algorithm can only underestimate.</p>\n<p><b>The output equals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>19</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow></math>.</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> be a maximum matching, and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></math> be its sets of left and right endpoints, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. By Step 1, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi></mrow></math> is a non-zero polynomial with integer coefficients. Every monomial of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi></mrow></math> is a product of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> variables, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. By the Schwartz-Zippel lemma, applied with the finite set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> of size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>20</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003B1;</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mi>P</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>20</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>20</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>The number of variables (at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>) plays no role in this bound. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> is a non-singular <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> submatrix of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Combined with the previous paragraph, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>Hence the algorithm outputs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>19</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow></math>, and its error probability is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p><em>What is graded:</em> Step 1 carries the proof. For full credit it needs either the no-cancellation argument (distinct bijections give distinct monomials, each with coefficient <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x000B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>) or an explicit citation of the class claim \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> iff <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> has a perfect matching\" applied to the induced subgraph <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, whose symbolic biadjacency matrix is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>. Either one earns full credit, since the exam allows any claim presented in class. Citing the class claim for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> itself is not enough: when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> it only says <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, which gives no information about the smaller minors. A proof that has neither leaves <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> unjustified, and the 0/1 biadjacency matrix shows why this matters. For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> that matrix is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x00028;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, which has rank <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, but <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. This is the counterexample from class, where the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002B;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo></mrow></math> terms cancel. The remaining points are for the one-sided bound (the output never exceeds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>) and for Schwartz-Zippel applied to one <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> minor, with degree at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p><b>Remarks.</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, the set used in 2023A-P5 and 2024A-P6, the bound is exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>, which is not less than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.1</mn></mrow></math>. Any <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>11</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> works.</li>\n<li>The error is one-sided, so running the algorithm <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> times independently and outputting the maximum answer reduces the error to at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>20</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Computing the rank of the 0/1 biadjacency matrix instead of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> is wrong. For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> it gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, but <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Verification, not part of the exam answer. (i) The algorithm was run 50 times on each of 400 random bipartite graphs with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></math> (check_edmonds.py). Its output never exceeded <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and the worst per-graph error rate was <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.02</mn></mrow></math>, below the bound of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.05</mn></mrow></math>. (ii) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> was computed over the field of rational functions by fraction-free Gaussian elimination with exact polynomial arithmetic, without using matchings, permutations or minors (check_edmonds_symrank.py). It equalled <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, computed by augmenting paths, on all 300 random graphs with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>, 129 of which have no perfect matching.</li>\n</ul>", "srcline": "<p>Template: Volk 2023A-P5 (Matrix Completion, 18 pts, single part). Its official solution substitutes values from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and applies Schwartz-Zippel to one <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> minor. The error-bound sentence is verbatim from 2025A-P5 and 2025C-P4. Hint: Motwani-Raghavan, <em>Randomized Algorithms</em>, Problem 7.7 (due to J. Edmonds), p.190: \"Let G(U,V,E) be a bipartite graph, and let A be the corresponding matrix of indeterminates as defined in Section 7.3. Show that the size of a maximum matching in G is exactly equal to the rank of the matrix A.\" Course anchor: Randomized deck, slides \"Matchings and Determinants\" and \"Symbolic Biadjacency Matrix\" (corpus), \"Claim: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> (as a polynomial) iff <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> has a perfect matching\"; the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> all-ones counterexample is on the preceding slide. The algorithm follows the lecture's \"Polynomial Identity Testing\" slide (substitute from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>; the error is one-sided) and the slide \"Testing zeroness of Determinant\" (degree at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>; evaluate the determinant by Gaussian elimination).</p>"}]}, {"id": "c26-alt-p2-b", "sid": "ALT · P2-b", "difficulty": "medium", "src": "Algebraic / FFT, 16 pts: Erickson FFT notes Ex 3(c) (bounded 3SUM), the exercise right after Ex 2(c) = 2026A-P2, in Volk's counting form", "parts": [{"part": null, "difficulty": "medium", "summary": "Count the sets of three distinct elements of S within -N..N that sum to zero, in time O(N log N), using the FFT and removing the degenerate triples.", "q": "<p><b>Problem (16 points)</b></p>\n<p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x02115;</mi></mrow></math> and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>. Give an algorithm that, given <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>, computes the number of sets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> of three <em>distinct</em> elements such that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, in time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p><em>Easier form he may ask instead (the source's decision form, Erickson Ex 3(c), with its parameters kept): let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math> be a set of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> integers, each between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>10000</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>10000</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. Give an algorithm that, given <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math>, determines whether <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math> contains three elements whose sum is zero, in time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</em></p>", "basis": "<p><b>Source adjacency.</b> 2026A-P2 (16 points: count the 3-term arithmetic progressions of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> in time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>) is a verified NEAR copy of Erickson's FFT notes, Exercise 2(c). Volk changed the bit string <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to a set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> and kept the running time. This item is part (c) of Exercise 3, the next exercise. Exercise 3 starts on the same page as Ex 2 (p.17), and part (c) is on p.18 (local copy). It uses the same trick: take the generating polynomial of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>, raise it to a power and read off the relevant coefficient(s). 2026A-P2 sums the coefficients of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>b</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math> in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> over all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>; this item reads the single coefficient of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math> in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></math>. Only the linear equation changes, from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. The other unused neighbours are Ex 1(b) (the size of the Minkowski sum) and Ex 4 (Hamming distance to every window, with the hint 'Consider 0s and 1s separately').</p>\n<p><b>Pattern.</b> In 2026 the exact hits came from unused neighbours of verified sources. Erickson Ex 2(c) and Williamson-Shmoys Ex 5.3 + 5.6 together gave 36 points of 2026A. FFT was a written problem in 5 of Volk's 10 exams: 2023A-P6 (sumset counts), 2024A-P5 (convolution), 2024B-P2 (Walsh-Hadamard), 2025B-P4 (exact division) and 2026A-P2 (3-term APs). A Moed C does re-skin the same year's Moed A: 2025C-P4 (equality of branching programs via Schwartz-Zippel) re-skins 2025A-P5 (determinantal equivalence via Schwartz-Zippel).</p>\n<p><b>Discount.</b> No Moed C has had an FFT written problem (0 of 2; 2025C had FFT only as a roots-of-unity T/F). This problem would also be a near-twin of 2026A-P2, whose solution the students have already seen.</p>\n<p><b>Probability.</b> Erickson 3(c) in this counting form: about 0.03. Erickson Ex 4 in its place: about 0.025. An FFT written problem anywhere on 2026C: about 0.20 (in slot P2: about 0.13, the figure used in mock-p2). This sits between the two research analyses. The Moed C analysis gives FFT about 30%, and that figure already counts the 0 of 2 Moed C. The coverage analysis ranks an FFT or matrix-multiplication reduction 'Lower' because 2026A-P2 already used FFT. I discount the 30% for that point: a second FFT counting problem in the same year would be a near-twin of a problem whose solution the students have seen. It is worth studying even at this low probability. The step that decides the grade here is removing the degenerate triples and converting ordered triples to sets, which is exactly where the official 2026A-P2 solution slips.</p>", "sol": "<p><b>Solution.</b> Define the polynomial\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nWe shift every exponent by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> so that all exponents lie in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>. Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>, and the coefficient list of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> can be written down in time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p><b>Step 1: ordered triples, repetitions allowed.</b> Expanding the product,\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nso the coefficient of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math> in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></math> is\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mi>:</mi><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nthe number of <em>ordered</em> triples with sum zero, where repetitions are allowed. We compute <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> (degree at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>) and then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> (degree at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>6</mn><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>). As we have seen in class, each of these products can be computed in time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> using the FFT-based polynomial multiplication algorithm.</p>\n<p><b>Step 2: removing triples with a repeated element.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> also counts triples such as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, which do not give a set of three distinct elements. Let\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi><mi>:</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nand let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> otherwise.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The triples counted in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> are exactly the triples <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>, so there are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> of them. By symmetry there are also <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> triples with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>If two of the equalities <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> hold, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, so the triple is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Therefore every pairwise intersection, and the intersection of all three, contains exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> triples.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>By inclusion-exclusion, the number of triples counted in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> with at least two equal coordinates is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>. So the number of ordered triples of <em>distinct</em> elements of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> with sum <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> is\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> can be computed in time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Keep a Boolean array <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> if and only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>, and for each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> check whether <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Equivalently, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> is the coefficient of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math> in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, which takes one more FFT multiplication.</p>\n<p><b>Step 3: from ordered triples to sets.</b> Every set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> of three distinct elements with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> gives exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x00021;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math> ordered triples of distinct elements with sum <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Conversely, every such ordered triple comes from exactly one such set. The algorithm therefore outputs\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p><b>Running time.</b> Building <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> takes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Two multiplications of polynomials of degree <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Reading one coefficient and computing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The total is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p><b>Decision version.</b> Erickson's 'three elements whose sum is zero' does not say whether an element may be used more than once, and 3SUM conventions differ. State which reading you use. If the three elements must be distinct, answer 'yes' if and only if the count above is positive, that is, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. If repetitions are allowed (so that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> count), answer 'yes' if and only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Erickson's original (the input set lies in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>10000</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>10000</mn><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>) is the case <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>10000</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>: building <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> takes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> time, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p><b>Sanity check.</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>. Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>22</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> (from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>) and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The formula gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>22</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, matching the three sets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>. (Checked by brute force against random sets as well.)</p>\n<p><b>Common mistakes.</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Returning <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>. For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> we get <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> (the orderings of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>), although no valid set exists. For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> we get <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, which is not even divisible by 6.</li>\n<li>Subtracting only the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> triple, or subtracting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> without adding back <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Not saying whether you count ordered or unordered triples. The official 2026A-P2 solution sums, over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>, the coefficient of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>b</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math> in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> minus <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. That coefficient counts <em>ordered</em> pairs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, so every progression is counted twice. The correct count is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is that coefficient.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Variants to prepare (same technique).</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Erickson Ex 4, Hamming distance to every window.</b> Given <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math> (the pattern reversed) and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. A pair of terms contributes to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math> exactly when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>. So the coefficient of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math> in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> is the number of positions <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Define <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> the same way for the 0s. Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the coefficient of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math> in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Return <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>min</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. This takes two FFT multiplications, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. On Erickson's example <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1100101</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1111111010101000000</mn></mrow></math> it returns <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Erickson Ex 1(b), size of the Minkowski sum.</b> With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> is the number of nonzero coefficients of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></mrow></math>, computed in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>M</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Solutions of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>.</b> With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, the number of ordered solutions <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></math>, repetitions allowed, is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> (the coefficient of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math> in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>). For distinct <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>, subtract the degenerate solutions: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi><mi>:</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>. Halve this to count unordered pairs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Zwick, Algorithms in Action, cross-correlation in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></b> (also Erickson Ex 0). Cut the long vector into <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x02308;</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>&#x02309;</mi></mrow></math> blocks of length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. Correlate each block with the short vector by one FFT multiplication of degree <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and add the overlapping outputs. The total is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ul>", "srcline": "<p>Jeff Erickson, <em>Algorithms</em> lecture notes, Appendix A 'Fast Fourier Transforms', Exercise 3, pp.17-18 (jeffe.cs.illinois.edu/teaching/algorithms/notes/A-fft.pdf): '(a) Describe an algorithm that determines whether a given set of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> integers contains two elements whose sum is zero, in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> time. (b)... three elements whose sum is zero, in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> time. (c) Now suppose the input set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math> contains only integers between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>10000</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>10000</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. Describe an algorithm that determines whether <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math> contains three elements whose sum is zero, in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> time.' Here it is put in Volk's counting form, the way he re-skinned the preceding Exercise 2(c) as 2026A-P2 (2026A official solution, Problem 2).</p>"}]}, {"id": "c26-alt-p3-a", "sid": "ALT · P3-a", "difficulty": "easy", "src": "Randomized, Chernoff sampling estimator, 16 pts: re-skin of Volk 2025B-P5 (fraction of legal 3-colourings within 1/n), itself a re-skin of 2024A-P2 (CNF density within 1/10)", "parts": [{"part": null, "difficulty": "easy", "summary": "Estimate, within additive error 1/n and with probability at least 1 - 2^(-n), the fraction of vertex subsets of a graph that are independent sets, using uniform sampling and the Chernoff bound.", "q": "<p><b>(16 points)</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> be an undirected graph with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> vertices. A set of vertices <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></math> is called <em>independent</em> if there is no edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math> such that both <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>. (In particular, the empty set is independent.)</p>\n<p>The <em>independence density</em> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math>, denoted <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, is the fraction of independent sets among all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math> subsets of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow></math>, that is,</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mi>V</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>S</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;is&#x000A0;an&#x000A0;independent&#x000A0;set</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>Give a randomized algorithm that runs in polynomial time that, given a graph <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math>, with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, outputs a number <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> such that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>.</p>", "basis": "<p><b>Why this is predicted.</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>A proven re-skin chain.</b> Volk has set this template twice. 2024A-P2 (18 pts) asks for the density <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C6;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> of a CNF formula within <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. 2025B-P5 (16 pts) keeps the structure (define a fraction, then ask for one estimate) and the core clause \"with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>,... outputs a number <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> such that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo></mrow></math>\". It rephrases the command from 2024A's \"Prove that there's a randomized polynomial time algorithm with the following property: given a CNF formula <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C6;</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> variables,...\" to \"Give a randomized algorithm that runs in polynomial time that given a graph <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math>,...\", swaps the object to the \"versatility\" <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, the fraction of legal 3-colourings, and tightens the precision from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>. The official 2025B solution samples <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> random colourings, applies Chernoff and takes \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>\" (meaning <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>). This item keeps the 2025B wording and parameters and swaps the object once more.</li>\n<li><b>The flavour 2026 did not use.</b> A Chernoff/union-bound written problem appeared in 9 of the 10 Volk exams from 2023A to 2026B, so one is expected in 2026C with probability about 0.85-0.9. Both 2026 written problems were constructions: 2026A-P3 (random vectors shatter every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>-set) and 2026B-P3 (a colouring with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.4</mn><mi>deg</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> neighbours of each colour). The T/F item 2026A-1d was a construction too. The coverage analysis names the sampling estimator as the natural variation, because it repeats neither of them.</li>\n<li><b>Why it is only an alternate.</b> Constructions make up 7 of the 9 Chernoff/union-bound written problems (the other 2 are the estimators 2024A-P2 and 2025B-P5): 2023B-P6, 2024B-P6, 2024C-P3, 2025A-P4, 2025C-P5, 2026A-P3 and 2026B-P3. That includes both earlier Moed C problems. Each C left out the estimator that its own year had used: 2024C after 2024A-P2, and 2025C after 2025B-P5. The Moed C and flow analyses predict that P3 will re-skin a construction.</li>\n<li><b>Probability (own estimate; no research report gives a figure for the estimator alone).</b> The Chernoff/union-bound slot is filled with probability about 0.9 (the Moed C analysis; the format analysis says 0.85). Estimators are 2 of the 9 written problems in that slot, so the estimator template on some object has probability about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.9</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>9</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02248;</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow></math> anywhere in 2026C. The format scorecard (the format analysis) puts the randomized unit at P3 with probability 0.55, so as P3 it is about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.55</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>9</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02248;</mo><mn>0.12</mn></mrow></math>. The two opposite signals above (the coverage analysis favours the estimator, the Moed C history disfavours it) are taken to cancel, so the base rate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>9</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> is kept. Independent sets are one of at least six natural objects, so this exact item has probability about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>0.2</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02248;</mo><mn>0.03</mn></mrow></math>. The solution below works unchanged for the other objects he could choose: dominating sets (the object of his 2023A-P2 ILP), satisfying assignments of a DNF formula, legal 4-colourings, acyclic orientations, or edge subsets whose spanning subgraph stays connected. (Vertex covers give exactly the same number as independent sets, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> is independent if and only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>V</mi><mi>&#x029F5;</mi><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> is a vertex cover.) Learn the template, not the object.</li>\n</ul>", "sol": "<p><b>Algorithm.</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></math>. On input <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, pick a random subset <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></math> by putting every vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></math> in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, independently of all other choices.</li>\n<li>For every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math>, check whether <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> is independent. To do this, go over all edges <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math> and check whether <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Output the fraction of samples that are independent sets, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>:</mi><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mtext>&#x000A0;is&#x000A0;independent</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Correctness.</b> Fix a set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></math>. Every vertex agrees with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math> (it is in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> if and only if it is in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math>) with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, independently, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. Hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> is uniformly distributed over the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math> subsets of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow></math>, and</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mtext>&#x000A0;is&#x000A0;independent</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mi>V</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>S</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;is&#x000A0;independent</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> be an indicator random variable for the event that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> is an independent set, and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The sets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></math> are chosen with independent coins, so the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>'s are mutually independent. Each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> equals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> otherwise. Therefore <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and the output of the algorithm is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>As we have seen in class, the Chernoff bound says the following. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math> is a sum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> i.i.d. random variables, each equal to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> otherwise, then</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>p</mi><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>We apply it with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>Finally, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. This inequality is equivalent to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, which holds because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>3.6</mn></mrow></math>. Hence, with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, as required.</p>\n<p><b>Running time.</b> Sampling one set takes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> time (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> random bits), and checking whether it is independent takes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> time. Over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></math> samples the total is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>5</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The counter and the final division use numbers of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> bits, so the algorithm runs in polynomial time.</p>\n<p><b>Grading note.</b> An answer at the level of the official 2025B solution earns full marks. Such an answer takes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> independent samples, that is, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></math> for a large enough constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> (with the class bound any <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>ln</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> works; the solution above uses <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), explains why <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, bounds the failure probability with Chernoff by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, and notes that each check is polynomial. The official 2025B solution writes \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>\", but what is needed is a lower bound: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> is also <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and fails (see the first remark below). The explicit constants above make the same argument rigorous.</p>\n<p><b>Remarks (common mistakes).</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Too few samples.</b> With precision <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> and failure probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, the Chernoff bound needs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, that is, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. In 2024A the precision was the constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> was enough there. Here it is not.</li>\n<li><b>Chebyshev is not enough.</b> Chebyshev only gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>n</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>. Reaching <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math> this way needs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math> samples, which is exponential time. The exponentially small tail of Chernoff, which uses the full independence of the samples, is what keeps <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> polynomial.</li>\n<li><b>Additive, not multiplicative.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> can be exponentially small. For example, the only independent sets of the complete graph <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02205;</mo></mrow></math> and the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> singletons, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>. For large <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, the algorithm most likely outputs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, and that answer is correct, because only additive error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> is required. A guarantee of the form <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>&#x000B1;</mi><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> cannot be obtained by uniform sampling: seeing even one independent set takes about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> samples. (Beyond the course: no polynomial-time algorithm can approximate the number of independent sets of a general graph within a factor of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> unless <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow></mrow></math>. Replacing every vertex with a large cloud of copies turns such an algorithm into one that finds the size of a maximum independent set.)</li>\n</ul>", "srcline": "<p>Re-skin of Volk's own <b>2025B Problem 5</b> (16 pts): the versatility <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, the fraction of legal 3-colourings, within <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. The official solution samples <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> colourings and applies Chernoff. 2025B-P5 is itself a re-skin of <b>2024A Problem 2</b> (18 pts): the density <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C6;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> of a CNF formula within <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. The class tool is the Chernoff bound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>p</mi><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math> from the Randomized lecture deck (slide 61, \"Chernoff Bound\"). Local text: 2024A paper; 2025B paper; 2025B official solution; Randomized lecture deck (slide 61; the bound is on).</p>"}]}, {"id": "c26-alt-p3-b", "sid": "ALT · P3-b", "difficulty": "medium", "src": "Random walks / spectral gap, lecture-theorem re-run, 16 pts (10+6): the class lazy-walk bound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow><msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msubsup><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msubsup></mrow></math> with a gap <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math>, plus Recitation 4 Claim 2 (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow></math> gives diameter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>); the lazy, one-sided analogue of the 2023B-P1(d) T/F (which is about the non-lazy walk under a two-sided eigenvalue bound), 2025C-P3 parameter-change recipe", "stem": "<p><b>Problem 3 (10+6=16 points)</b></p><p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> be an undirected, connected, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>-regular graph on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> vertices, and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> be its normalized adjacency matrix, that is, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> otherwise. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>, and denote the eigenvalues of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</p><p>Consider the following <em>lazy random walk</em> on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math>: starting from a vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math>, at each step the walk either stays in place with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, or, with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, moves to a random neighbor of the current vertex (chosen uniformly and independently of all previous choices).</p><p>Suppose that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math> for some <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x02308;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>ln</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x02309;</mo></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p>You may use any property we proved in class regarding the matrices <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>.</p>", "parts": [{"part": "a", "difficulty": "medium", "summary": "Re-run the class lazy-walk eigenvalue argument with a spectral gap eps to show that the walk from s is at t after ceil(2 ln n / eps) steps with probability at least 1/(2n).", "q": "<p><b>(a) (10 points)</b> Prove that for every two vertices <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></math>, the probability that the lazy random walk that starts at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math> is at the vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> after exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> steps is at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>.</p>", "basis": "<p><b>Probability.</b> This exact problem: about 0.025. A random-walk / spectral written problem anywhere on 2026C: about 0.25, the figure in the slot table of the Moed C analysis (the same row that gives the isolation lemma about 0.25 and Karger about 0.10); randomized 2SAT, never examined, adds little to it. In the P3 slot, where 2025C put its random-walk problem: about 0.11. Within the family this exact version gets only about a tenth, because the lazy-walk proof was already the 2025C problem and the family has several other forms, for example the 2025C walk with another stay probability, 2SAT, the expander mixing lemma, the multiplicity of the eigenvalue 1, bipartiteness and the eigenvalue <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. A re-run of some lecture theorem with a changed parameter, on any topic: about 0.80.</p><ul><li><b>The topic is due.</b> Random walks and spectral facts appeared in 2023B-P1(d), 2025A-P1(d), 2025B-P1(d) (all T/F) and in 2025C-P3 (16-point written problem). They are completely absent from 2026A and 2026B, with no T/F item and no problem. Yet hw2026 set 2 spent Question 1 on them (the spectral gap of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>) and Question 2 on the expander mixing lemma.</li><li><b>Moed C pattern.</b> Each Moed C added 1-2 class topics that its own A/B skipped; 2025C added exactly a random-walk written problem. Every Moed C also re-ran a class proof with one parameter changed: 2024C-P5 (the LLL bound), 2024C-P6 (a strong-duality case via Farkas) and 2025C-P3 (the class lazy-walk theorem with stay probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> changed to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>). The official solution of 2025C-P3 was three lines of eigenvalue bookkeeping followed by &quot;The rest of the proof is identical to what we need in class [sic]&quot;.</li><li><b>Source adjacency.</b> The proof is the Randomized deck's &quot;Calculating the error&quot; and &quot;Bounding the error&quot; slides, which state &quot;We showed: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msubsup><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msubsup></mrow></math>&quot;. Here the class's polynomially small gap is replaced by a gap <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math>. Part (a) is the lazy, one-sided analogue of the 2023B-P1(d) T/F (&quot;all the eigenvalues in the normalized adjacency matrix of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math>, except the largest one, are at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> in absolute value... the probability that a random walk that starts from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math> will reach <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> steps is at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>&quot;, official answer True, &quot;using the same analysis we saw in class&quot). The 2023B item is about the non-lazy walk and assumes that every eigenvalue of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> except the largest is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> in absolute value. Here only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is bounded, from above. That suffices only because the walk is lazy: under this one-sided hypothesis the 2023B statement for the non-lazy walk is false for bipartite graphs. Turning a spectral T/F into a written problem is the same promotion that produced 2025C-P3 from 2025A-P1(d) and 2025B-P1(d).</li><li><b>Why this version and not 2SAT.</b> Within the random-walk family, this version changes one parameter of a proof the cohort saw twice, in the lecture and in recitation 4. That is the 2025C-P3 recipe. The class 2SAT slides give no hitting-time proof (&quot;After at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> steps we'll reach the vertex 0 with high probability&quot). The explicit hitting-time bound is Mitzenmacher-Upfal &sect;7.1.1, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>, the expected time to reach <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> from a state in which <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> variables agree, is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>. Re-indexed by the distance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>, as the class slides index the walk, this is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The course never taught this technique.</li><li><b>Against.</b> The lazy-walk proof was the 2025C problem only one year ago. The same skipped-topic slot could also go to Schwartz-Zippel/PIT (research estimates 0.25-0.55), the isolation lemma (about 0.25) or Karger (about 0.10). That is why this exact problem gets only 0.025.</li></ul>", "sol": "<p><b>Setup.</b> For a vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>&#x0211D;</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math> be the vector with 1 in coordinate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and 0 elsewhere, and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math> and 0 otherwise, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>. So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is exactly the probability that one lazy step goes from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> (and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> is symmetric). As we have seen in class, if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></math> is a distribution on the vertices then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi><mi>q</mi></mrow></math> is the distribution obtained by picking a vertex according to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></math> and making one lazy step. By induction on the number of steps, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the distribution of the position of the lazy walk that starts at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math> after exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> steps. We need to show that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>.</p><p><b>Eigenvalues of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>.</b> As we saw in class, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> is real symmetric with all row sums equal to 1. It therefore has real eigenvalues <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and an orthonormal basis of eigenvectors <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow></mrow></math>. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> has the same eigenvectors, with eigenvalues <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Hence for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>,</p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><p>This is where laziness is used. For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> itself, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> is bipartite, and a bound on the second eigenvalue says nothing about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>.</p><p><b>The class bound.</b> Write <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>. By orthonormality <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>&#x027E9;</mi></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><msubsup><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msubsup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. By linearity,</p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><p>Again by orthonormality (every cross term vanishes),</p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x02016;</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x02016;</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><msubsup><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msubsup><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msubsup><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></mrow></msubsup><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><msubsup><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msubsup><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><p>where we used <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x02016;</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x02016;</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msubsup><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msubsup></mrow></math>. This is exactly the claim proved in class, and it may be quoted without re-deriving it.</p><p><b>Choosing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>.</b> Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>x</mi></msup></mrow></math> for every real <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>ln</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> by the choice <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x02308;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>ln</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x02309;</mo></mrow></mrow></math>,</p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>ln</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><p><b>From the norm to the coordinate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>.</b> No single coordinate of a vector exceeds its Euclidean norm in absolute value: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msqrt><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math>. Applying this to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow></mrow></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, and therefore</p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mtext>the&#x000A0;walk&#x000A0;is&#x000A0;at&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>t</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;after&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>k</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;steps</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><p>where the last inequality is equivalent to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, that is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0220E;</mo></mrow></math></p><p><b>Remarks.</b></p><ul><li>The proof gives more: every vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow></math>. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x02308;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>c</mi><mi>ln</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x02309;</mo></mrow></mrow></math> the error is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>.</li><li>If the hypothesis is stated for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> instead, as on the recitation-4 slide (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math>), then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>. The same proof then works with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x02308;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>ln</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x02309;</mo></mrow></mrow></math>.</li><li>Likely deductions, for three gaps: using <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> to bound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> without noting that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> (this fails for the non-lazy walk); jumping from a small 2-norm to a lower bound on one coordinate without the step <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math>; and writing &quot;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msubsup></mrow></math> is small&quot; without checking <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math> for the stated <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>.</li></ul>", "srcline": "<p>Volk, Randomized lecture deck: the &quot;For technical reasons: we'll work with the lazy random walk matrix <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>&quot;, &quot;Calculating the error&quot; and &quot;Bounding the error&quot; slides (Randomized lecture deck: &quot;We showed: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msubsup><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msubsup></mrow></math>&quot). Part (a) is the lazy, one-sided analogue of 2023B-P1(d) (T/F about the non-lazy walk when all eigenvalues of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> but the largest are at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> in absolute value; official answer True: &quot;Using the same analysis we saw in class... for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>&quot;; 2023B paper). The parameter change copies the recipe of 2025C-P3 (2025C paper).</p>"}, {"part": "b", "difficulty": "easy", "summary": "Deduce from the positive probability of being at t after k lazy steps that every two vertices are joined by a path of length at most k, so the diameter is O(log n / eps).", "q": "<p><b>(b) (6 points)</b> Conclude that the diameter of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The diameter is the maximum, over all pairs of vertices <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, of the length of a shortest path between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>. In particular, if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math> is a constant then the diameter of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>", "basis": "<p><b>Probability.</b> This part comes with (a), so it carries the same 0.025. It is Recitation 4's &quot;Claim 2: If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow></math> then the diameter of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>&quot;, which has never been examined. The lecture-flow analysis lists the recitation-4 spectral claims among the unexamined lecture and recitation sources for the missing random-walk slot. The same slide gives the two-cliques counterexample to the converse. That counterexample is a ready-made T/F: &quot;if the diameter of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math> for a constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math>&quot; (False).</p><p><b>Other forms of the same family to practise.</b> A lecture-theorem re-run of some kind on 2026C: about 0.80.</p><ul><li><b>Isolation lemma for the minimum and the maximum</b> (2025C-P1(c) written out; Jukna Ex. 11.4). The weights <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are chosen independently and uniformly from an arbitrary set of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> integers. Prove that with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, both the minimum-weight set of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x02131;</mi></mrow></math> and the maximum-weight set of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x02131;</mi></mrow></math> are unique. For the minimum, use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>min</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x02131;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mo>min</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x02131;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>S</mi><mi>&#x029F5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> as in class. For the maximum, use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x02131;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x02131;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>S</mi><mi>&#x029F5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Suppose two distinct sets attain the maximum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> belongs to exactly one of them. Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> does not depend on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, this has probability at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, and a union bound over the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> bad events finishes the proof. Note that for weights in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, substituting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></math> does <em>not</em> reduce the maximum to the minimum unless all sets in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x02131;</mi></mrow></math> have the same size, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</li><li><b>Randomized 2SAT.</b> The deck's slide asks &quot;Why doesn't this work for 3SAT?&quot;. In an unsatisfied 3-clause, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B2;</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B1;</mi></mrow></math> are only guaranteed to differ on one of the three variables, so a flip decreases the distance with probability only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>. The class argument needs probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> of stepping towards 0, so it gives no polynomial bound. A second variant changes the flip rule.</li><li><b>Karger stopped early.</b> Contract random edges until <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> vertices remain. A fixed minimum cut of size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> survives with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>. When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> vertices remain, every degree is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>, so there are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> edges, and the cut is hit with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>. Therefore <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mo>&#x0220F;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>.</li><li><b>Expander mixing lemma</b> (hw2026 set 2, Q2(c)). For a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>-regular <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> with adjacency eigenvalues <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>max</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><msqrt><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>.</li></ul>", "sol": "<p><b>Solution.</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> be two vertices. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> their distance is 0, so assume <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>. By part (a), <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Expanding the matrix power,</p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mo>&#x022EF;</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><p>This is a sum of non-negative terms. It is positive, so some term is positive: there is a sequence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. In probabilistic language, the event &quot;the lazy walk is at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> after <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> steps&quot; has positive probability, so some run of the walk realises it. Now <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> if and only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math>. So every two consecutive vertices of the sequence are either equal (the walk stayed in place) or adjacent in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math>.</p><p>Delete the repetitions. What remains is a walk in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> that uses at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> edges. A shortest path from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> is no longer than any walk between them, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> were arbitrary,</p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x02308;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>ln</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x02309;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><p>If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math> is a constant, this is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0220E;</mo></mrow></math></p><p><b>Remarks.</b> The run of length exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> may contain stays and repeated vertices, so the argument gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>, not equality. The converse is false (Recitation 4): take two disjoint cliques of size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, pick distinct vertices <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> in the first and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> in the second, add the edges <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, and delete <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, so that the graph is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>-regular. This graph has diameter 3. But the vector <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> that is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> on one clique and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> on the other is orthogonal to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow></mrow></math>, and only the 2 connecting edges have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x02260;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math>, so with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> its Rayleigh quotient gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>8</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. So a small diameter does not give a constant spectral gap. (With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow></math> connecting edges forming a matching, one must delete <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> edges inside each clique, not one.)</p>", "srcline": "<p>Recitation 4 (random walks and isolation lemma), &quot;Spectral Graph Theory&quot;, Claim 2: &quot;If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow></math> then the diameter of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>&quot;. The proof chooses <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>0.9</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, obtains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>, and concludes &quot;diam<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>&quot; (recitation 4). The two-cliques counterexample to the converse is at.</p>"}]}, {"id": "c26-alt-p4-a", "sid": "ALT · P4-a", "difficulty": "medium", "src": "LP: write an ILP + LP integrality, 20 pts (6+6+8); DPV Algorithms Ex 7.29 'Hollywood' (the exercise after DPV 7.28, the plausible source of 2026A-P4), with the threshold r-trick of hw2026 set 3 Q3", "stem": "<p><b>Problem 4 (6+6+8=20 points)</b></p>\n<p>A film producer is seeking actors and investors for his new movie. There are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> available actors, and actor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> charges <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> dollars. For funding, there are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> available investors. Investor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> will provide <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> dollars, but only on the condition that certain actors <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> are included in the cast (all of the actors in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> must be chosen in order to receive funding from investor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>). You may assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The producer's profit is the sum of the payments from investors minus the payments to actors. The goal is to choose a set of actors <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> that maximizes the profit\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nDenote the maximum profit by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>.</p>", "parts": [{"part": "a", "difficulty": "easy", "summary": "Write a 0/1 integer program for the actors and investors profit problem and justify its variables and constraints.", "q": "<p><b>(a) [6 points]</b> Write an integer linear program, in which all the variables take values in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, that corresponds to this problem. Explain your answer briefly: write what are the variables and what is their meaning, and explain why every feasible solution to the IP corresponds to a solution to the original problem and why the optimum of the IP equals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p><em>Practice note: attempt (a) before reading (b), because (b) fixes one particular program.</em></p>", "basis": "<p><b>Pattern.</b> A write-an-IP-from-scratch problem appeared in 5 of the 8 Volk exams from 2023A to 2025C: 2023A-P2 (dominating set), 2024A-P4 (weighted Max-SAT), 2024C-P4 (bin packing, a Moed C, taken from the pre-Volk 2014B-P7a), 2025A-P3 (makespan) and 2025B-P2a (set cover). Neither 2026 paper had one. 2026A only asked students to explain an LP or IP it gave them (the min-cost unit flow LP in P4(a) and the Max DiCut IP in P5(b)). 2026B had just 7 points of LP theory/ILP (the ellipsoid T/F 2026B-1a), the lowest share of any exam in the coverage research's categories, which count LP-relaxation rounding such as 2026B-P4 (18 points) as approximation. The closest precedent is 2024B, which also had little LP theory/ILP (12 points, all T/F; its LP-rounding P5, 16 points, is likewise counted as approximation); 2024C then brought LP back with a Farkas proof and the bin-packing IP (14 points). hw2026 set 4 Q2 (max clique, subset sum, min colouring) and Q3(a) (set cover) drilled IP writing, and the LP recitation used 2024A-P4 as its worked binary-IP example. The wording of (a) is Volk's fixed template from 2024A-P4 and 2024C-P4 (\"write what are the variables and what is their meaning, and explain why every feasible solution to the IP corresponds to a solution to the original problem\").</p>\n<p><b>Source adjacency.</b> DPV 7.29 is the exercise right after DPV 7.28 (\"A linear program for shortest path\"), the plausible (NEAR) source of 2026A-P4. In 2026A Volk lifted textbook exercises almost word for word (W&amp;S 5.3 and 5.6 for P5, Erickson's FFT Exercise 2(c) for P2), and the July \"source mine\" of unused exercises next to his sources matched two 2026A problems exactly (36 points). The problem also covers LP integrality (TUM slides, never a primary exam topic) in the same question.</p>\n<p><b>Probability.</b> This exact problem: about 0.03. No Moed C has used a fresh textbook exercise so far (2024C and 2025C re-skinned earlier items). A write-an-IP part anywhere on 2026C: about 0.35 (the format research gives 0.30). As a standalone P4-type problem: about 0.12.</p>\n<p><b>Write-an-IP variants to practise.</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>DPV 7.27, change-making: take an integer variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for the number of coins of denomination <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></math>, with the constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>. The LP relaxation is not integral. For denominations <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></math>, the point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is LP-feasible, but no integral solution exists.</li>\n<li>Undirected Max-Cut as a linear IP, a sibling of 2026A-P5(b) that answers the approximation deck's \"Not a linear constraint!\" slide: maximize <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> subject to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The relaxation always has value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> (take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), so it is useless for rounding.</li>\n<li>W&amp;S Exercise 5.4 is on the same page as W&amp;S 5.3, the source of 2026A-P5 (about 0.03). Take the Max-SAT IP of 2024A-P4 and set each variable to true independently with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>. For a clause with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> literals, the AM-GM inequality gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mtext>&#x000A0;unsatisfied</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></math>. The function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></math> is concave in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> and is at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> (equal for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>), so it is at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>. This gives a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>-approximation. The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>-literal step is heavier than 2026A-P5(c).</li>\n<li>Repeats of his own templates: bin packing (2024C-P4), makespan (2025A-P3), dominating set (2023A-P2) and minimum colouring (hw2026 set 4 Q2(c)).</li>\n</ul>", "sol": "<p>For every actor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> we introduce a variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> that indicates whether actor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> is hired, and for every investor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> a variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> that indicates whether the producer collects the payment of investor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>. The IP is:</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>maximize</mtext></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\" /></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>subject&#x000A0;to</mtext></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>for&#x000A0;every&#x000A0;</mtext><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>m</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;and&#x000A0;every&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\" /><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>for&#x000A0;every&#x000A0;</mtext><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>m</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></math></div>\n<p><b>Meaning.</b> The objective is the total payment collected from investors minus the total salary of the hired actors. For a fixed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>, the constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> for all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> say that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> can be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>, that is, investor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> pays only if all the actors in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> are hired. (The condition \"all of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> is hired\" is an AND of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> conditions, so it is written with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> linear constraints, one per actor.)</p>\n<p><b>Feasible solutions are solutions.</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> be feasible and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>:</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> be the cast it describes. Every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> satisfies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> by the constraints, so, since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>,\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nSo every feasible solution is a cast, whose profit is at least the objective value, and in particular the IP optimum is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p><b>Solutions are feasible solutions.</b> Conversely, given a cast <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>, set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> if and only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> if and only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> then every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> is in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> holds, and if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> the constraints hold trivially. The objective value of this solution is exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Taking <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> to be an optimal cast shows that the IP optimum is at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>. Together, the optimum of the IP is exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p><b>Remark (another correct answer).</b> The AND can also be written with a single constraint per investor, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>. For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> values it also says that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>, so this IP is also a correct answer to (a) and earns full marks there. Its relaxation is weaker, though. For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></math> we have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, but <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> satisfies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and has value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>5</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>. Every integral feasible solution has value at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, so that relaxation has no integral optimal solution. This is why part (b) fixes the per-pair program <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>", "srcline": "<p>Dasgupta, Papadimitriou and Vazirani, <em>Algorithms</em>, Exercise 7.29 \"Hollywood\", part (a) (\"Express this problem as an integer linear program in which the variables take on values <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>\"), p.245. The explanation request is copied from Volk's 2024A-P4 and 2024C-P4.</p>"}, {"part": "b", "difficulty": "easy", "summary": "Given the per-pair integer program, relax it to an LP, show its optimum is at least OPT, and show that each investor variable may be taken as the minimum of its actors' variables.", "q": "<p><b>(b) [6 points]</b> From now on, consider the following integer program <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, in which <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> indicates whether actor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> is chosen and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> indicates whether the payment of investor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> is received (it may differ from the program you wrote in (a)):</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>maximize</mtext></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\" /></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\" /><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>subject&#x000A0;to</mtext></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>for&#x000A0;every&#x000A0;</mtext><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>m</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;and&#x000A0;every&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\" /><mtd columnalign=\"left\" /><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>for&#x000A0;every&#x000A0;</mtext><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>m</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></math></div>\n<p>You may use without proof that the optimal value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>. We'll relax <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> by replacing the constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, and denote by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math> the optimal value of the relaxation. Prove that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>. Prove also that the relaxation has an optimal solution <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> in which <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>min</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>.</p>", "basis": "<p><b>Why <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is stated in (b).</b> DPV 7.29(b) says only \"Now relax this to a linear program, and show that there must in fact be an integral optimal solution\". That claim depends on the formulation. The per-investor constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a correct answer to (a), but its relaxation has no integral optimal solution in general (counterexample in the (a) solution, checked with scipy linprog: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, relaxation value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>5</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>). The per-pair program <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> gave <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math> on 3000 random instances. So the part names the IP, as Volk did in 2026A-P5, where (b) stated the Max DiCut IP and (c) began \"We'll relax the integer program from (b) by replacing the constraints...\". In 2025B-P2(b) he asked instead for \"relaxation and rounding of the integer linear program from item (a)\". That is safe for set cover, whose natural IP is unique up to notation, but it is not safe here. Stating <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> shows a correct answer to (a) to anyone who reads ahead. Most of the (a) points are for the explanation (the meaning of the variables and both directions of the correspondence), which <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> does not supply.</p>\n<p><b>Structure.</b> Volk usually splits such a statement into a warm-up step and a main proof, as he split W&amp;S 5.6 in 2026A-P5 and as hw2026 set 3 Q3 is split into parts (a) to (f). The warm-up here is exactly hw2026 set 3 Q3(b) (densest subgraph: replace <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>; it stays feasible and the objective does not drop). Part (c) then uses it the way Q3(c) does: in the min form, a threshold on the actor variables decides the investor variables. The inequality <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math> is the recitation slide \"OPT(BIP) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo></mrow></math> OPT(reduced-LP)\". It is also the relaxation-bounds-OPT step at the end of the official solution of 2026A-P5(c), a maximization problem like this one (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>). The minimization versions, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, end 2025B-P2(b) (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>) and 2026B-P4(b) (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>3</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>). Given that an IP appears, a \"relax it and compare with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>\" part is very likely. This exact part goes with the problem (about 0.03).</p>", "sol": "<p>The relaxation of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>maximize</mtext></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\" /></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>subject&#x000A0;to</mtext></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>for&#x000A0;every&#x000A0;</mtext><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>m</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;and&#x000A0;every&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\" /><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>for&#x000A0;every&#x000A0;</mtext><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>m</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></math></div>\n<p>It is feasible (all variables equal to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>) and bounded (every variable is in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, so the objective is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>), so, as we have seen in class, it has an optimal solution.</p>\n<p><b><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>.</b> Every feasible solution of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> satisfies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so it is a feasible solution of the relaxation with the same objective value. In particular an optimal solution of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, whose value is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math> (as given, and as shown in the solution of (a)), is feasible for the relaxation, and therefore <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p><b>The minimum form.</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> be any optimal solution of the relaxation and define <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msubsup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>min</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>. This is well defined since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo></mrow></math>. The pair <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is feasible:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msubsup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>, by the definition of the minimum;</li>\n<li><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msubsup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msubsup></mrow></math> is the minimum of numbers in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>;</li>\n<li>the constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> are unchanged.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Its value is at least the value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Feasibility of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>, hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mo>min</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msubsup></mrow></math>, and since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>,\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msubsup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nBecause <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math> is the maximum, equality holds. So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an optimal solution with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>min</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>.</p>", "srcline": "<p>DPV <em>Algorithms</em>, Exercise 7.29(b), first half (\"Now relax this to a linear program\"), p.245. Stating the IP before relaxing it follows 2026A-P5(b) and (c). The minimum step is Volk's hw2026 set 3 Q3(b) (densest-subgraph LP, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>).</p>"}, {"part": "c", "difficulty": "medium", "summary": "Prove the relaxation has an integral optimal solution by hiring the actors above a uniformly random threshold, and conclude a polynomial time algorithm.", "q": "<p><b>(c) [8 points]</b> Prove that the relaxation from (b) has an optimal solution which is integral, that is, all of its coordinates are in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>. Conclude that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math> and that there is a polynomial time algorithm for the problem.</p>\n<p>Hint: let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> be an optimal solution as in (b), pick <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> uniformly at random, and consider the set of actors <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>:</mi><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>", "basis": "<p>The hint is the threshold trick from hw2026 set 3 Q3(c) to (f). There, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> from Q3(b), the set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>v</mi><mi>:</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> satisfies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>&#x027FA;</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></math>, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>v</mi></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math>, and finally \"there exists <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow></math>\". Here the min form of (b) gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02286;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mi>&#x027FA;</mi><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></math> in the same way. Every 2026 student has written this argument once. Deterministic threshold rounding was the core of 2025B-P2(b) (set cover, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></math>) and 2026B-P4 (prize-collecting vertex cover, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>); a random threshold is the natural next step. LP integrality (the LP deck's \"Totally Unimodular Matrices\" slides and the Kőnig proof in recitations 7 to 9) has never been examined as a primary topic in 10 Volk exams; it appears only as a remark in the 2023A-P4(c) solution. Every Moed C so far has added one or two class topics that its year's Moed A and B skipped. DPV itself frames the question as integrality \"as is the case, for example, with maximum flow and bipartite matching\", which is exactly the LP deck's slide before the TUM definition. My estimate for an integrality or TUM proof part on 2026C is about 0.08, and for this exact part 0.03.</p>", "sol": "<p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> be an optimal solution of the relaxation with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>min</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>, which exists by (b). For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>:</mi><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p><b>The investors that pay for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</b> For every investor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> and every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow></math>,\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02286;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mi>&#x027FA;</mi><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>r</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;for&#x000A0;every&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>&#x027FA;</mi><msub><mo>min</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>r</mi><mi>&#x027FA;</mi><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nHence\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p><b>Expectation.</b> Pick <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> uniformly at random. For every actor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math>, let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> be an indicator random variable for the event that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></math>, that is, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. For every investor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>, let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> be an indicator random variable for the event that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></math>, which by the above is the event that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02286;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow></math> is uniform in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>,\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nand in the same way <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup></mrow></math>. By linearity of expectation,\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p><b>Some <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow></math> is optimal.</b> For every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a set of actors, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math> by the definition of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math> by (b). Hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>. The set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math> changes only when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow></math> passes one of the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> numbers <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup></mrow></math>, and the sets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math> are nested, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math> takes at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> distinct values, and so does <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. If all of these values were smaller than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> would be at most the largest of them, which is smaller than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, a contradiction. So there is some <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, and together with the upper bound, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p><b>An integral optimal solution.</b> Set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> otherwise, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02286;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> otherwise. This is a feasible solution of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>: if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> then every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> is in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Its value is exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>. It is also feasible for the relaxation, so it is an optimal solution of the relaxation, and all of its coordinates are in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>(In fact every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow></math> works. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is constant on each interval between consecutive values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, and each such interval has positive length. The function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is non-negative with expectation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, so it is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> on every interval.)</p>\n<p>(Remark. The min form is what makes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> exactly the indicator that investor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> pays for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Without it, one can still round both families at threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow></math>: if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></math> then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>, so the rounded point is feasible for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and the same expectation argument goes through.)</p>\n<p><b>Conclusion.</b> We showed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math> and that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is an optimal set of actors. The algorithm follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Solve the relaxation in polynomial time (as we have seen in class, e.g. with the ellipsoid method) and obtain an optimal solution <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. By (b), replacing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup></mrow></math> by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>min</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup></mrow></math> keeps it optimal and does not change <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup></mrow></math>, so the analysis above applies to this <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Compute <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, in time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> each, for the at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> candidate sets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>:</mi><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. Every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> is one of them: if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo></mrow></math>, let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup></mrow></math> be the smallest value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></math>; then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>:</mi><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Output the best candidate.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is a candidate, the output has profit <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, and the total running time is polynomial.</p>\n<p><b>Alternative proof (total unimodularity).</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> be the constraint matrix of the relaxation of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. It has one row <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>, with exactly one <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and one <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, and the rows <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, each with a single <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. We show that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> is TUM by induction on the size of a square submatrix <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>, as in the recitation proof for bipartite incidence matrices:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> has a zero row, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>If some row of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> has exactly one nonzero entry <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x000B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, expanding along it gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x000B1;</mi><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math> for a smaller square submatrix <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math>, which is in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x000B1;</mi><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> by induction.</li>\n<li>Otherwise every row of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> contains a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so the columns of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> sum to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Adding slack variables brings the LP to standard form with matrix <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>&#x02223;</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, which is still TUM (expand along the identity columns), and the right-hand side (zeros and ones) is integral. As we have seen in class, all vertices are then integral. The LP is feasible and bounded, so it has an optimal vertex, and that vertex is integral.</p>", "srcline": "<p>DPV <em>Algorithms</em>, Exercise 7.29(b), second half (\"show that there must in fact be an integral optimal solution (as is the case, for example, with maximum flow and bipartite matching)\"), p.245. The hint and the proof re-use hw2026 set 3 Q3(c) to (f) (the threshold set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> on top of the min form of Q3(b)). The TUM route uses the LP deck's \"Totally Unimodular Matrices\" slides (corpus LP deck) and the recitation 7 to 9 TUM proof for bipartite incidence matrices.</p>"}]}, {"id": "c26-alt-p4-b", "sid": "ALT · P4-b", "difficulty": "medium", "src": "LP duality + approximation, 20 pts (6+6+8); continuation of 2026B-P4 (prize-collecting vertex cover) by the 2025B-P2 to 2025C-P2 step; Williamson-Shmoys section 1.4 (Lemma 1.7, Theorem 1.8)", "stem": "<p><b>Problem 4 (6+6+8=20 points)</b></p>\n<p>In the prize-collecting vertex cover problem, the input is an undirected graph <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Every vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></math> has a non-negative weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math>, and every edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>e</mi></mrow></math> has a non-negative \"penalty\" <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math>. We can either pay <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> to include <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> in our cover, or pay a penalty <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math> if the edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>e</mi></mrow></math> is uncovered. The goal is to find a subset of the vertices such that the total weight of the vertices in the set, plus the sum of penalties of uncovered edges, is minimal.</p>\n<p>Consider the following LP relaxation:</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>(P)</mtext></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>minimize</mtext></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\" /><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>subject&#x000A0;to</mtext></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>for&#x000A0;every&#x000A0;edge&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\" /><mtd columnalign=\"left\" /><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></math></div>\n<p>You may use any claim presented in class or proved in the homework.</p>", "parts": [{"part": "a", "difficulty": "easy", "summary": "Derive the dual of the prize-collecting vertex cover relaxation by adding slack variables and reading off the transposed constraints.", "q": "<p><b>(a) [6 points]</b> Show that the following LP is the dual of (P):</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>(D)</mtext></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>maximize</mtext></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\" /><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>subject&#x000A0;to</mtext></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>for&#x000A0;every&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mtext>(1)</mtext></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\" /><mtd columnalign=\"left\" /><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>for&#x000A0;every&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></math></div>", "basis": "<p><b>Whole problem: 0.03. Any continuation of 2026B-P4 (dual rounding, randomized rounding, or the set-cover generalisation): 0.07.</b></p>\n<p><b>The mechanism.</b> 2025C-P2 continued 2025B-P2 in the same slot, P2. It copied the set-cover preamble nearly word for word (the element index <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> became <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>, and 2025B's part (a), 'Write an integer linear program that corresponds to this problem', was replaced by the given 'Recall the integer LP that corresponds to this problem') and moved from Williamson-Shmoys section 1.3 (threshold rounding, which is what 2025B-P2(b) asked) to section 1.4, 'Rounding a dual solution'. The official 2025C solution file still has 2025B's point header, '(10+10=20 points)', although the question paper says 6+8+8=22. So the C paper was made by editing the B file. 2026B-P4 is a section 1.3-type threshold rounding (threshold 1/3, 3-approximation, 6+12=18 points). Taking the same step gives exactly this problem in slot P4.</p>\n<p><b>This part.</b> It is 2025C-P2(a), 'Show that the following LP is the dual of the relaxation' (6 points), with the vertex-cover LP. 2026A-P4(b), 'Compute the dual LP to (P)', has the same 6-point size. The class states the primal-dual pair for the standard form min <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> (LP deck, 'Duality' slides), and it also takes duals of LPs in other forms. The LP deck works a max/<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo></mrow></math> factory example with the rule 'Variable for every primal constraint / Constraint for every primal variable' (LP lecture deck), and then the dual of the max-flow LP. Recitation 7-9 derives, with a slack variable, that the primal <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> has the dual <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> ('Some Duality Exercise (2025A)', recitations7_8_9). hw2026 ex3 Q2 derives a dual with surplus variables (Homework_2026-solutions_ex3). The official 2025C solution adds slack variables and reads off <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>, and the model solution above does the same. Quoting the recitation rule for the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo></mrow></math> form with the matrix <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>&#x02223;</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an equally valid shortcut; the solution notes it.</p>\n<p><b>This LP pair was shown in class.</b> Recitation 7-9, 'Bipartite Matching-VC Duality' (recitations7_8_9), takes the dual of the relaxed matching LP: maximize <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math> subject to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0220B;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. That LP is exactly (D) with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> (rename <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math>). Its correct dual is exactly (P) with unit weights, since the upper bounds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> produce the variables <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The recitation's own dual, '<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>', drops the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math> and adds a bound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> that the primal does not produce. So students have seen this pair, in the unit-weight case and with a sloppy dual. That makes part (a) a natural exam item. It also names the error to expect when grading: a derivation that ignores the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> columns loses the constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math> and cannot reach (D) (see the grading notes in the solution). This evidence does not answer the discounts below, so the estimate stays at 0.03.</p>\n<p><b>Discounted because:</b> dual rounding, primal-dual and complementary slackness appear in no 2026 deck or recitation. The only 'dual' in the Approximation deck is 'dual of max flow'. 2024C did not continue 2024B-P5. If 2026C also has a separate LP-duality problem, this would put two dual derivations on one paper, which no Volk exam has done.</p>", "sol": "<p><b>Solution.</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>, and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> be the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> incidence matrix of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> is an endpoint of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>e</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> otherwise. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow></mrow></math> be the all-ones vector of length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The row of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> that belongs to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> has exactly two ones, at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>, so the constraints of (P) are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>We bring (P) to standard form by adding a slack variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for every edge:</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mtext>minimize&#x000A0;</mtext><msup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mi>z</mi><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>subject&#x000A0;to&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>M</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>I</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>I</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>This is an LP of the form: minimize <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mi>X</mi></mrow></math> subject to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, with variable vector <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>&#x0211D;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, constraint matrix <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x00028;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mi>M</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>I</mi></mtd><mtd><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>I</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> (one row per edge), <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. As we have seen in class, its dual is: maximize <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> subject to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>, with one variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math> per row of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>, that is, per edge constraint of (P). Here the dual is</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mtext>maximize&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>subject&#x000A0;to&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x00028;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>I</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>I</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>&#x00029;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo>&#x00028;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mi>w</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>p</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>Reading the constraints row by row (there is one dual constraint per primal variable):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the row of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math> that belongs to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mi>y</mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math>, which is constraint (1);</li>\n<li>the row of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>I</mi></mrow></math> that belongs to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math>;</li>\n<li>the row of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></math> that belongs to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, that is, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This is exactly (D).</p>\n<p><b>Shortcut.</b> (P) has the form: minimize <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mi>X</mi></mrow></math> subject to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x00028;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mi>M</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>I</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. As we have seen in the recitation, the dual of an LP of this form is: maximize <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> subject to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. The rows of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math> are the rows of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math> and of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>I</mi></mrow></math> above, so this gives (D) directly. An answer that quotes this rule correctly earns full marks.</p>\n<p><em>Grading notes.</em> Typical errors:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ignoring the columns of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math>. This loses the constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math>, so the derivation cannot end at (D). Recitation 7-9 makes the mirror-image slip on the unit-weight case of this LP pair: its dual of the relaxed matching LP has no <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Showing only that the value of every feasible <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> of (D) is at most the value of every feasible solution of (P) (weak duality). This does not show that (D) is the dual of (P).</li>\n</ul>", "srcline": "<p>2025C-P2(a) (2025C paper) with the 2026B-P4 preamble and LP (2026B official solution). The dual is Williamson &amp; Shmoys, section 1.4, LP (1.3), p.20-21 (ws-book.txt), adapted to the penalty variables. Recitation 7-9 'Bipartite Matching-VC Duality' (recitation 7-8-9) dualizes the unit-weight case of (D), and its 'Some Duality Exercise (2025A)' gives the rule for the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo></mrow></math> form.</p>"}, {"part": "b", "difficulty": "easy", "summary": "Show that in an optimal dual solution every edge with no tight endpoint has its dual variable equal to its penalty.", "q": "<p><b>(b) [6 points]</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup></mrow></math> be an optimal solution for (D), and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> be the set of vertices <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> for which the constraint (1) holds with equality, that is,</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi><mi>:</mi><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>Prove that every edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math> that has no endpoint in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> satisfies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</p>", "basis": "<p>This is 2025C-P2(b) ('the sets whose constraint (2) holds with equality form a set cover') with one change: an edge may now stay uncovered, and then it must pay its penalty. The official 2025C solution replaces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup></mrow></math> by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003F5;</mi></mrow></math> and uses optimality, which is Williamson-Shmoys Lemma 1.7. The same argument works here once the upper bound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math> is added to the minimum that defines <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003F5;</mi></mrow></math>. That is the only new idea in the part, and it is the kind of small twist Volk adds when he reuses a problem (as 2025C-P5 did with 2024B-P6, changing 2 symbols to 3). Probability as for part (a): about 0.03 for this exact problem.</p>", "sol": "<p><b>Solution.</b> Suppose, towards a contradiction, that some edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02260;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup></mrow></math> is feasible, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></math>, constraint (1) is strict for both endpoints:</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003F5;</mi></mrow></math> be the smallest of the three slacks:</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003F5;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>min</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>Define <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math> by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msubsup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003F5;</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>f</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msubsup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></math>. Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math> is feasible for (D). The variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math> appears only in constraint (1) for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>, in constraint (1) for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>, and in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>f</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msubsup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003F5;</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></math> by the choice of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003F5;</mi></mrow></math>. The same holds for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>For every other vertex, the left-hand side of (1) does not change.</li>\n<li><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msubsup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003F5;</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math> by the choice of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003F5;</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>But <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>f</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msubsup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003F5;</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup></mrow></math>, which contradicts the optimality of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup></mrow></math>. Hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math> for every edge with no endpoint in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math>.</p>", "srcline": "<p>Williamson &amp; Shmoys, Lemma 1.7, p.22 (ws-book.txt): 'The collection of subsets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>I</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math>, is a set cover', proved by setting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msubsup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003F5;</mi></mrow></math>. Also the official solution of 2025C-P2(b) (2025C official solution).</p>"}, {"part": "c", "difficulty": "medium", "summary": "Prove that taking the tight vertices and paying penalties of uncovered edges is a 2-approximation via charging and weak duality.", "q": "<p><b>(c) [8 points]</b> Consider the algorithm that solves (D), outputs the set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> from item (b), and pays the penalty <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math> of every edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>e</mi></mrow></math> that has no endpoint in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math>. Prove that this is a 2-approximation algorithm for the prize-collecting vertex cover problem.</p>", "basis": "<p>This is 2025C-P2(c), 'Show an efficient <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math>-approximation algorithm... based on solving (D) and picking a set cover as described in item (b)' (8 points), for the prize-collecting vertex cover. The official 2025C solution uses exactly the charging chain of Williamson-Shmoys Theorem 1.8: cost <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>f</mi><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>. The only new step is that uncovered edges enter the sum once, through part (b).</p>\n<p><b>Checked.</b> A brute-force test on about 1,500 random graphs with 2 to 6 vertices solved (P) and (D) with an LP solver and compared the result with the exact OPT. It confirmed that the optima of (P) and (D) are equal, so (D) really is the dual. It confirmed the claim of (b) (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math> on every uncovered edge) and the bound of (c), <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>e</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math> (worst ratio exactly 2). It also found a worst ratio of 3 for the tight-edge variant and 2 for the primal-dual variant. A separate test on 800 random instances, with the expectation computed exactly, confirmed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math> for the randomized variant (worst ratio 2).</p>\n<p><b>Other forms of the same slot</b> (together about 0.07 with this one): randomized rounding of (P); prize-collecting set cover with frequency <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math>; local ratio (W&amp;S Exercise 7.3); VC half-integrality (W&amp;S Exercise 1.5(a)). The randomized rounding taught in the course is the Approximation deck's rule 'set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>' ('Will see an example in HW'). Its homework example, hw2026 s4-p3 (Homework_2026-solutions_ex4), takes each set with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup></mrow></math>, repeats <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003F5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> rounds and takes a union bound, so here it would give only an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> guarantee. The factor-2 randomized variant in the solution (probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, then the AM-GM step) scales the LP values first; no deck, recitation or 2026 homework does that. Feige's handout 1 Q1 only partly overlaps W&amp;S Exercise 1.5; the handout itself says 'Partly overlaps Problem 1.5 in [WS11]'. Its (b) is close to W&amp;S 1.5(a), but its bipartite-doubling part (a) and its part (c) (an optimal integral cover that keeps the 1s and drops the 0s) are not in W&amp;S, and W&amp;S 1.5(b) (planar graphs, 3/2) is not in Feige.</p>", "sol": "<p><b>Solution.</b> <em>Running time.</em> (D) is feasible (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>) and bounded (its value is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>e</mi></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math>), so it has an optimal solution <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup></mrow></math>. As we have seen in class, an LP can be solved in polynomial time (for example with the ellipsoid method). (D) has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> variables and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> constraints, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup></mrow></math> is found in polynomial time, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> is then computed in time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Every subset of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow></math> is a feasible solution of the problem, so the output is feasible.</p>\n<p><em>Cost.</em> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi></mrow></math> be the set of edges with no endpoint in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math>. The cost of the output is</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>For every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup></mrow></math> by the definition of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math>. For every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup></mrow></math> by item (b). Hence</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02229;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>where the second equality changes the order of summation: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup></mrow></math> appears once for every endpoint of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>e</mi></mrow></math> that lies in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math>. (This is the charging argument: every vertex of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> is paid for by its incident edges, and every uncovered edge pays its own penalty.) An edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></math> has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02229;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, and an edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></math> has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02229;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02229;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p><em>Comparison with OPT.</em> By weak duality, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, the optimum of (P). Directly: for every feasible <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> of (P),</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>using <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and the constraints of (P) for the first inequality, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> with the constraints of (D) for the last one. Also <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>: if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup></mrow></math> is an optimal solution, then setting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> exactly for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> exactly for the edges not covered by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup></mrow></math> gives a feasible solution of (P) whose value is the cost of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup></mrow></math>. Therefore</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p><b>Remarks.</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The factor 2 is tight for this algorithm. Take a single edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. Then (D) is: maximize <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math> subject to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, both <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> are tight, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> while <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>It matters that the algorithm pays only for edges not covered by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math>. If it paid <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math> for every edge with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math> (the 'take all tight sets' rule of the set-cover view, where every edge lies in 3 sets: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> and its own penalty), the same computation gives only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>e</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup></mrow></math>. On a single edge with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, that rule pays 3 while <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Compare with 2026B-P4: threshold rounding of (P) at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> gives only 3. Dual rounding gives 2.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Variants of this part.</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Primal-dual (no LP solver).</em> Start with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo></mrow></math>. While some edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>e</mi></mrow></math> has no endpoint in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math>, raise <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math> until constraint (1) of an endpoint or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math> becomes tight, and add the tight endpoints to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math>. No edge at a vertex of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> is raised again, so the vertices of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> stay tight. At the end <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is feasible for (D) and every uncovered edge has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The same charging gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>e</mi></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><em>Prize-collecting set cover with frequency <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math>.</em> The same proof gives an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math>-approximation: an uncovered element is charged once and a covered one at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> times. Threshold rounding of the primal at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> this is 2026B-P4.</li>\n<li><em>Randomized rounding of (P).</em> Put each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> in the cover independently with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The expected vertex cost is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>v</mi></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup></mrow></math>. An edge is uncovered only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. Then, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup></mrow></math>, it is uncovered with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup></mrow></math>. So the expected cost is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ul>", "srcline": "<p>Williamson &amp; Shmoys, Theorem 1.8, p.22-23 (ws-book.txt): 'The dual rounding algorithm described above is an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math>-approximation algorithm for the set cover problem' (charging argument plus weak duality). The primal-dual variant is W&amp;S section 1.5. The official solution of 2025C-P2(c) (2025C official solution) uses the same chain.</p>"}]}, {"id": "c26-alt-p5-a", "sid": "ALT · P5-a", "difficulty": "medium", "src": "LP-rounding approximation, 20 pts (6+6+8); Feige, Weizmann Approximation algorithms final exam (20 Feb 2019) Q1 'K3 removal' items 2-3 (item 1, the strongly polynomial factor-3 algorithm, is a variant under (b)); re-skins Volk 2023B-P3 / 2026B-P4 (1/3-threshold rounding) and the random partition of 2023A-P3 / 2026A-P5(a), derandomized as on the approximation deck's conditional-expectations slides", "stem": "<p><b>Problem (6+6+8 = 20 points).</b> In the <em>triangle removal</em> problem, the input is an undirected graph <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> vertices, where every edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math> has a non-negative weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math>. A <em>triangle</em> in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> is a set of three vertices <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> such that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math>. The goal is to find a set of edges <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math> of minimum total weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math> such that the graph <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mi>&#x029F5;</mi><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> contains no triangle.</p>", "parts": [{"part": "a", "difficulty": "easy", "summary": "Write an integer linear program with a 0/1 variable per edge and one covering constraint per triangle for minimum-weight triangle removal.", "q": "<p><b>(a) [6 points]</b> Write an integer linear program that corresponds to this problem, with a variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math> for every edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math>. Explain your answer briefly.</p>", "basis": "<p><b>Template: write an IP from scratch.</b> A part of the form 'write an integer linear program for X' appeared in 5 of the 8 Volk exams from 2023A to 2025C (2023A-P2 dominating set, 2024A-P4 weighted Max-SAT, 2024C-P4 bin packing, 2025A-P3 makespan, 2025B-P2(a) set cover) and in neither 2026 exam. The research analyses treat its return as possible, not likely. The flow research says only that 'A write-an-ILP item may return' and lists it as one of three candidate slots missing from 2026A/B. The coverage research rates 'an LP-theory proof and/or an ILP formulation' Medium-high, as one candidate among several, on the 2024B-to-2024C precedent (an LP-light Moed B followed by a Moed C with a bin-packing ILP). The format research dissents: 'A from-scratch \"write the ILP\" part is less likely (0.30)'. The wording here is 2025B-P2(a) ('Write an integer linear program that corresponds to this problem. Explain your answer briefly.'). The constraint has the same shape as the hyperedge constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> in the LP-based solution of Volk's 2023B-P3, because the triangles of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> form a 3-uniform hypergraph whose vertices are the edges of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math>.</p><p><b>Source adjacency.</b> Feige's Weizmann final exam (20 Feb 2019) asks exactly this in Question 1, item 2 ('Write an integer program (IP) representing the K3R problem, in which the variables are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> for every edge'). The homework handouts of the same Feige course contain the three Williamson-Shmoys exercises that this research matched to Volk problems: 5.1 (extended in handout 5 Q2; the NEAR source of 2023A-P3), 5.3 + 5.6 (handout 5 Q3, in the exact (a)/(b)/(c) layout of 2026A-P5; confirmed verbatim) and 6.1 (handout 6 Q1; graded only PLAUSIBLE/NEAR as the source of 2026B-P5). The handouts are derivatives of W&amp;S, and no Volk problem has yet been traced to Feige's exam itself, so the exam is a plausible co-source, not a confirmed one. It is a take-home exam. Its items 1-3 are the short ones (10, 10 and 5 points); items 4 and 5 are open-ended ('Up to 15 points, depending on what you find', 'Up to 10 points'). This problem uses items 2 and 3. Item 1, a strongly polynomial factor-3 algorithm, is listed as a variant under (b).</p><p><b>Probability.</b> About 0.03 for this exact problem (this item's own estimate). The wider families, with the source of each figure: an LP-rounding approximation problem at P5, about 0.28 (the mock-p5 item's estimate); the format research's P4 'LP-based' slot, 0.55, which also covers pure duality problems ('given LP/IP (explain or compute the dual) or relaxation + rounding'), and its P5 Approximation-deck slot (approximation algorithm, SDP/PSD or lattice), 0.50; a from-scratch write-an-IP part, 0.30 from the format research and about 0.35 in the sibling item alt-p4-a. Of the three parts, this one transfers best: any write-an-IP part tests the same skill, one 0/1 variable per decision and one covering constraint per forbidden structure.</p>", "sol": "<p><b>Solution.</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4AF;</mi></mrow></math> be the set of triangles of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math>. For every edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math> we use a variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> which indicates whether <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>e</mi></mrow></math> is removed, that is, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> if and only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></math>.</p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>minimize</mtext></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\" /></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>subject&#x000A0;to</mtext></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>for&#x000A0;every&#x000A0;triangle&#x000A0;</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4AF;</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\" /><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>for&#x000A0;every&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></math></div><p><b>Correctness.</b> Sets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math> and vectors <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>E</mi></msup></mrow></math> are in bijection via <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> if and only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></math>, and the objective is exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Removing edges cannot create a new triangle, so the triangles of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mi>&#x029F5;</mi><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are exactly the triangles <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4AF;</mi></mrow></math> none of whose three edges is in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>F</mi></mrow></math>, i.e. with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. This is exactly the case in which the constraint of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is violated. Hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> is feasible if and only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mi>&#x029F5;</mi><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> has no triangle, and the optimum of the program equals the optimum <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math> of the problem.</p><p><b>Size.</b> There are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> variables and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x1D4AF;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> triangle constraints, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4AF;</mi></mrow></math> can be listed in time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> by checking every triple of vertices, so the program has polynomial size. (If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> has no triangle there are no covering constraints and the optimum is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo></mrow></math>.)</p>", "srcline": "<p>Feige, <em>Approximation algorithms</em> (Weizmann Institute, 2018/19; course page wisdom.weizmann.ac.il/~feige/Apx2018/), final exam of 20 February 2019, Question 1 'K3 removal (K3R)', item 2 (IP with a variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> per edge). The constraint shape comes from Volk 2023B-P3 (the LP-based 'different solution' in the official solution: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for every hyperedge), and the wording from Volk 2025B-P2(a).</p>"}, {"part": "b", "difficulty": "easy", "summary": "Prove that solving the LP relaxation and removing every edge whose fractional value is at least one third is a 3-approximation.", "q": "<p><b>(b) [6 points]</b> We relax the integer program from (a) by replacing the constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Consider the algorithm that solves the relaxation and, given an optimal solution <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup></mrow></math>, returns the set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi><mi>:</mi><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>. Prove that this algorithm is a 3-approximation algorithm for the triangle removal problem.</p>", "basis": "<p><b>Template: threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> rounding.</b> This is Volk's most repeated approximation problem. It appeared in 2023B-P3 (vertex cover in a 3-uniform hypergraph, 3-approximation, 16 pts; the official solution first gives the greedy algorithm that takes all three vertices of an arbitrary remaining hyperedge, so the chosen hyperedges are disjoint, and then gives this 1/3 rounding as 'A different solution'), 2024B-P5 (the recitation 10 weighted hitting set, threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>, 16 pts), 2025B-P2(b) (set cover with frequency <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math>, 10 pts) and 2026B-P4 (prize-collecting vertex cover, threshold 1/3, 6+12 = 18 pts): 16+16+10+18 = 60 points over 4 exams, the total the coverage research gives for 'LP threshold rounding'. Triangle removal is weighted vertex cover in the 3-uniform hypergraph of triangles, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> and the proof is 2026B-P4(b) line by line.</p><p><b>Moed C pattern.</b> Moed C problems are siblings of that year's A/B problems applied to a new object (2024C-P2 of 2024B-P4, 2024C-P3 of 2024B-P6, 2025C-P2 of 2025B-P2), so a 1/3-threshold rounding on a new covering problem is a natural sibling of 2026B-P4. <em>Against:</em> 2025C followed 2025B's primal threshold rounding with <em>dual</em> rounding rather than repeating it. The P5 slot may therefore ask for the dual (variant below) or for randomized rounding instead.</p><p><b>Variant (Feige item 2, second half).</b> Write the dual of the relaxation. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> dropped, which does not change the optimum since weights are non-negative, the dual is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4AF;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow></math> subject to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>&#x0220B;</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>: a fractional packing of triangles. A 2025C-P2-style follow-up would ask to show that the edges whose dual constraint is tight in an optimal dual solution remove every triangle and weigh at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math> (W&amp;S §1.4 with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>). This is likely only if no other dual appears on the paper.</p><p><b>Variant (Feige item 1, 10 points there).</b> 'Design a strongly polynomial time algorithm that provides a factor 3 approximation for K3R.' The algorithm of (b), with the LP solved as in class, does not qualify: the ellipsoid method takes a number of steps that grows with the bit length of the weights. (Tardos' algorithm for LPs with a 0/1 constraint matrix would make it strongly polynomial, but it is not part of the course.) With unit weights, the greedy solution of 2023B-P3 carries over: while a triangle is left, remove all three of its edges. The chosen triangles are edge-disjoint, so if there are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> of them then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. With weights, use local ratio. Keep residual weights <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math>, initially <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math>. While some triangle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math> has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> on all three of its edges, let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>min</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math> and subtract <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow></math> from the residual weight of each edge of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math>. Return <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>e</mi><mi>:</mi><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>. Every step makes at least one more residual weight 0, so there are at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> steps, each using <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> comparisons and subtractions. When the loop stops, every triangle has an edge in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>F</mi></mrow></math>. Setting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow></math> (and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for triangles never chosen) gives a feasible solution of the dual above, since residual weights stay non-negative, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>T</mi></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>. Every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></math> has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>&#x0220B;</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow></math>, hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>T</mi></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>&#x02229;</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>T</mi></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>3</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>. (Checked against the exact optimum on 1500 random weighted graphs in scratchpad/k3_localratio_check.py; the worst ratio was exactly 3.) Local ratio and primal-dual proofs are not on the 2026 approximation deck, which has only the unweighted greedy vertex cover, so on Volk's paper this variant is less likely than (b).</p><p><b>Probability.</b> About 0.28 for an LP-rounding approximation at P5 (the mock-p5 item's estimate) and about 0.03 for this problem (this item's own estimate).</p>", "sol": "<p><b>Solution.</b> By (a) the relaxation has polynomially many variables and constraints, so as we have seen in class it can be solved in polynomial time (e.g. by the ellipsoid algorithm). It is feasible (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>e</mi></mrow></math>) and its objective is non-negative, so an optimal solution <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup></mrow></math> exists. Denote its value by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>. For every edge let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> otherwise, so that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>e</mi><mi>:</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>.</p><p><b>Feasibility.</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> be a triangle of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup></mrow></math> is feasible, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so at least one of the three values is at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> (otherwise the sum would be smaller than 1). That edge is in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>F</mi></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is not a triangle of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mi>&#x029F5;</mi><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Every triangle of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mi>&#x029F5;</mi><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is a triangle of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math>, hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mi>&#x029F5;</mi><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> contains no triangle.</p><p><b>Cost.</b> For every edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>e</mi></mrow></math> it holds that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>3</mn><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup></mrow></math>: if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> this is true since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, and if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> by the rounding rule. Since all weights are non-negative,</p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>3</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><p>The last inequality holds because the program is a relaxation: the 0/1 vector of an optimal set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup></mrow></math> is a feasible solution of the LP of value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p><b>Running time.</b> Listing the triangles takes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, solving the LP takes polynomial time, and rounding takes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. This is the vertex cover rounding we saw in class, with the threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> in place of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, because every constraint has three variables.</p><p><em>Remark (not required).</em> The factor 3 cannot be improved for this rounding rule. Take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></math> with unit weights. Every edge lies in exactly 2 of the 4 triangles, so summing the 4 constraints gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>e</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. Removing two disjoint edges destroys all 4 triangles, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. The vector <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> for all 6 edges is an optimal LP solution, and rounding it returns all 6 edges, which is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>.</p>", "srcline": "<p>Feige, Weizmann <em>Approximation algorithms</em> final exam (20 Feb 2019), Question 1 item 2 ('Show how to round the linear programming relaxation of this IP so as to get a factor 3 approximation for K3R'). The proof re-skins Volk 2026B-P4(b), 2025B-P2(b) and 2023B-P3 with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, and the approximation deck slides 'Rounding Scheme for VC' / 'Bounding the Approximation Ratio'. Item 1 of the same question (a strongly polynomial factor-3 algorithm, 10 points) is the variant in the basis.</p>"}, {"part": "c", "difficulty": "medium", "summary": "Show with a random bipartition that some triangle-removing edge set has at most half the total weight, and derandomize it by conditional expectations.", "q": "<p><b>(c) [8 points]</b> Prove that for every graph <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> there is a set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math> such that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mi>&#x029F5;</mi><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> contains no triangle and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Give a deterministic polynomial-time algorithm that finds such a set.<br>(Hint: a bipartite graph contains no triangle.)</p>", "basis": "<p><b>Template: random cut plus derandomization by conditional expectations.</b> The approximation deck proves 'Claim: in every graph <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> edges, there’s a cut whose size is at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>' with a random cut, and derandomizes it on the 'Method of Conditional Expectations' slides. Only the random half has been graded so far: 2023A-P3 (Max Triple Cut; the question asks only for 'an efficient algorithm that obtains a 2/3-approximation', and the official solution gives the random 3-partition) and 2026A-P5(a) (a random cut gives 1/4 for Max DiCut). Derandomization is mentioned in one sentence of the 2023A-P3 solution ('This algorithm can be converted to a deterministic algorithm using the conditional expectations method, as seen in class.') and has never been required in a graded part, so the 8 points here would be its first appearance. Here the removed set is the complement of a random cut, and the hint makes feasibility immediate. The coverage research lists conditional expectations among the approximation techniques 2026 has not used, and each Moed C adds 1-2 class topics that its year's A/B skipped.</p><p><b>Source.</b> Feige's final exam, Question 1 item 3, almost word for word: 'Show that every graph contains a solution <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>E</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math> of cost <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Give a deterministic algorithm that finds such a solution. (... the hint that bipartite graphs contain no <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math>.)'</p><p><b>Variants.</b> W&amp;S Exercise 5.2: the greedy Max-Cut algorithm (put vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> on the side that cuts more of its edges to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>) is a 1/2-approximation and is the conditional-expectations derandomization. Feige handout 5 Q2(c)(d): a uniformly random balanced <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>-partition cuts each edge with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, plus a deterministic version (about 0.03, a Moed C re-skin of 2023A-P3).</p><p><b>Probability.</b> About 0.03 for this problem (this item's own estimate). The coverage research rates 'An approximation problem with a technique 2026 did not use (Goemans-Williamson rounding, conditional expectations, greedy, or LLL)' as Medium.</p>", "sol": "<p><b>Existence.</b> Put every vertex in a set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi></mrow></math> with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, independently of all other vertices, and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>V</mi><mi>&#x029F5;</mi><mi>U</mi></mrow></math>. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>F</mi></mrow></math> be the set of edges whose two endpoints are on the same side (both in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi></mrow></math> or both in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>W</mi></mrow></math>).</p><p><em><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>F</mi></mrow></math> is always a solution.</em> Every edge of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>&#x029F5;</mi><mi>F</mi></mrow></math> goes between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>W</mi></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mi>&#x029F5;</mi><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is bipartite with sides <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></math>. It contains no triangle: among any three vertices <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></math>, two lie on the same side (pigeonhole), and the edge between them, if it exists in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math>, is in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>F</mi></mrow></math>. This holds for every outcome, including <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo></mrow></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo></mrow></math>.</p><p><em>Expected weight.</em> For every edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math>, let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math> be an indicator random variable for the event that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> are on the same side. Then</p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><p>Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math>, by linearity of expectation</p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><p>A random variable cannot always be larger than its expectation, so some choice of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi></mrow></math> gives a solution <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>F</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math> (the probabilistic method).</p><p><b>Deterministic algorithm (method of conditional expectations, as seen in class for Max-Cut).</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> be the vertices, and suppose <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> have already been assigned to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi></mrow></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>W</mi></mrow></math>. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> be the set of edges whose two endpoints are both among <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> and on the same side, and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> be the set of edges with at least one endpoint not yet assigned. The conditional expectation of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> given these choices is</p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003A6;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><p>because an edge with both endpoints assigned is in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>F</mi></mrow></math> with probability 1 or 0, and an edge with an unassigned endpoint is in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>F</mi></mrow></math> with probability exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, as before. So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003A6;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> can be computed in polynomial time. We have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003A6;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003A6;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> for the final partition. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> goes to each side with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003A6;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><msubsup><mi>&#x003A6;</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><msubsup><mi>&#x003A6;</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>&#x003A6;</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>&#x003A6;</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> are the conditional expectations after also placing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi></mrow></math> or in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>W</mi></mrow></math>. Hence one of the two is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003A6;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>, and we place <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> on that side. The invariant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003A6;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math> holds for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math>, so at the end <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003A6;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math>, and by the argument above <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mi>&#x029F5;</mi><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is bipartite and hence triangle-free.</p><p><b>Explicit form and running time.</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow></math> (resp. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>W</mi></msub></mrow></math>) be the total weight of the edges from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> to the already assigned vertices in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi></mrow></math> (resp. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>W</mi></mrow></math>). Only these edges change their contribution, from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math> or 0, so</p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>&#x003A6;</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003A6;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>W</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>W</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003A6;</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003A6;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><p>So the algorithm places <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi></mrow></math> if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>W</mi></msub></mrow></math> and in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>W</mi></mrow></math> otherwise: each vertex goes to the side to which its already assigned neighbours are connected with smaller total weight. One can also check this directly. When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is placed, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>F</mi></mrow></math> gains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>W</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, and every edge is counted in exactly one step (when its later endpoint is placed), so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math>. With adjacency lists each edge is inspected twice, so the running time is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>", "srcline": "<p>Feige, Weizmann <em>Approximation algorithms</em> final exam (20 Feb 2019), Question 1 item 3 (5 points there, with the bipartite hint). The technique comes from the Volk approximation deck ('Approximating Max Cut', 'Method of Conditional Expectations'), Volk 2023A-P3 and 2026A-P5(a), and Williamson &amp; Shmoys, Exercise 5.2, pp. 135-136 (greedy Max-Cut equals derandomization by conditional expectations).</p>"}]}, {"id": "c26-alt-p5-b", "sid": "ALT · P5-b", "difficulty": "medium", "src": "Lattices / LLL (Approximation deck), 20 pts (6+8+6): LLL'82 Prop. 1.6 (the induction in its proof, then (1.7), then (1.8)) = Galbraith Lemma 17.2.8(1),(3) + Thm 17.2.12(4) = Regev TAU HW2 4(b) (the j=i case of (1.7)), 4(d) ((1.7)) and 4(c) ((1.8)); siblings of 2024C-P5 = (1.9), which appears in all three sources, and which one Volk used is not established", "stem": "<p><b>Problem 5 (6+8+6=20 points)</b></p>\n<p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> be a lattice and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> be a reduced basis for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>, as computed by the LLL algorithm. As in class, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> denote the Gram-Schmidt vectors of the basis: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>&#x027E9;</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>&#x027E9;</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>. Recall that the basis is <em>reduced</em> if</p>\n<ul>\n<li><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and</li>\n<li><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Reminder (the columns of the matrix are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>):</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo>&#x00028;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><mo>&#x022EF;</mo></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mo>&#x022EF;</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>You may use any claim presented in class.</p>", "parts": [{"part": "a", "difficulty": "easy", "summary": "Prove that in an LLL-reduced basis the squared Gram-Schmidt lengths shrink by at most a factor 2 per step: ||b~_j||^2 is at most 2^(i-j) ||b~_i||^2 whenever j is at most i.", "q": "<p><b>(a) (6 points)</b> Prove that for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>", "basis": "<ul>\n<li><b>Source adjacency (the exact origin is not established).</b> Parts (a), (b) and (c) are the three steps of the proof of Proposition 1.6 in Lenstra, Lenstra and Lovász (1982): first the induction <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> (this part), then (1.7) (part (b)), then (1.8) (part (c)). Galbraith, <em>Mathematics of Public Key Cryptography</em>, has the same three statements as Lemma 17.2.8(1), Lemma 17.2.8(3) and Theorem 17.2.12(4). Regev's TAU homework has (1.7) and (1.8) as HW2 Problem 4(b)/(d) and 4(c). All three sources also contain Volk's 2024C-P5, with the same inequality (VERBATIM in content, not in wording). 2024C-P5 reads \"Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> be a lattice and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> a reduced basis for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> as computed by the LLL algorithm. Prove that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>\". LLL'82 reads \"Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> be a reduced basis for a lattice <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>&#x0211D;</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math>... (1.9) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>\". Galbraith's Theorem 17.2.12(5) is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. Regev's Problem 4(a) reads \"Show that a δ-LLL reduced basis <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> of a lattice Λ with δ = 3/4 satisfies... (a) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mi>&#x0039B;</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>\". Which of the three Volk used is not established. Evidence for LLL'82: the wording (\"a reduced basis for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>\", the letter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>), and the deck's lattice section follows LLL'82 §1, with the same definition of \"reduced\" and the same constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>. The printed \"Reminder\" <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x0220F;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo></mrow></math> is a step in both the LLL'82 proof and Galbraith's. The Regev link is PLAUSIBLE: it is a TAU course, and Volk's exams (the 2024C-P5 reminder, the 2026B-1d solution) write <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> as Regev does, where LLL'82 and Galbraith write <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup></mrow></math>. Nothing shows that Volk took 2024C-P5 from it. None of these statements except (1.9) has appeared on his exams.</li>\n<li><b>Moed C pattern.</b> Every Moed C so far has a problem that re-proves a lecture theorem: 2024C-P5 (the LLL bound), 2024C-P6 (a strong-duality case via Farkas) and 2025C-P3 (the random-walk proof redone for a lazy walk). This part is the chain from the lecture proof of \"Short Vector in Reduced Basis\" (Approximation deck: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo>&#x022EF;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>), started at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> instead of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>. The official 2024C-P5 solution opens the same way: \"We proved that for a reduced basis, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>. By induction on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math>...\".</li>\n<li><b>Tools.</b> Every step uses a claim from the deck: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>, the orthogonal expansion from \"Lower bound on shortest vector\", <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x0220F;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo></mrow></math>. No outside material is needed.</li>\n<li><b>Probability.</b> This problem (parts (a)-(c)) or a near variant (the general-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> lemma at the end of the solution to (c), or a re-proof of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>): <b>0.025</b>. Any written LLL or lattice problem on 2026C: <b>0.10</b>.</li>\n</ul>", "sol": "<p>As we have seen in class, in a reduced basis <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. For completeness, here is the argument. The vectors <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> are orthogonal, so the second property of a reduced basis says</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msubsup><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>By the first property <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>. Equivalently, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>We prove the claim by induction on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>, both sides equal <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></math>, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>. So by the induction hypothesis for the pair <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, and by the inequality above with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>This is the class chain started at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> instead of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo>&#x022EF;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>.</p>", "srcline": "<p>Lenstra, Lenstra and Lovász, <em>Factoring polynomials with rational coefficients</em>, Math. Ann. 261 (1982), proof of Proposition 1.6 (https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01457454). From (1.5) and (1.4), <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, \"so by induction\" <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. Local OCR copy:, where the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo></mrow></math> signs are garbled. The same statement is Galbraith, <em>Mathematics of Public Key Cryptography</em> v2.0, Lemma 17.2.8(1), p.370: \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>\", with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> (http://www.math.auckland.ac.nz/~sgal018/crypto-book/crypto-book.html). Regev's homework has no separate item for it. In the course it is the chain in the proof of \"Short Vector in Reduced Basis\" (Approximation deck).</p>"}, {"part": "b", "difficulty": "medium", "summary": "Prove that ||b_i||^2 is at most 2^(i-1) ||b~_i||^2 for every i, and conclude the same bound for ||b_j||^2 whenever j is at most i (LLL'82 (1.7)).", "q": "<p><b>(b) (8 points)</b> Prove that for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>and conclude that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>.</p>", "basis": "<ul>\n<li><b>Lineage.</b> This is LLL'82 (1.7), \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>\". Its proof in the paper first does the case <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></math>, through the intermediate line <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>, and this part asks for the same two steps. The case <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> is Regev's HW2 Problem 4(b), \"For any <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo></mrow></math>\". The general case is 4(d), \"For any <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo></mrow></math>\", with the names <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> swapped. In Galbraith the two steps are Lemma 17.2.8(2), <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>, and Lemma 17.2.8(3). Volk has not used any of them.</li>\n<li><b>Moed C pattern, continued.</b> The lecture lemma gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> (deck). This part proves the same bound for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></math>. It is the same kind of one-parameter re-proof as 2025C-P3, which redid the lecture random-walk proof for a lazy walk.</li>\n<li><b>Promotion signal (weaker this year).</b> In 2024 Moed A had a Gram-Schmidt T/F, 2024A-1c: \"There exists a polynomial time algorithm that given <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>&#x0211D;</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math> finds an orthonormal basis for the subspace spanned by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></math>\". It sits in the lattice section of the deck but does not mention lattices. Moed B had a lattice T/F (2024B-1c), and Moed C had the 14-point LLL problem 2024C-P5. So the 2024 \"promotion\" of lattices holds only if 2024A-1c is counted loosely, as a Gram-Schmidt/lattice item. In 2026 lattices appear only in 2026B-1d (the lattice spanned by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>), not in 2026A. The topic asked as T/F in both 2026 sittings is traces and the characteristic polynomial, so the promotion argument favours that topic.</li>\n<li><b>Coverage.</b> The mock P5 already covers the SDP and Goemans-Williamson family. Without this alternate the lattice family would be uncovered. Gram-Schmidt/lattice items were worth 26 points in 2024: 6 for 2024A-1c, 6 for 2024B-1c and 14 for 2024C-P5. Counting only the items that mention lattices, the total is 20.</li>\n</ul>", "sol": "<p><b>The case <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></math>.</b> By the definition of the Gram-Schmidt vectors, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The vectors <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> are pairwise orthogonal, so when we expand <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>&#x027E9;</mi></mrow></math>, every mixed term <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>l</mi></msub><mi>&#x027E9;</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></math> vanishes. This is the same computation as in the class proofs that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msub><mo>min</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo></mrow></math> and that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo></mrow></math>. Hence</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msubsup><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>By the first property of a reduced basis <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, and by part (a) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></math>. Therefore</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. Finally, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math> is equivalent to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, that is, to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></math>, which holds for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>. For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> both sides are equal, since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p><b>The general case.</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. By the case just proved (for the index <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>) and by part (a),</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p><b>Note.</b> The expansion needs orthogonality, and the triangle inequality is not enough. For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, the triangle inequality and part (a) only give <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mo>&#x02248;</mo><mn>1.71</mn><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo></mrow></math>, which is weaker than the required <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mo>&#x02248;</mo><mn>1.41</mn><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>", "srcline": "<p>Lenstra, Lenstra and Lovász (1982), Proposition 1.6, (1.7): \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>\". Its proof: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>, and then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>. Regev, <em>Lattices in Computer Science</em>, Tel Aviv University, Fall 2009, Homework 2, Problem 4(b) (the case <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></math>) and Problem 4(d) (the general case) (https://cims.nyu.edu/~regev/teaching/lattices_fall_2009/homework2.pdf). Parts 4(a)-(f) are identical in the Fall 2004 Homework 2; only 4(g) differs. Galbraith v2.0, Lemma 17.2.8(2),(3), pp.370-371.</p>"}, {"part": "c", "difficulty": "easy", "summary": "Prove that the orthogonality defect of an LLL-reduced basis, the product of the basis lengths divided by det L, is at most 2^(n(n-1)/4).", "q": "<p><b>(c) (6 points)</b> Prove that</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msubsup><mo>&#x0220F;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>(The ratio <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mo>&#x0220F;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo></mrow><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> is called the <em>orthogonality defect</em> of the basis. It equals 1 when the basis is orthogonal, so this shows that an LLL-reduced basis is not far from orthogonal.)</p>", "basis": "<ul>\n<li><b>Lineage.</b> This is the right-hand inequality of LLL'82 (1.8), <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x0220F;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The same two-sided statement is Galbraith's Theorem 17.2.12(4), printed next to 17.2.12(5), which is 2024C-P5. It is also Regev's HW2 Problem 4(c), \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A0;</mi><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mi>&#x0039B;</mi></mrow></math>\", with his remark that this ratio is the orthogonality defect and that Hadamard's inequality makes it at least 1. The footprint research named (1.8) as the natural unused Moed C re-proof. Volk has not used it.</li>\n<li><b>Why it is chained to (a) and (b).</b> Given (b) and the class claim <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x0220F;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo></mrow></math>, part (c) takes three lines. Volk builds his parts so that one feeds the next: in 2026B-P5 part (c) says \"Use part (b) in order to prove the weak duality theorem\", and in the 2026B-P4(b) solution \"The first equality is exactly part (a)\". The proof of 2024C-P5 was also a product of per-index inequalities.</li>\n<li><b>Changed-parameter variant.</b> The remark after (1.9) in LLL'82 says that if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> is replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, then the powers of 2 are replaced by the same powers of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>. This is the kind of change Volk made in 2025C-P3, where he redid the lecture random-walk proof for a lazy walk. The variant is solved at the end of the model solution.</li>\n<li><b>Probability.</b> The same as part (a): 0.025 for this problem or a near variant, and 0.10 for any written lattice problem. If lattices come back only as a T/F item, the one-line facts used here are natural candidates: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo></mrow></math>, an orthogonality defect of at least 1, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> not depending on the basis.</li>\n</ul>", "sol": "<p>As we have seen in class (this is also the reminder), <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mo>&#x022EF;</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo></mrow></math>. The reason is that the matrix with columns <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> equals the matrix with columns <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> times an upper triangular matrix with 1 on the diagonal, and the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> are orthogonal.</p>\n<p>By part (b) with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></math>, after taking square roots (both sides are non-negative), <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. We multiply these <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> inequalities between non-negative numbers:</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msubsup><mo>&#x0220F;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x0220F;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><msubsup><mo>&#x0220F;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p><b>Remark (the other direction, in case it is asked).</b> As we have seen in class, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math>. This also follows from the identity at the start of the solution to (b). Hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x0220F;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x0220F;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo></mrow></math> for every basis of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>, reduced or not; this is Hadamard's inequality. So for an LLL-reduced basis,</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mo>&#x0220F;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo></mrow><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p><b>Variant to practise (the lecture lemma with a changed parameter).</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, and call the basis <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math>-reduced if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> for all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> for all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math>. Prove that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo></mrow></math> for every non-zero <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p><em>Solution.</em> Exactly as in part (a), <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msubsup><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003B1;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, we have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. By induction, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>min</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>. As we have seen in class, every non-zero <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> satisfies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msub><mo>min</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo></mrow></math> (the proof in class never used that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> is a shortest vector). Hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mi>v</mi><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>, that is, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo></mrow></math>. For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> we get <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, which is the bound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math> from class.</p>\n<p>The same substitution works in (a), (b) and (c); this is the remark after (1.9) in LLL'82. Part (a) becomes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>, with the same induction. In part (b), since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msup><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, the sum becomes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>, and</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msup><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#x003B1;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math></div>\n<p>because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, that is, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>&#x003B1;</mi></mrow></math>. So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>, hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></math>. Part (c) becomes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mo>&#x0220F;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>.</p>", "srcline": "<p>Regev, <em>Lattices in Computer Science</em> (TAU, Fall 2009; parts 4(a)-(f) are identical in Fall 2004), Homework 2, Problem 4(c): \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A0;</mi><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mi>&#x0039B;</mi></mrow></math>\", with the orthogonality-defect remark. It is also LLL'82 (1.8), the right-hand inequality; the left-hand inequality is Hadamard's inequality (1.10). Galbraith v2.0, Theorem 17.2.12(4), p.372: \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x0220F;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>\" (proof). The general-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> variant is the remark after (1.9): \"the powers of 2... must be replaced by the same powers of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>\".</p>"}]}]}, {"tier": 2, "open": false, "title": "True/false pool — 12 more statements in his style", "blurb": "Each statement is a one-line twist on a specific slide or textbook exercise, which is how 39 of his 40 past T/F items were built. Aim for a one-line justification under two minutes each.", "items": [{"id": "c26-tf-pool-lp", "sid": "T/F POOL · P1(a) LP", "difficulty": "medium", "src": "T/F pool for slot P1(a), 4 items x 7 pts: LP deck duality cases, simplex reduced costs, vertices, and the Approx deck Max-Cut IP; lineage hw2025 Set 4 P3(a)/2024C-P6/hw2026 s3-Q5, B&amp;T Thm 3.1(b)/2024A-1d/2025B-P3, 2024B-1b to 2026A-1a, 2026A-P5(b)", "stem": "<p><b>Problem 1 (4&times;7=28 points), candidate items for slot (a).</b> For each of the following statements, write whether they are true or false, and briefly explain your answer.</p><p>(Pool: each statement below is a stand-alone 7-point item. Throughout, for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>&#x0211D;</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>&#x0211D;</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>&#x0211D;</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math>, a linear program in standard form is minimize <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> subject to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, as in class.)</p>", "parts": [{"part": "a", "difficulty": "easy", "summary": "If the standard-form primal is infeasible then its dual is unbounded: false, since both can be infeasible.", "q": "<p>Let (P) and (D) be the following pair of linear programs, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>&#x0211D;</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>&#x0211D;</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>&#x0211D;</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math>:</p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>minimize</mtext></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>subject&#x000A0;to</mtext></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>maximize</mtext></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>subject&#x000A0;to</mtext></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></math></div><p>If (P) is infeasible, then (D) is unbounded.</p>", "basis": "<p><b>Lineage.</b> Volk's duality family: 2023B-1a (bounded non-empty primal implies equal optima, True), 2024A-1a (both feasible but the primal optimum strictly larger, False), then the free-response proof 2024C-P6 &lsquo;if (P) is infeasible and (D) is feasible, then (D) is unbounded&rsquo;. That problem was already Problem 3(a) of the homework Problem Set 4 filed in the corpus as hw2025 (its solution file is headed &lsquo;Spring Semester, 2024&rsquo), and the official 2024C solution repeats the homework solution almost word for word. It was then recycled as hw2026 Set 3 Q5(a) with the same (P)/(D) display. So the chain is homework &rarr; 2024C-P6 &rarr; hw2026. The T/F form of this family has not appeared since 2024A. This item is that proof statement with the hypothesis &lsquo;(D) is feasible&rsquo; dropped. Dropping a hypothesis is one of his standard T/F traps: 2024A-1b (PSD from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> alone, without the other principal minors), 2024C-1c (BPP without <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>&#x003B2;</mi></mrow></math>), 2026A-1b (Schwartz-Zippel without the product set).</p><p><b>Slide.</b> The LP deck's &lsquo;Strong Duality&rsquo; slide lists &lsquo;Easier cases: 1. (P) and (D) infeasible 2. (P) infeasible, (D) unbounded 3. (D) infeasible, (P) unbounded&rsquo;. The item merges cases 1 and 2: it claims that an infeasible (P) always falls into case 2. The T/F research's list of lecture claims that were never examined includes &lsquo;both primal and dual infeasible&rsquo;.</p><p><b>Slot.</b> Slot (a) was an LP item in 7 of Volk's 10 exams (exceptions 2025A and 2025B; 2025C put it in (d)), including both 2026 exams. The T/F answer key is about 3T/1F, and 4 of the 11 LP items were False.</p><p><b>Probability.</b> About 0.05 for this exact item. It is the strongest of the four because it has the longest lineage (slide, two homework sets, 2024C-P6). The four pool items together have about 0.165 (0.05 + 0.045 + 0.04 + 0.03). Adding the mock exam's ellipsoid item (about 0.06) gives about 0.225. These are probabilities that the item appears in the 2026C T/F block in any slot. Item (d) is at least as likely to sit in slot (d) as in slot (a), so the share of slot (a) itself is closer to 0.15 for the pool and 0.21 with the mock's item.</p><p><b>Variants for the same slot.</b> &lsquo;It is possible that both (P) and (D) are infeasible&rsquo;: True, with the same example. &lsquo;The dual of the dual is the primal&rsquo;: True, stated on the deck's &lsquo;Duality&rsquo; slide. Write (D) in standard form with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo></msup></mrow></math> and a slack <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math>. Its dual forces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, and the substitution <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></math> gives back (P).</p>", "sol": "<p><b>False.</b> It is possible that both (P) and (D) are infeasible (this is one of the &lsquo;easier cases&rsquo; of the strong duality theorem listed in class). For example, take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Then (P) requires <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so it is infeasible, and (D) requires <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so it is infeasible as well. In particular (D) is not unbounded: an unbounded program must have feasible solutions of arbitrarily large value.</p><p>(If one insists that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> have full row rank, take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x00028;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></math>: (P) forces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, and (D) contains the constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.)</p><p>The statement becomes true if we add the hypothesis that (D) is feasible, as we proved in the homework: by Farkas' lemma there is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, and for a feasible <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> of (D) the points <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, are feasible with objective value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mo>&#x0221E;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>", "srcline": "<p>LP deck, &lsquo;Strong Duality&rsquo; slide (LP lecture deck): &lsquo;Easier cases: 1. (P) and (D) infeasible 2. (P) infeasible, (D) unbounded 3. (D) infeasible, (P) unbounded&rsquo;. Goemans' LP notes (the deck's reading), p.LP-16: &lsquo;We should point out however that both the primal and the dual might be infeasible.&rsquo; The item drops the hypothesis &lsquo;(D) is feasible&rsquo; from 2024C Problem 6 (2024C official solution). The same proof is Problem 3(a) of hw2025 Problem Set 4 (2025 homework 4 solution: &lsquo;Let y be a feasible solution for (D). Since (P) is infeasible, by the version Farkas Lemma from Problem 1, there&rsquo;s y&prime; such that (y&prime)<sup>T</sup>A &ge; 0 and (y&prime)<sup>T</sup>b &lt; 0... hence (D) is unbounded&rsquo), and again hw2026 Set 3 Q5(a) (2026 homework 3 solution).</p>"}, {"part": "b", "difficulty": "medium", "summary": "A negative reduced cost at a basic feasible solution rules out optimality: false, because of degeneracy.", "q": "<p>Let (P) be a linear program in standard form, minimize <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> subject to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> be a basic feasible solution of (P) with a basis <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>, and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow></math> be the reduced cost vector corresponding to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> and the basis <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>. If some entry of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow></math> is strictly negative, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> is not an optimal solution of (P).</p>", "basis": "<p><b>Direct source (NEAR).</b> Bertsimas-Tsitsiklis Theorem 3.1(b), Section 3.1: &lsquo;If x is optimal and nondegenerate, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>&rsquo;. Its contrapositive reads: if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> is nondegenerate and some reduced cost is negative, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> is not optimal. The item is exactly this contrapositive with &lsquo;nondegenerate&rsquo; dropped, which is his drop-a-hypothesis trap (2024A-1b, 2024C-1c, 2026A-1b). B&amp;T is already a verified source for this family: 2024A-1d is B&amp;T Ex 3.6(a), and 2023A-1a is close to B&amp;T Ex 3.18(a).</p><p><b>Lineage.</b> The reduced-cost family: 2024A-1d (&lsquo;if in the vector <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow></math> all entries are strictly positive, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> is the unique optimal solution&rsquo;, True; this is Bertsimas-Tsitsiklis Ex 3.6(a)) and 2025B-P3 (prove that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> implies optimality, 16 points). The remaining natural item is the converse direction. The converse is false without non-degeneracy, which is exactly why B&amp;T Ex 3.6(b) carries the hypothesis &lsquo;and is nondegenerate&rsquo;. Degeneracy was already his trap in 2023A-1a (can the simplex move to a new vertex at equal cost?), an item traced to B&amp;T Ex 3.18(a) and to Goemans p.LP-8. A closer ancestor is the homework: hw2025 Problem Set 4, Problem 2 proves that Bland&rsquo;s rule cannot cycle. In a cycle a variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math> enters the basis with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> while the &lsquo;fickle&rsquo; variables &lsquo;remain 0 throughout the process&rsquo;, so the pivot does not move the vertex or lower the cost. That is the same phenomenon as this item.</p><p><b>Slide.</b> The simplex slide says &lsquo;If there's <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> such that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, we can reduce the cost by increasing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>&rsquo;. The deck assumes non-degeneracy only afterwards (&lsquo;Suppose <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>. We say <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> is non-degenerate&rsquo) and names the failure on the &lsquo;Issues with the Simplex Method&rsquo; slide: &lsquo;In this case we can't increase <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>. We call such solutions degenerate&rsquo;. That is a slide claim with a hidden hypothesis, the kind of claim Volk turns into a False item (see 2026B-1a, feasible centre assumed optimal).</p><p><b>Probability.</b> About 0.045. The wording copies 2024A-1d (&lsquo;a basic feasible solution with a basis <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>&rsquo;, &lsquo;the reduced cost vector corresponding to&rsquo), so it is cheap for him to write. Here &lsquo;and the basis <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>&rsquo; is added, because a degenerate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> has several bases with different vectors <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow></math>, and the item must say which one is meant. It is slightly below item (a) because 2025B-P3 already covered this slide in 2025.</p>", "sol": "<p><b>False.</b> As we saw in class, every feasible <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> satisfies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msubsup><mover><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>N</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> means the cost goes down if we increase <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>. But if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> is degenerate (some basic variable is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>), it may be impossible to increase <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> at all while staying feasible.</p><p>Counterexample: minimize <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> subject to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. The only feasible point is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> is optimal. With the basis <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> we have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> is a basic feasible solution for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>, and</p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><p>Note that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow></math> depends on the basis <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> and not only on the vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math>. For the other basis <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> of the same vertex, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, which certifies optimality. What we saw in class is only that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> implies optimality, not the converse.</p><p>The statement is true when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> is non-degenerate. If no entry <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is positive, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> can grow without bound and (P) is unbounded, as we saw in class. Otherwise the step size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>min</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>:</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is strictly positive, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Increasing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math> gives a feasible point of cost <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>z</mi></mrow></math>.</p>", "srcline": "<p>LP deck, simplex-method slide (LP lecture deck): &lsquo;If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> is an optimal solution!... If there's <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> such that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, we can reduce the cost by increasing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>&rsquo;. Non-degeneracy assumption: &lsquo;Suppose <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>. We say <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> is non-degenerate&rsquo;. &lsquo;Issues with the Simplex Method&rsquo; slide: &lsquo;What if there's <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> that equals 0? In this case we can't increase <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>. We call such solutions degenerate&rsquo;. Bertsimas &amp; Tsitsiklis, <em>Introduction to Linear Optimization</em>, Section 3.9, Exercise 3.6 : &lsquo;If x is the unique optimal solution and is nondegenerate, then the reduced cost of every nonbasic variable is positive&rsquo;. Its part (a) was 2024A-1d. B&amp;T Section 3.1 &lsquo;Optimality conditions&rsquo;, Theorem 3.1 (grep &lsquo;Theorem 3.1 Consider&rsquo;; the OCR drops the bar and the &ge; sign): &lsquo;Theorem 3.1 Consider a basic feasible solution x associated with a basis matrix B, and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover></mrow></math> be the corresponding vector of reduced costs. (a) If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, then x is optimal. (b) If x is optimal and nondegenerate, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.&rsquo; The item is the contrapositive of (b) without &lsquo;nondegenerate&rsquo;. Homework ancestor: hw2025 Problem Set 4, Problem 2, Bland&rsquo;s rule (2025 homework 4 solution: &lsquo;since s enters the basis, by the description of the simplex algorithm, c&#771;<sub>s</sub> &lt; 0&rsquo;;: &lsquo;the fickle variables remain 0 throughout the process&rsquo).</p>"}, {"part": "c", "difficulty": "easy", "summary": "Every non-empty polyhedron given by inequalities has a vertex: false, a strip in the plane has none.", "q": "<p>Every non-empty polyhedron <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>&#x0211D;</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mi>:</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> has at least one vertex.</p>", "basis": "<p><b>Lineage.</b> The vertex/BFS chain is Volk's most regular T/F lineage: 2024B-1b (decide whether <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is a vertex, True), 2024C-1a (at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math> vertices, True), 2025C-1d (&lsquo;x is optimal iff x is a vertex&rsquo;, False), 2026A-1a (a BFS stays a BFS under a new objective, True). That is 4 of the 7 exams from 2024B to 2026B. Also, every later sitting within a year reused at least one T/F sub-topic of the previous sitting (6 of 6), and BFS (2026A-1a) is one of the 2026 sub-topics available for reuse. 2025C-1d was an over-generalization of &lsquo;some optimum is a vertex&rsquo;. This item over-generalizes the standard-form corollary in the same way (the same trap as 2026B-1a).</p><p><b>Slide.</b> The deck's &lsquo;Vertices&rsquo; slide states the counterexample outright: &lsquo;Not every polyhedron has a vertex. E.g., <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>:</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>&rsquo;. Goemans' notes, the course reading that already fed 2023A-1a, 2023B-1b and 2024C-1a, have the same remark on p.LP-5. &lsquo;Polyhedra without vertices&rsquo; is on the research's list of lecture claims that were never examined.</p><p><b>Probability.</b> About 0.04. The lineage is strong, but 2026A-1a has just used this chain, and the answer can be read straight off one slide.</p><p><b>Variant for the same slot (ellipsoid, from the same deck).</b> &lsquo;In the ellipsoid algorithm, the volume of the ellipsoid at least halves in every iteration&rsquo;: False. Class shows that the ratio of volumes in one step is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, so class only guarantees a drop by a factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> steps (LP deck: &lsquo;After 2(n + 1) steps: volume decreased by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>&rsquo). For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> the ratio is exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac><msqrt><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x02248;</mo><mn>0.77</mn><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, and it increases towards <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> grows. Keep this only if the mock's ellipsoid item is replaced, since one ellipsoid item is enough.</p>", "sol": "<p><b>False.</b> Take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>&#x0211D;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>:</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, that is, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x00028;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></math>. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi></mrow></math> is non-empty. For every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></math>, the vector <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> satisfies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></math>, so by the definition from class <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> is not a vertex. Equivalently, every row of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> is a multiple of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, so no point has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> linearly independent tight constraints.</p><p>What we saw in class holds for standard form: if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo></mrow></math>, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi></mrow></math> has a vertex. To see this, apply the theorem &lsquo;if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is finite, then for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></math> there is a vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>&rsquo; with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. The minimum is then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, which is finite because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo></mrow></math>. A standard-form polyhedron cannot contain a line because of the constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p>", "srcline": "<p>LP deck, &lsquo;Vertices&rsquo; slide (LP lecture deck): &lsquo;Not every polyhedron has a vertex. E.g., <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>:</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>. However, we will prove that if an LP in standard form has an optimal solution, then it is obtained by a vertex&rsquo;. Goemans, LP lecture notes, p.LP-5 : &lsquo;such a set P might not have any vertex. For example, consider <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>:</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>&rsquo; and &lsquo;Corollary 3: If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo></mrow></math>, then P has a vertex.&rsquo;</p>"}, {"part": "d", "difficulty": "medium", "summary": "An efficient algorithm can output, from G, an integer linear program whose optimum is the max-cut size: true.", "q": "<p>There exists an efficient algorithm that, given an undirected graph <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, outputs an integer linear program (that is, a linear objective function, linear equality and inequality constraints, and the constraint that all variables are integers) whose optimal value equals the size of a maximum cut in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math>.</p>", "basis": "<p><b>Slide.</b> The Approximation deck introduces Max-Cut with an IP whose key constraint is &lsquo;Not a linear constraint! Recall that for min-cut this was possible (dual of max flow). Weird.&rsquo; A student who remembers only that line answers False. The item tests whether you can linearize it (the item asks for an efficient algorithm that outputs the program; without that, &lsquo;maximize <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> subject to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> integer&rsquo; with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> the max-cut size would make it trivially true), which is the move used in 2026A-P5(b) (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> for directed cut). The undirected constraints are exactly those in Williamson-Shmoys Ex 4.7(b), and Volk lifted 2026A-P5 from Williamson-Shmoys Ex 5.3 and 5.6.</p><p><b>Lineage.</b> 2024B-1a (a polynomial-time ILP solver implies P=NP, True) is the other ILP-expressiveness item. Writing an ILP from scratch appeared in 5 of the 8 exams from 2023A to 2025C and was skipped in 2026A/B apart from the given DiCut program. A T/F item is a cheap way to bring it back.</p><p><b>Probability.</b> About 0.03, the lowest of the four. The Max-Cut material belongs to the approximation unit, so it is at least as likely to sit in slot (d). 2026A-P5 has also just spent the linearization trick. It is the pool's only True item. 29 of the 40 past T/F items were True, but slot (a) was True in only 6 of 10 exams, so the slot leans only mildly towards True.</p><p><b>Variant for the same slot (integrality).</b> &lsquo;If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> has only integral vertices for every integral <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow></math>, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> is totally unimodular&rsquo;: False. Take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x00028;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mn>2</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x00028;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>2</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is integral, so the polyhedron is empty or the single integral point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>b</mi></mrow></math>, but the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> submatrix <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> has determinant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. This is the converse of the deck's TUM claim.</p>", "sol": "<p><b>True.</b> The constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x02295;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> we wrote in class is not linear. We rename the variables: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> for every vertex (instead of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math>) and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> for every edge (instead of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>). Since we maximize, it suffices to bound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> from above by linear constraints. The algorithm outputs the following program:</p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>maximize</mtext></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\" /></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>subject&#x000A0;to</mtext></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>for&#x000A0;every&#x000A0;</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\" /><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>for&#x000A0;every&#x000A0;</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\" /><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>for&#x000A0;every&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\" /><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>for&#x000A0;every&#x000A0;</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></math></div><p>If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, the first constraint forces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the second forces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x02260;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math>, both constraints only say <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Given a cut <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mi>&#x029F5;</mi><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> iff <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> iff <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> crosses the cut. This is feasible with value equal to the size of the cut. Conversely, for a feasible <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>v</mi><mi>:</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>. Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> only for edges crossing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mi>&#x029F5;</mi><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, so the value is at most the size of that cut. Hence the optimum is exactly the size of a maximum cut. This is the same argument as for the directed-cut program in 2026A Problem 5(b).</p><p>Each constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> stands for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> together with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> integer, and the same for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The program has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> variables and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> constraints besides the bounds, and all its numbers are small constants (coefficients <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x000B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, right-hand sides <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>). So the algorithm writes it down in polynomial time (in time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with a sparse encoding of the constraints), without knowing the value of the maximum cut. (The word &lsquo;efficient&rsquo; matters: the program &lsquo;maximize <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> subject to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> integer&rsquo; also has optimum <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>, but writing it requires computing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>, i.e. solving Max-Cut, which is NP-hard.)</p>", "srcline": "<p>Approximation deck, &lsquo;Max Cut&rsquo; IP slide (Approximation lecture deck): &lsquo;Variables: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> for every edge, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> for every vertex... <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x02295;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math>... Not a linear constraint! Recall that for min-cut this was possible (dual of max flow). Weird.&rsquo; The linear form is Williamson &amp; Shmoys, Exercise 4.7(b), p.103 : &lsquo;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math>&rsquo;. Directed sibling: 2026A Problem 5(b) = W&amp;S Ex 5.6(a) (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>).</p>"}]}, {"id": "c26-tf-pool-rand", "sid": "T/F POOL · randomized", "difficulty": "medium", "src": "T/F pool for P1(b)/(d), 7 pts each: BPP with a 1/poly gap (2023A-1c, 2024C-1c; Motwani-Raghavan Problem 4.8), isolation for the maximum (2025C-1c, 2026B-1b; Jukna Ex 11.4), the matching determinant (deck slide; 2024C-1d), random walks on bipartite graphs (2025B-1d; deck lazy walk)", "stem": "<p><b>Problem 1</b> (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>28</mn></mrow></math> points). For each of the following statements, write whether they are true or false, and briefly explain your answer.</p><p><em>This is a pool: each statement is a candidate for one slot of Problem 1, most likely (b) or (d). A real paper would carry at most one or two of them.</em></p>", "parts": [{"part": "a", "difficulty": "medium", "summary": "True: a BPP algorithm whose two acceptance thresholds differ by only 1/(n+1) can be amplified to error 1/3 by a majority vote over polynomially many runs.", "q": "<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mrow></math> (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> is the input length).</p>", "sol": "<p><b>True.</b> Write <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, so the class on the left is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Its thresholds satisfy <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p><em>The inclusion <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02287;</mo></mrow></math>.</em> For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> we have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>. So an algorithm that shows <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> also meets the new thresholds on every input of length at least 2. The finitely many inputs of length at most 1 are answered from a fixed table, with no error. Hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p><p><em>The inclusion <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02286;</mo></mrow></math>.</em> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> via a polynomial time algorithm <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math>. The algorithm <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math> runs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> times, each time with fresh independent random coins. It accepts iff more than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> of the runs accept (a majority vote, as in class). Fix an input <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>M</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;accepts&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> be an indicator random variable for the event that the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math>-th run accepts, and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> are i.i.d. with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></math>, so by the Chernoff bound we saw in class<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p><ul><li>If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math> errs only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>.</li><li>If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math> errs only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>.</li></ul><p>In both cases the error event implies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so the error probability is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0.28</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math> makes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> calls to a polynomial time algorithm, so it runs in polynomial time, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p><p>(Taking <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></math> instead gives error at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, which is Motwani-Raghavan Problem 4.8. What matters is that the gap <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> is at least an inverse polynomial. With gap 0, as in 2024C-1c, the claim is false.)</p>", "basis": "<p>BPP amplification is a two-item Randomized T/F chain, and its answer flipped once: 2023A-1c (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, True), then 2024C-1c (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, False, the gap is dropped). It has not been asked in the five sittings since (2025A, 2025B, 2025C, 2026A and 2026B; a grep for BPP over the exam corpus finds only 2023A and 2024C), and its last use was a Moed C. The obvious next link lies between the two: a gap that shrinks with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> but stays inverse-polynomial. This is exactly Motwani-Raghavan Problem 4.8, and Motwani-Raghavan is the course textbook for the Randomized unit. The T/F analysis names 'BPP amplification (unused since 2024C)' as a slot (b) candidate and lists <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, another inverse-polynomial-gap equality, among its most likely reused twists. The statement copies the form of 2023A-1c, including '(<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> is the input length)'. It rests on the deck's 'Error reduction' slides (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, majority vote) and on the class Chernoff bound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>p</mi><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, so checking the constant is a one-line computation. The offset from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> is written as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> rather than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> the lower threshold is negative at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> is undefined for the empty input (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>). With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> the thresholds meet the class condition <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> (they are 0 and 1 at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>).</p><p><b>Probability:</b> about 0.07 for this item or a variant with the same idea (an inverse-polynomial gap that shrinks with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>), the highest in this pool. The figure is a product of two estimates. (1) A BPP item in the 2026C T/F block: about 0.2. The Moed C analysis puts 'BPP amplification' at about 0.35 as a C-flavoured candidate, but that figure rests on 2024C alone. Against it: BPP was a T/F item in 2 of the 10 Volk papers and in 1 of the 2 Moed C's (2025C took isolation instead); the July 2026 prediction that BPP was 'due back' missed in both 2026A and 2026B (format scorecard); the 2026 homework dropped the RP/ZPP/BPP exercise that hw2025 set 1 had; and the same-year reuse rule pulls the Randomized T/F slot toward isolation (2026B-1b) and Schwartz-Zippel (2026A-1b). (2) Given a BPP item, the twist is an inverse-polynomial gap: about 0.35. The two earlier items covered a constant gap and a zero gap, so this is the one untested case; the alternatives are another constant pair or a one-sided RP/ZPP claim. So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.2</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mn>0.35</mn><mo>&#x02248;</mo><mn>0.07</mn></mrow></math>. About 0.18 that at least one of the four pool items appears in 2026C: they compete for the same one or two Randomized slots, so the four estimates roughly add.</p><p><b>Variants for the same slot</b> (Randomized-deck claims never yet asked as T/F):</p><ul><li>'Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> where the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> are only pairwise independent with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></math>. Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>p</mi><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>p</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>.' True: the variance computation from class uses only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>, which needs only pairwise independence (the deck says 'So far we only used pairwise independence').</li><li>'The cycle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>) has exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> minimum cuts.' True: the minimum cut has 2 edges, and every pair of edges splits the cycle into two arcs, giving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> distinct minimum cuts. So Karger's bound is tight (Zwick PS2 Ex 2.3, hw2026 set 1 problem 3).</li></ul>", "srcline": "<p>Randomized lecture deck, 'Error reduction' and 'Error Reduction, Two-sided Case' slides (corpus) and the 'Chernoff Bound' slide. Motwani &amp; Raghavan, <em>Randomized Algorithms</em>, Problem 4.8 (Chapter 4 problems, p.98): 'Consider a BPP algorithm that has an error probability of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>... Using the Chernoff bound... show that a polynomial number of independent repetitions of this algorithm suffice to reduce the error probability to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math>.' Re-parameterizes 2023A-1c and 2024C-1c.</p>"}, {"part": "b", "difficulty": "easy", "summary": "True: the isolation lemma also isolates a unique maximum-weight set with probability at least one half when the weights come from 1 to 2n.", "q": "<p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x02131;</mi></mrow></math> be a nonempty family of subsets of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> be a weight function chosen at random, uniformly and independently for each element. The weight of a set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Then with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, there is a unique set in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x02131;</mi></mrow></math> of maximum weight.</p>", "sol": "<p><b>True.</b> This is the isolation lemma for the maximum. As remarked in class, the lemma also holds for the maximum weight. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, the probability that the maximum-weight set is not unique is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>.</p><p>The proof from class carries over. For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> define<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msup><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x02131;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x02131;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>S</mi><mi>&#x029F5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div>and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> if one of the two maxima is over an empty family. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> depends only on the weights of elements other than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>. Now suppose <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> both have the maximum weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math>. Weights are positive, so neither set contains the other, and there is some <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>&#x029F5;</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>. The first maximum is then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> and the second is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. By a union bound over the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> elements, the maximum is not unique with probability at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>.</p><p>(Applying the lemma to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></math> does not work directly: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and the sets in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x02131;</mi></mrow></math> may have different sizes. Compare 2026B-1b, with the same <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x02131;</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow></math>: the lemma isolates the extreme set. It does not make all set weights distinct.)</p>", "basis": "<p>Same-year reuse: every later sitting has reused at least one T/F sub-topic from the previous sitting of the same year (6 out of 6). Isolation was 2026B-1b (same <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x02131;</mi></mrow></math>, same random <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow></math> into <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, claim 'all subset sums distinct', False). Its official solution already names the true statement: 'the isolation lemma only guarantees that the minimum weight set is unique with high probability'. The exact ancestor is a Moed C item, 2025C-1c: edge weights from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, a unique minimum cut and a unique maximum cut with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, True. Its official solution uses the maximum version of the lemma. There are two sources. One is the deck's remark 'Same holds with maximum weight', right under the lemma. The other is Jukna Exercise 11.4 ('With what probability will there be a unique set with the maximal weight?'), next to Exercise 11.5 (product weights), which matches 2023A-1b. The isolation family so far has three T/F items, 2023A-1b (True), 2025C-1c (True) and 2026B-1b (False), so the chain went T, T, then F. It also has one free-response problem, 2024A-P3 (random edge weights from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> give a unique minimum weight path between every pair of vertices with probability at least 0.9). This item keeps the parameters of 2026B-1b word for word and asks for the uniqueness of the extreme set, with the maximum twist of 2025C-1c. The case for it is the same-year reuse rule, not the answer pattern of the chain.</p><p><b>Probability:</b> about 0.04, discounted because it nearly repeats 2025C-1c.</p>", "srcline": "<p>Randomized deck, Isolation Lemma slide, 'Remarks: 1. Only meaningful when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> 2. Same holds with maximum weight' (corpus), proof on. Jukna, <em>Extremal Combinatorics</em>, 2nd ed., Exercise 11.4, p.163. Re-skins 2026B-1b (same <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x02131;</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>) with the answer of 2025C-1c.</p>"}, {"part": "c", "difficulty": "easy", "summary": "True: if a bipartite graph has exactly one perfect matching, its 0/1 biadjacency matrix has determinant plus or minus one.", "q": "<p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> be a bipartite graph with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> that has exactly one perfect matching. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math> be its biadjacency matrix: rows are indexed by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>, columns by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> otherwise. Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p>", "sol": "<p><b>True.</b> By definition<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><msubsup><mo>&#x0220F;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>As we have seen in class, the term of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C3;</mi></mrow></math> is nonzero iff <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math>, that is, iff <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>:</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is a perfect matching. In that case the product equals 1. So the permutations with a nonzero term correspond one-to-one to the perfect matchings of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math>. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> has exactly one perfect matching, so exactly one term of the sum is nonzero, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x000B1;</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p>(The hypothesis 'exactly one' matters. 'If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> has a perfect matching then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>' is false: the class counterexample <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x00028;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> has two perfect matchings whose terms cancel. More generally, since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02261;</mo><mn>1</mn><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mo>&#x00028;</mo><mi>mod</mi><mspace width=\"0.333em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> has the same parity as the number of perfect matchings of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> (this is 2024C-1d). So an odd number of perfect matchings already forces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.)</p>", "basis": "<p>This comes straight from a slide. The deck's 'Matchings and Determinants' slide asks 'Is the converse also true? Perfect Matching <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x021D2;</mo></mrow></math> non-zero det? No. Counterexample <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x00028;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>', and blames the signs: 'the + and - can cancel out'. Volk usually builds a T/F by changing one hypothesis of a slide claim (dropping a hypothesis, swapping a constant, overgeneralizing). Here the True version adds the hypothesis that removes the cancellation (a unique matching), and the False version asks the bare converse. The family has been examined once, and it was a Moed C item: 2024C-1d (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>mod</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> decides the parity of the number of perfect matchings, True). Recitation 2 repeats the claim that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> implies a perfect matching. Neither 2026 exam touched matchings or had a PIT free-response problem (PIT appeared only as the Schwartz-Zippel T/F 2026A-1b), although PIT free-response appeared in every Moed A from 2023 to 2025 and in 2025C. 2026B-P2 did use the same bipartite biadjacency matrix (rows indexed by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>, columns by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow></math>), but to count common neighbours, and the statement above copies the definition from its official solution. So the matching unit is due, and a T/F is the cheapest way to cover it.</p><p><b>Probability:</b> about 0.035. The True version (unique matching) and the False version ('has a perfect matching') are about equally likely.</p>", "srcline": "<p>Randomized deck, 'Matchings and Determinants' slides: 'Claim: if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> doesn't have a perfect matching, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>... Is the converse also true? Perfect Matching <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x021D2;</mo></mrow></math> non-zero det? No. Counterexample... The problem is with the signs' (corpus). Recitation 2, 'Perfect Matching on Bipartite Graph'. Sibling of 2024C-1d.</p>"}, {"part": "d", "difficulty": "medium", "summary": "False: on a connected regular bipartite graph such as the 4-cycle the non-lazy random walk alternates between the two sides and never converges to the uniform distribution.", "q": "<p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> be an undirected connected <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>-regular graph on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> vertices with normalized adjacency matrix <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>. For a vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math>, let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>&#x0211D;</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math> be the vector which is 1 in the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math>-th coordinate and 0 elsewhere. Then for every vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math>, the vectors <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> converge to the uniform distribution <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mo>&#x0221E;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>", "sol": "<p><b>False.</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> be the 4-cycle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></math> with vertices <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math> and edges <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, which is connected and 2-regular. Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, so<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mi>k</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;odd</mtext><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>2</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;even</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div>In both cases <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x02016;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x02016;</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, so the sequence does not converge: the walk alternates between the two sides of the bipartition.</p><p>In spectral terms: as we have seen in class, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> is bipartite iff <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The component of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> along the eigenvector <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is multiplied by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></math> and never decays. For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mi>&#x027E9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>. This is why in class we worked with the lazy walk <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>. Its eigenvalues <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> lie in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> is connected, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> does converge to uniform. (The statement is true for connected regular graphs that are not bipartite.)</p>", "basis": "<p>Spectral and random-walk facts have filled slot (d) three times: 2023B-1d (all nontrivial eigenvalues at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> in absolute value, so a walk reaches <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> within <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> steps, True), 2025A-1d (the eigenspace of 1 is one-dimensional for connected <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math>, True) and 2025B-1d (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x021D2;</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> bipartite, True). The topic was then promoted to a free-response problem in 2025C-P3 (a lazy walk with stay probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>). Random walks are absent from both 2026 exams, though hw2026 set 2 Question 1 kept the spectral facts about the lazy walk in play. The deck says only 'For technical reasons: we'll work with the lazy random walk matrix'. The reason is the bipartite fact on the next slide ('<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> bipartite iff <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>'). A T/F that drops the laziness is Volk's usual 'drop a hypothesis' trap (2024C-1c, 2026A-1b). It would also be the first False item in the spectral family.</p><p><b>Probability:</b> about 0.035.</p><p><b>Variants in the same family:</b></p><ul><li>'Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> be an undirected connected <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>-regular graph of diameter at most 3, and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> be the second largest eigenvalue of its lazy random walk matrix <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>. Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow></math>.' False. This is lifted from a recitation slide: recitation 4 states the converse of its Claim 2 ('If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow></math> then the diameter of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>') as false, 'diameter of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x021CF;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003F5;</mi></mrow></math>', with two cliques of size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> joined by 2 edges, 'regularized by deleting an edge from each clique', of diameter 3 and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> (corpus; Claim 2 with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow></math> follows on). Concretely, take two copies of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>6</mn></msub></mrow></math>, delete the edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> from the first and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> from the second, and add <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>. The graph has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></math>, is 5-regular and has diameter 3. The vector <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> that is 1 on the first clique and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> on the second is orthogonal to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mn>28</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>13</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>15</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>13</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>15</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>14</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>15</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow></math> (numerically <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02248;</mo><mn>0.946</mn></mrow></math>).</li><li>'In every strongly connected directed graph, a random walk from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math> reaches <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> within <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></math> steps with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.' False (the deck: 'This doesn't work for directed graphs'). Take edges <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. Each attempt from 1 reaches <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, and there are at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></math> attempts, so the probability is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li></ul>", "srcline": "<p>Randomized deck, 'Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of A' ('For technical reasons: we'll work with the lazy random walk matrix <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>', corpus) and 'Spectral Graph Theory' ('<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> bipartite iff <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>'). Sibling of 2025B-1d. The Moed C precedent for the lazy walk is 2025C-P3.</p>"}]}, {"id": "c26-tf-pool-alg-apx", "sid": "T/F POOL · P1(c)/(d)", "difficulty": "medium", "src": "T/F pool, 4 items x 7 pts for slots P1(c)/(d): Algebraic deck (Newton identities; Bounding omega) and Approximation deck (Gram-Schmidt lower bound; PSD). Lineage: 2026B-1c, 2023A-1d/2025C-1b, 2026B-1d, 2024A-1b.", "stem": "<p><b>Problem 1-style items (7 points each).</b> For each of the following statements, write whether it is true or false, and briefly explain your answer. You may use any claim presented in class or proved in the homework.</p><p><em>Pool for slots (c) (algebraic) and (d) (linear algebra from the Approximation deck); on the real paper at most one or two of these would appear.</em></p>", "parts": [{"part": "a", "difficulty": "easy", "summary": "True or false: two real matrices whose powers up to n have equal traces are similar.", "q": "<p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>&#x0211D;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> are similar (that is, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>P</mi><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math> for some invertible matrix <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi></mrow></math>).</p>", "basis": "<p><b>Same-year reuse.</b> Characteristic polynomial / traces / Newton's identities is the only sub-topic asked as a T/F item in <em>both</em> 2026 sittings: 2026A-1c (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with fewer than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></math> operations, True) and 2026B-1c (equal traces of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, give the same eigenvalues, True). The chain is 2024B-1d, then 2026A-1c, then 2026B-1c. Every later sitting has reused at least one T/F sub-topic of the sitting before it in the same year, 6 times out of 6 (2023A to B, 2024A to B, 2024B to C, 2025A to B, 2025B to C, 2026A to B), so a 2026B topic is very likely to come back in 2026C.</p><p><b>The twist.</b> Changing one word of 2026B-1c ('same eigenvalues' to 'similar') is the cheapest False version. It is Volk's usual move of overgeneralizing a slide claim (2026B-1a 'feasible' to 'optimal', 2025C-1d 'some optimum is a vertex' to 'iff'). The official 2026B-1c solution is a one-line appeal to Newton's identities, so students who memorised it will answer True.</p><p><b>Source.</b> Algebraic deck, slides 'Power sum polynomials' and 'Newton's identities'. Horn and Johnson 2.4.P10 states the fact exactly: equal traces of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> give the same characteristic polynomial, and nothing more.</p><p><b>Probability</b> that this item (or its <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x1D53D;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> variant) appears: about 0.05. Slot (c) is algebraic in about 80% of papers.</p><p><b>Algebraic-slot variants in the same pool:</b></p><ul><li>'For every invertible <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>&#x0211D;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math> is a polynomial in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> of degree at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.' True: by Cayley-Hamilton (the deck's 'Matrix Inversion' slide), <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo>&#x022EF;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li><li>'<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> can be computed with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> arithmetic operations.' True: it equals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> needs only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> operations.</li><li>'A graph <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> contains a cycle of length 4 iff <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.' False: every edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> gives the closed walk <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> of length 4 (Zwick's slides count closed walks, not cycles).</li><li>Seidel: 'If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math> has an edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> iff <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x02308;</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x02309;</mi></mrow></math>.' True.</li></ul>", "sol": "<p><b>False.</b> As we have seen in class, Newton's identities let us compute the coefficients of the characteristic polynomial from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. So the hypothesis does imply that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> have the same characteristic polynomial, and hence the same eigenvalues with the same algebraic multiplicities (this was 2026B-1c). But two matrices with the same eigenvalues need not be similar. Take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> and</p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x00028;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x00028;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><p>Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. However, for every invertible <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi></mrow></math> we have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mi>B</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> are not similar.</p><p><em>Remarks.</em> (1) The conclusion does hold when both matrices are known to be diagonalizable (for example, symmetric): then both are similar to the same diagonal matrix <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. (2) Over a field of characteristic <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> even the eigenvalue claim fails, because Newton's identities divide by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>: over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x1D53D;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, but their characteristic polynomials are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02260;</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>.</p>", "srcline": "Algebraic deck, 'Power sum polynomials' and 'Newton's identities' slides (Algebraic lecture deck). Horn and Johnson, <em>Matrix Analysis</em>, Problem 2.4.P10: 'Show that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> have the same characteristic polynomials, and hence the same eigenvalues, if and only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></math> for all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>.' One-word twist of 2026B-1c."}, {"part": "b", "difficulty": "medium", "summary": "True or false: applying a 48-multiplication algorithm for 4 by 4 matrices recursively beats Strassen's exponent.", "q": "<p>Suppose there is an algorithm that multiplies two <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math> matrices over any ring (not necessarily commutative) using <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>48</mn></mrow></math> multiplications (and any number of additions and subtractions). Then applying this algorithm recursively to the decomposition of the input matrices into <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math> blocks gives an algorithm that multiplies two <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> real matrices using <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003B2;</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> arithmetic operations, for some constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>7</mn></mrow></math>.</p>", "basis": "<p><b>Lineage.</b> The matrix-multiplication count family ran in three sittings in a row: 2025A-1c (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> products <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> need time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></math>, False), 2025B-1b (an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> product in fewer than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></math> operations, True) and 2025C-1b (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>, True). It goes back to 2023A-1d ('<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math> with at most 49 multiplications', True) and skipped both 2026 sittings. A family that has rested for one year and matches a slot filled by algebra about 80% of the time is a reasonable return candidate for Moed C.</p><p><b>Source.</b> Algebraic deck, slide 9 'Bounding <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></math>': 'If we can multiply <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> matrices with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> multiplications, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>'. This claim has never been a T/F item (the T/F analysis lists it among the unexamined lecture claims). Slide 10 'Known bounds on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></math>' is an image that plots the history of the upper bound, from Strassen's 2.81 down below 2.4 with Coppersmith-Winograd (1990) and on to Alman-Williams and later work. Slide 11 mentions the Google search for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></math> algorithms ('They did not lower <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></math>') without giving a count. That gap invites a concrete number. 48 is the natural number to choose: it is one less than the 49 of 2023A-1d, and the comparison needs no calculator, only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mn>48</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mn>49</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>7</mn></mrow></math>. Volk's Hebrew notes give the same bound (Thm 3.6 <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>7</mn></mrow></math>; Thm 5.8: if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k</mi></mrow><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>t</mi><mi>&#x027E9;</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></math> then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>log</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mi>r</mi></mrow></math>, which for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>48</mn></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mn>48</mn></mrow></math>).</p><p><b>Why the conclusion names the recursive algorithm.</b> Because slide 10 shows <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>2.38</mn></mrow></math>, a conclusion such as 'then there is an algorithm that multiplies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> matrices in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003B2;</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> for some <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>7</mn></mrow></math>', or 'then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></math>' for any <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>2.372</mn></mrow></math>, is true without the hypothesis, and 'True, since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>2.38</mn></mrow></math>' would be a complete answer that never uses the 48. Volk's own solutions use the known bounds on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></math> in exactly this way (2025A-1c: 'We've seen in class how to do it using fast matrix multiplication in time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, regardless of the assumption'; 2025B-1b: '<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math> which is less than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></math>'). So the statement asks what the algorithm built from the hypothesis achieves. The answer then depends only on comparing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mn>48</mn></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>7</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p><b>Probability:</b> about 0.045, counting the False twin below.</p><p><b>Variants:</b></p><ul><li>False twin: 'Suppose there is an algorithm that multiplies two <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> matrices over any ring using <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>23</mn></mrow></math> multiplications. Then applying this algorithm recursively to the decomposition of the input matrices into <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> blocks gives an algorithm that multiplies two <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> real matrices using <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003B2;</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> arithmetic operations, for some constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>7</mn></mrow></math>.' False: for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>3</mn><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow></math> the recursive algorithm performs exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>23</mn><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mn>23</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math> scalar multiplications, so it is not <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003B2;</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> for any <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mn>23</mn></mrow></math>. And <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mn>23</mn><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>17</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>7</mn></mrow></math>, since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>23</mn><mn>6</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>148035889</mn><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>129140163</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>3</mn><mrow><mn>17</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>7</mn><mn>6</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>117649</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>131072</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>17</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math> (numerically <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mn>23</mn><mo>&#x02248;</mo><mn>2.854</mn><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>2.807</mn><mo>&#x02248;</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>7</mn></mrow></math>). The hypothesis actually holds (Laderman 1976; Bl&auml;ser's survey: '<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>19</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>&#x027E9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>23</mn></mrow></math>... (We would need <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>21</mn></mrow></math> to get an improvement.)'), which makes the trap sharper. The conclusion has to name the recursive algorithm: 'then there is an algorithm with exponent below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>7</mn></mrow></math>' would be True by slide 10.</li><li>DPV Exercise 2.27(b), as a T/F item about the specific algorithm: 'Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mo>&#x00028;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mi>a</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>b</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>c</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>d</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x00028;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>c</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mtd><mtd><mi>b</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> matrix can be squared with 5 multiplications. Consider the following algorithm for squaring an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> matrix: use a divide-and-conquer approach as in Strassen's algorithm, except that instead of getting 7 subproblems of size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, we now get 5 subproblems of size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> thanks to this formula. This algorithm correctly computes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> and runs in time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.' False because the algorithm computes the wrong result, not because of the time bound. For blocks the formula relies on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and both fail for matrices. For example, take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math> and the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> blocks <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x00028;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x00028;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Then the top-right block of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, but the formula gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Also, three of the five products (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>) are products of two different matrices, not squarings, so they are not subproblems of the same kind. The statement must be about this algorithm (DPV asks 'What is wrong with the following algorithm'), because 'squaring is possible in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>' on its own is an open problem: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x02248;</mo><mn>2.32</mn></mrow></math> is below the best known bound on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></math>, and by DPV 2.27(c) squaring is as hard as multiplication.</li></ul>", "sol": "<p><b>True.</b> As we have seen in class, if we can multiply <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> matrices with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> multiplications then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>, and the proof is exactly the recursive algorithm in the statement. Let us analyze it. We may assume <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>4</mn><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow></math> (otherwise pad the matrices with zeros up to the next power of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>, which multiplies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> by less than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></math> and does not change the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> bound). Split each matrix into <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math> blocks, each of size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>. The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math> algorithm works over any ring, in particular over the (non-commutative) ring of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> real matrices, so it correctly computes the product from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>48</mn></mrow></math> products of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> matrices, each computed recursively, plus a constant number (depending only on the fixed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math> algorithm) of block additions and subtractions, which cost <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Hence</p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>48</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mtext>so</mtext><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mn>48</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><p>since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mn>48</mn><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mn>16</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. Finally,</p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mn>48</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mn>49</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><msup><mn>7</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><p>so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mn>48</mn><mo>&#x02248;</mo><mn>2.79</mn></mrow></math> is below Strassen's exponent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x02248;</mo><mn>2.81</mn></mrow></math>. (Strassen's algorithm applied recursively once multiplies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math> matrices with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>7</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>49</mn></mrow></math> multiplications (2023A-1d), so any <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math> scheme with fewer than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>49</mn></mrow></math> products beats it.)</p><p><em>Remarks.</em> (1) The words 'over any ring' are needed, because the recursion applies the algorithm to blocks, and blocks do not commute. Over commutative rings, Winograd's 1968 inner-product trick already multiplies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math> matrices with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msup><mn>4</mn><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msup><mn>4</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>48</mn></mrow></math> multiplications, but it uses <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, so it cannot be applied to blocks and says nothing about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></math>. (2) The statement is about the recursive algorithm on purpose. The weaker conclusion 'there is an algorithm that multiplies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> matrices using <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003B2;</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> operations for some <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>7</mn></mrow></math>' holds regardless of the hypothesis, since we saw in class (slide 'Known bounds on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></math>') that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>2.38</mn></mrow></math>. The point here is that the hypothesis on its own already gives the exponent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mn>48</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>7</mn></mrow></math>.</p>", "srcline": "Algebraic deck, slide 9 'Bounding <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></math>' (Algebraic lecture deck): 'If we can multiply <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> matrices with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> multiplications, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>'; slide 10 'Known bounds on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></math>' (image only; the plot drops below 2.4 from Coppersmith-Winograd 1990 on); slide 11 (the Google <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>/<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></math> search). Volk's Hebrew notes, Thm 3.6 (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>7</mn></mrow></math>, from Strassen) and Thm 5.8. Chain: 2023A-1d (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>49</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>7</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>), then 2025C-1b (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>). Winograd's commutative trick: Bl&auml;ser, 'Fast Matrix Multiplication', ToC Graduate Surveys 5 (2013), Example 4.8, stated there only for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> times <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> matrices with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> products. The count 48 for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math> is derived here, not quoted: a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math> product is the sum of two <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> products, each costing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>24</mn></mrow></math> by Example 4.8, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>48</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mn>4</mn><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msup><mn>4</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> in total. Twin: Laderman's 23 for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, Bl&auml;ser. DPV variant: Dasgupta, Papadimitriou and Vazirani, <em>Algorithms</em>, Exercise 2.27(b)."}, {"part": "c", "difficulty": "medium", "summary": "True or false: in the lattice spanned by (2,0) and (1,2), the vector (2,0) is a shortest non-zero vector.", "q": "<p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><msup><mi>&#x0211D;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> be the lattice spanned by the vectors <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Then the vector <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is a shortest non-zero vector in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>.</p>", "basis": "<p><b>Lineage.</b> Lattices were a T/F topic in 2024A-1c (Gram-Schmidt), 2024B-1c (sorted orthogonal basis, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> is shortest, True) and 2026B-1d (lattice spanned by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> shortest, True). After two T/F appearances in 2024 the topic became the free-response 2024C-P5 (LLL bound). By the same-year reuse rule (6 of 6), the 2026B lattice item is one of four 2026B T/F topics that could get a 2026C sibling.</p><p><b>Source.</b> The basis <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is Micciancio's CSE206A Lecture 1, Example 9, the standard example that Gram-Schmidt vectors need not lie in the lattice. The Approximation deck makes the same point ('Note that this operation doesn't preserve the lattice spanned by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>!') right before the 'Lower bound on shortest vector' claim that decides the item.</p><p><b>The twist.</b> The answer stays True, but the argument of the official 2026B-1d solution no longer works. That solution used <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x02124;</mi></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> is an orthogonal basis. A student who copies that argument either gets stuck on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> or wrongly answers False. Only the general bound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msub><mo>min</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo></mrow></math> settles it.</p><p><b>Probability:</b> about 0.03.</p><p><b>Variants:</b></p><ul><li>'The Gram-Schmidt vectors of a lattice basis lie in the lattice.' False: here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>.</li><li>'If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a reduced basis (as defined in class), then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a shortest non-zero vector.' False: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>8</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is reduced (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>89</mn><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow></math>), but <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>89</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>. LLL only guarantees <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>.</li><li>The False sibling from the T/F analysis: the lattice spanned by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> claimed shortest (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is shorter).</li></ul>", "sol": "<p><b>True.</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The Gram-Schmidt process gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> and</p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>&#x027E9;</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>&#x027E9;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><p>As we have seen in class, every non-zero <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> satisfies</p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msub><mo>min</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><p>Hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is a shortest non-zero vector of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>.</p><p>Note that, unlike 2026B-1d, here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> is not an integer and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>: every vector of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x02124;</mi></mrow></math>, a second coordinate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> forces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, and then the first coordinate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> is odd, not <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> is not a basis of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>, and the orthogonal-basis argument of 2026B-1d does not apply. The lower bound from class does not need it. A direct check also works: for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, and if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</p>", "srcline": "D. Micciancio, UCSD CSE206A (Winter 2012), Lecture 1 'Introduction to Lattices', Example 9: 'The Gram-Schmidt orthogonalization of the basis <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>T</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>T</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>. However this is not a lattice basis for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> because the vector <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></math> does not belong to the lattice.' Approximation deck, 'Gram-Schmidt Orthogonalization' and 'Lower bound on shortest vector' slides (Approximation lecture deck). True twist of 2026B-1d."}, {"part": "d", "difficulty": "easy", "summary": "True or false: a symmetric matrix whose leading principal minors are all non-negative is PSD.", "q": "<p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>&#x0211D;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math> be a symmetric matrix all of whose leading principal minors are non-negative, that is, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the top-left <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> submatrix of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>. Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> is PSD.</p>", "basis": "<p><b>Source and lineage.</b> The Approximation deck's 'PSD Matrices' slide lists four equivalent definitions, the fourth being 'All principal minors of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> are non-negative'. Volk already made a T/F item from that line: 2024A-1b ('Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> be a symmetric matrix. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> is PSD', False, counterexample <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></math>). Replacing 'all principal minors' by 'leading principal minors' is the same move, and this is a well-known trap, because Sylvester's criterion only works with strict inequalities. Dropping a hypothesis is one of his most common T/F moves (2024A-1b, 2024C-1c, 2026A-1b).</p><p><b>Why 2026C.</b> PSD matrices got more weight in 2026: hw2026 sheet 2 Q6 (the PSD equivalences: eigenvalues, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>), hw2026 sheet 4 Q4 (Goemans-Williamson rounding for Max-2-LIN) and 2026B-P5 (Max-Cut SDP dual, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> PSD). Slot (d) holds a linear-algebra fact from the Approximation or spectral material in about 65% of papers.</p><p><b>Probability:</b> about 0.03.</p><p><b>Variant:</b> 'The value of the Max-Cut SDP relaxation of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> equals the size of its maximum cut.' False: three unit vectors at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>120</mn><mo>&#x02218;</mo></msup></mrow></math> give <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>9</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, while the maximum cut of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</p>", "sol": "<p><b>False.</b> Take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> and</p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x00028;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><p>Its leading principal minors are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, both non-negative. But for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></math> we get <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>A</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> is not PSD (equivalently, it has the eigenvalue <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>). The criterion we saw in class requires <em>all</em> principal minors to be non-negative, and the non-leading <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> principal minor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mn>22</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> is negative.</p><p><em>Remark.</em> The strict version is true (Sylvester's criterion): if all leading principal minors are strictly positive, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> is positive definite. The example shows that the criterion fails once zero minors are allowed.</p>", "srcline": "Approximation deck, 'PSD Matrices' slide (Approximation lecture deck): 'The following are equivalent definitions for PSD matrices:... 4. All principal minors of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> are non-negative'. Re-skin of 2024A-1b ('If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> is PSD', False; 2024A official solution P1(b))."}]}]}], "overview": "<p><b>Where this comes from.</b> Rebuilt on 25 September 2026, after Moed A and Moed B. Every 2026A and 2026B question was traced to its origin, and each claimed source was opened and checked by two independent reviewers. The course's own lecture decks, recitations and all past papers were analysed alongside. The prediction then came from four independent predictors, each with a different lens (Moed C history, source documents, coverage gaps, true/false). Their predictions were merged, critiqued and revised. Every practice problem below was written with a model solution and checked twice for mathematical errors. The full report, including the source map and all probabilities, is <a href=\"EXAM-INTEL-2026C.md\">EXAM-INTEL-2026C.md</a> in this folder.</p>\n\n<h3>How the July prediction did</h3>\n<div class=\"tscroll\"><table>\n<tr><th>Part of the July prediction</th><th>Result against the real 2026A and 2026B</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Format: \"nearly certain\" 32 / 20 / 16 / 16 / 16</td><td><b>Wrong.</b> The real papers were 28/16/16/20/20 (A) and 28/18/16/18/20 (B). The format is fixed within a year but changes every year, and the \"byte-for-byte 2025\" evidence came from a stale header in the 2025C solution PDF.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>The mock exam (the part you practised)</td><td><b>0 of the 9 real written problems.</b> The only overlap was at the level of problem type: a random construction appeared in both papers, as the mock's P5 predicted.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Slot-by-slot blueprint</td><td>Hits: a Chernoff/union-bound construction (both papers), the FFT \"wildcard\" (A-P2), and fast matrix multiplication in Moed B only (B-P2). High-confidence misses: a Schwartz–Zippel algorithm \"in every Moed A\" and an ILP-modelling problem.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>\"Source mine\": unused exercises next to his verified sources</td><td><b>Two exact hits, worth 36 of 2026A's 100 points.</b> Erickson FFT Ex. 2(c) became A-P2, and Williamson–Shmoys Ex. 5.3 + 5.6 became A-P5 almost word for word. Nothing in 2026B was an exact hit.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>\"Homework is the single best predictor\"</td><td><b>Wrong.</b> None of the 16 items in 2026 was a homework problem. The flow runs the other way: old exam problems become homework.</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Lesson applied here:</b> the format comes from this year's papers, not last year's. The practice set is built around the families and documents he actually draws from, rather than one guessed exam. The mock is only the single most likely pick per slot.</p>\n\n<h3>Where the 2026 questions actually came from</h3>\n<div class=\"tscroll\"><table>\n<tr><th>Item</th><th>Pts</th><th>Verified origin</th></tr>\n<tr><td>A-P2 · 3-term arithmetic progressions via FFT</td><td>16</td><td><b>Erickson, FFT notes, Exercise 2(c)</b> (count \"well-spaced triples\"); the same problem is in CMU 15-451's FFT notes. Note that the official solution double-counts: the correct answer is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>b</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>A-P3 · random vectors shatter every set of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> coordinates</td><td>16</td><td><b>Jukna, <em>Extremal Combinatorics</em> §3.3 \"Universal sets\"</b> (the Kleitman–Spencer theorem)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>A-P4 · min-cost unit-flow LP; its dual is the shortest path</td><td>20</td><td><b>Dasgupta–Papadimitriou–Vazirani, Exercise 7.28</b>; also the LP-duality notes of CMU 15-451</td></tr>\n<tr><td>A-P5 · Max DiCut: random <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mrow></math>, the IP, rounding with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></td><td>20</td><td><b>Williamson–Shmoys Ex. 5.3 + 5.6, verbatim</b> (also Feige's Weizmann handout 5)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>B-P5 · SDP dual <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>&#x02AB0;</mi><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and weak duality</td><td>20</td><td><b>Williamson–Shmoys Ex. 6.1</b> (the dual of the Max-Cut SDP; also Feige's handout 6)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>B-P2 · common neighbours via <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></math></td><td>18</td><td>His own re-skin of 2024B-P4 and the Seidel \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>\" slide</td></tr>\n<tr><td>B-P3 · colouring with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0.4</mn><mi>deg</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> neighbours of each colour</td><td>16</td><td>His own re-skin of 2023B-P6 / 2024B-P6 (set balancing)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>B-P4 · prize-collecting vertex cover, threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mrow></math></td><td>18</td><td>His own re-skin of 2024B-P5 (recitation 10) and 2023B-P3</td></tr>\n<tr><td>All 8 T/F items</td><td>56</td><td>One-line traps on specific slides: LP \"Bases\" and ellipsoid slides, the Schwartz–Zippel slide, the Csanky power-sum and Newton-identity slides, the isolation-lemma \"fantasy soccer\" slide, and Gram–Schmidt</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n\n<h3>How he builds a paper</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>True/false is a lecture quiz.</b> 39 of 40 past T/F items restate a slide claim with a twist: negate it, swap a constant, drop a hypothesis, or overgeneralise. A few are textbook exercises (Motwani–Raghavan Problem 12.22, Bertsimas–Tsitsiklis 3.6(a)). 29 of the 40 answers were True, and slot (a) was an LP fact in 7 of 10 papers.</li>\n<li><b>Written problems</b> in 2026 were fresh textbook exercises lifted nearly verbatim (Williamson–Shmoys Ch. 5–6, Erickson FFT, Jukna, DPV) plus re-skins of his own recent problems.</li>\n<li><b>Moed C</b> (2024C, 2025C): about two-thirds of the points re-skinned same-year A/B problems or older templates. Every Moed C had one \"re-prove a class theorem with a changed parameter\" problem. Each added one or two topics that its year's A/B skipped, and its T/F items reused sub-topics from the previous sitting.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Predicted 2026C blueprint</h3>\n<div class=\"gtool\"><div class=\"gtool-h\">FRAME</div><div class=\"gtool-body\">\n<p>3 hours, 100 points, all compulsory, two double-sided A4 sheets. <b>P1 = 4 T/F × 7 = 28</b>, then <b>four written problems of 14–22 points</b> (0.85). Moed C usually shifts one to three weights by about 2 points, so the exact 2026A split is only 0.25 and the 2026B split 0.20. Time budget: T/F about 50 min, a 16-point problem about 29 min, a 20-point problem about 36 min.</p>\n</div></div>\n<div class=\"tscroll\"><table>\n<tr><th>Slot</th><th>Most likely content</th><th>Probability</th></tr>\n<tr><td>P1 (a)</td><td>LP fact: an ellipsoid sibling of 2026B-(a), duality edge cases, vertices/BFS, simplex degeneracy</td><td>0.75</td></tr>\n<tr><td>P1 (b)</td><td>A randomness-lemma trap: Schwartz–Zippel, isolation, BPP, Markov/Chebyshev</td><td>0.75</td></tr>\n<tr><td>P1 (c)</td><td>Algebraic: a third trace / Newton / Cayley–Hamilton item (0.30), or matrix-multiplication counts</td><td>0.75</td></tr>\n<tr><td>P1 (d)</td><td>A matrix/eigenvalue/lattice fact: lattice/PSD/SDP 0.22, spectral 0.20</td><td>0.55</td></tr>\n<tr><td>P2 (16–18)</td><td>Single-part algebraic algorithm: counting via <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></math> / fast matrix multiplication (0.25), FFT (0.13), a Schwartz–Zippel algorithm (0.10), traces (0.08)</td><td>0.62</td></tr>\n<tr><td>P3 (16)</td><td>A Chernoff + union-bound construction (0.48) or a sampling estimator (0.13)</td><td>0.72</td></tr>\n<tr><td>P4 (18–20)</td><td>LP: derive and interpret a dual (0.15), write an ILP (0.12), an LP-theory re-proof (0.08); or a Schwartz–Zippel algorithm (0.13)</td><td>mixed</td></tr>\n<tr><td>P5 (6+6+8)</td><td>Approximation deck: LP-rounding approximation (0.28), SDP/Goemans–Williamson (0.15), LLL (0.07)</td><td>0.55</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Near-certain:</b> a Chernoff/union-bound written problem (0.85); at least one written problem that continues or re-skins a 2026A/B problem (0.78); a homework or class proof re-used with a changed parameter (0.80).</li>\n<li><b>Likely:</b> an LP-duality part (0.60) brings LP back after 2026B's 7 points, just as 2024C did after the LP-light 2024B. An LP-based approximation part: 0.55.</li>\n<li><b>Families 2026A/B skipped that could return:</b> write-an-ILP (0.35), a Schwartz–Zippel/PIT algorithm (0.33), SDP/Goemans–Williamson (0.22), random walks/spectral (0.13), LLL (0.10).</li>\n<li><b>No single written problem is above about 6%.</b> Drill families rather than instances: counting via <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></math>/traces, Chernoff balancing, symbolic-determinant PIT, derive-and-interpret a dual, threshold or randomized rounding with an ILP, and SDP weak duality.</li>\n<li>Difficulty should be no harder than 2026A/B (0.75).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>How to use this tab</h3>\n<p>Do the <b>mock</b> as a timed paper (it is the single most likely problem per slot). Then do the <b>alternates</b>: together with the mock they cover the families that carry most of the probability. Use the <b>T/F pool</b> for fast drills; each item cites the slide or exercise it is built from. The <em>Why predicted</em> box under each problem gives the evidence and a calibrated probability. Treat those numbers as honest odds, not guarantees.</p>\n"};
